{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012oGzFL9hVqQCW5adppUjGW","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t                                     The pathwidth complex\n2\t                          Alexandre Linhares                 Horacio Hideki Yanasse\n3\t                           affiliation to be added              affiliation to be added\n4\t\n5\t                                             Draft, 6 October 2026\n6\t\n7\t\n8\t                                                     Abstract\n9\t      The minimization of open stacks problem (MOSP) asks for the order in which to make a set of patterns\n10\t      so that the fewest customer orders are open at once. Linhares and Yanasse (2002) listed twelve problems\n11\t      from operations research, VLSI design and graph theory, MOSP among them, as equal to it “plus or\n12\t      minus one”, and gave no proofs. We ask which of these equalities are true, and what follows when results,\n13\t      algorithms and benchmarks are moved between the twelve problems through pathwidth, the quantity that\n14\t      connects them. We find four things. First, eight of the twelve rows are exact, counting pathwidth itself:\n15\t      each equals pathwidth or pathwidth plus one. Two lie in bands one and two wide, and two are false as\n16\t      stated; every relation is proved, and machine-checked in Lean. Second, the literature has settled on the\n17\t      name pathwidth, used more than three times as often as the other eleven names together, and the three\n18\t      communities rarely cite one another. Third, the best exact algorithm for MOSP, the search of Chu and\n19\t      Stuckey (2009), is a pathwidth algorithm, and two of its published dominance theorems are false. The\n20\t      correct rule was already known for pathwidth; we show that it is tested by one bipartite matching per\n21\t      candidate, and prove the repaired search sound. In our implementation it costs at most 2% more search\n22\t      nodes on each benchmark set, and it re-proved all 115 benchmark optima that rested on the published\n23\t      rules, changing none. Fourth, we release 17,714 benchmark graphs, distinct up to isomorphism, from\n24\t      twenty collections, 16,087 of them with a certified pathwidth and a layout that attains it.\n25\t\n26\t\n27\t1    Introduction\n28\tA factory makes a set of patterns, one at a time, and each customer’s order needs some of them. A customer’s\n29\tstack opens when the first pattern the customer needs is made, and it closes when the last one is made. The\n30\tminimization of open stacks problem (MOSP) asks for the order of the patterns that keeps the fewest stacks\n31\topen at any one time. In the cutting industry, where the problem arose, the patterns are cutting patterns and\n32\tthe customers are piece types (Yanasse, 1997b). Figure 1.1(a) gives a small instance. Six customers, a to f ,\n33\tneed four patterns: P1 = {a, b}, P2 = {b, c, d}, P3 = {c, d, e} and P4 = {e, f }. Made in order A, P1 P2 P3 P4 ,\n34\tthe patterns keep at most 3 stacks open. Made in order B, P1 P3 P4 P2 , they keep 5 open, because customer b\n35\tis served first but waits for P2 , which comes last. Of the 24 orders, 2 reach 3 (order A and its reverse), 16\n36\tgive 4 and 6 give 5. MOSP is NP-hard (Linhares, 2001; Linhares and Yanasse, 2002).\n37\t    The same matrix is a problem in VLSI design. In a gate matrix layout the patterns are gates, drawn as\n38\tvertical lines in some order, and the customers are nets. Each net is a horizontal wire from the first gate it\n39\tconnects to the last, and wires that overlap must lie on different horizontal tracks. The task is to order the\n40\tgates so that the fewest tracks suffice. Figure 1.1(b) and (c) draw the two orders. The wires are the same;\n41\tonly the gate order changes which wires overlap. Order A packs into 3 tracks and order B needs 5. The\n42\tnumber of tracks a gate order needs is its density, the most wires crossing any one gate, and that is exactly\n43\tthe number of open stacks there. So the two problems compute the same number from the same matrix\n44\t(Linhares and Yanasse, 2002, Proposition 2).\n45\t\n46\t\n47\t                                                         1\n48\t\f(a)                              (b)                                   (c)                                 (d)\n49\t      P1     P2    P3     P4           P1       P2       P3       P4           P1       P3       P4   P2\n50\t                                   a        c                 f            a        c\n51\t a     1      0     0      0                                                                                             c\n52\t                                  b                  e                     b\n53\t b     1      1     0      0                                                                                a     b               e   f\n54\t                                            d                                       d                                    d\n55\t  c    0      1     1      0\n56\t                                                                                    e\n57\t d     0      1     1      0                3 tracks\n58\t                                                                                             f                         {a, b}\n59\t e     0      0     1      1\n60\t                                                                                                                      {b, c, d}\n61\t  f    0      0     0      1                                                        5 tracks\n62\t                                                                                                                      {c, d, e}\n63\t A     2      3     3      2\n64\t                                                                                                                       {e, f}\n65\t B     2      4     5      3\n66\t      open stacks per position                                                                                   bags of A: width 2\n67\t\n68\t\n69\tFigure 1.1: One instance, three readings, one number. (a) Six customers and four patterns, with each customer’s open\n70\tstack shaded in order A; order A keeps at most 3 stacks open and order B at most 5, and only 2 of the 24 orders reach\n71\t3. (b, c) The same matrix as a gate matrix layout: the same two orders need 3 and 5 tracks (the shaded gate is where\n72\tthe density peaks). (d) The same matrix as a graph, one clique per pattern: the open sets of order A are the bags of a\n73\tpath decomposition of width 2, so the optimum is the pathwidth plus one (Theorem 2.3).\n74\t\n75\t\n76\t     The same matrix is also a graph. Make each customer a vertex, and join two customers when some\n77\tpattern needs both, so that each pattern becomes a clique (Figure 1.1(d)). Write down, for order A, the set\n78\tof open stacks at each position: {a, b}, {b, c, d}, {c, d, e}, {e, f }. These sets cover every vertex and every\n79\tedge, and each customer appears in a run of consecutive sets. In graph theory such a sequence is a path\n80\tdecomposition, its sets are bags, and its width is the size of its largest bag minus one. The least width over\n81\tall path decompositions is the pathwidth of the graph, here 2. The construction works in both directions, so\n82\tthe fewest open stacks is always the pathwidth plus one (Theorem 2.3).\n83\t     Linhares and Yanasse (2002) collected twelve such problems, from operations research, VLSI design\n84\tand graph theory, in the table that Table 1.1 reproduces. Each computes, on corresponding inputs, “a function\n85\t f (Π) that is either equal to the number of open stacks or closely related to it (plus or minus one)” (Linhares\n86\tand Yanasse, 2002, p. 1764). The table gives a reference for each problem, but no proofs and no graph of\n87\twhich problem reduces to which. “Plus or minus one” has two readings. Under the literal reading, f is\n88\twithin one of the number of open stacks on every input. Under the fixed-offset reading, f equals the number\n89\tof open stacks plus one constant, the same on every input. Only the second makes solving one problem the\n90\tsame as solving another. We call the twelve the pathwidth complex, because pathwidth is both the name\n91\tthe literature uses most (Section 3) and the quantity through which every true relation in the table is proved\n92\t(Section 2). This paper asks: which of the equalities in the Linhares–Yanasse table are true, and what follows\n93\twhen results, algorithms and benchmarks are moved between the twelve problems through pathwidth?\n94\t     The table has a history that explains why the question is still open. It was first printed in the first author’s\n95\tdoctoral thesis (Linhares, 2001, Table 2.1, p. 13), supervised by the second author, which proved MOSP NP-\n96\thard and equal to gate matrix layout. Its rows were collected over the years of the doctorate, while reading\n97\tthe literature of each field. Each came with a reference and nothing else. No row’s relation to open stacks\n98\twas checked, no published proof was restated, and no graph said which problem reduces to which. The\n99\ttable appeared again, row for row, as Table 1 of Linhares and Yanasse (2002), and it has been read since as\n100\ta statement of equivalence. Garcia de la Banda and Stuckey (2007), for instance, credit the speed of their\n101\tsearch in this journal to “the fact that the problem is fixed parameter tractable (Linhares and Yanasse 2002)”.\n102\tSo the table is already used to justify algorithms, and whether it is true matters. If it is true, every result\n103\tabout any one of the twelve problems transfers to all the others. Looking back, the table was the thesis’s\n104\tmost important finding, and this article does what the thesis did not: it states each problem precisely, proves\n105\t\n106\t                                                                       2\n107\t\fTable 1.1: Twelve problems from three fields, published as one family: eight rows are exact, counting the pathwidth\n108\trow itself; two are bands; two are false as the table states them. The Linhares–Yanasse table, reproduced from Linhares\n109\tand Yanasse (2002, Table 1, p. 1764) with its row order, wording and references as printed, and its caption: “Display\n110\tof a family of equivalent problems studied independently on the literature”. The shaded column is ours. It gives the\n111\trelation to pathwidth (pw) that is true, proved in Section 2 (Table 2.3).\n112\t\n113\tProblem                      Discipline             References                                   This paper\n114\tMOSP                         Operations research    Yanasse (1997b); Fink and Voß (1999)         exact: pw + 1\n115\tGate matrix layout           VLSI design            Möhring (1990); Wing et al. (1985)           exact: pw + 1\n116\tOne-dimensional logic        VLSI design            Ohtsuki et al. (1979)                        exact: pw + 1\n117\tPLA folding                  VLSI design            Möhring (1990)                               falsea\n118\tInterval thickness           Graph theory           Kashiwabara and Fujisawa (1979)              exact: pw + 1\n119\tNode search game             Graph theory           Kirousis and Papadimitriou (1985)            exact: pw + 1\n120\tEdge search game             Graph theory           Kirousis and Papadimitriou (1986)            band: pw to pw + 2\n121\tNarrowness                   Graph theory           Kornai and Tuza (1992)                       exact: pw + 1\n122\tSplit bandwidth              Graph theory           Fomin (1998)                                 band: pw to pw + 1\n123\tGraph path-width             Graph theory           Kinnersley (1992)                            exact: pw (reference)\n124\tEdge separation              Graph theory           Lengauer (1981)                              falseb\n125\tVertex separation            Graph theory           Kinnersley (1992)                            exact: pw\n126\ta Exact (pw + 1) for multiple folding, with any number of nets per track. b As cutwidth; the vertex separator game of the same\n127\t\n128\treference is exact (pw, for graphs with an edge).\n129\t\n130\t\n131\twhat is true and refutes what is not. The communities that could have checked the table rarely read each\n132\tother: of 844 works that cite the table’s papers, only six cite both an open-stacks paper and a graph-theory\n133\tpaper (Section 3).\n134\t\n135\tWhat we find.\n136\t\n137\t     • The table, settled. Eight of the twelve rows are exact, the pathwidth row itself counted as one of them:\n138\t       the other seven equal pathwidth or pathwidth plus one on every input, outside degenerate cases such\n139\t       as an empty graph. Two rows, split bandwidth and edge search, lie in bands one and two wide. Two\n140\t       rows, PLA folding and edge separation, are false as stated, with counterexamples. A variant of each\n141\t       false row is exact (Section 2).\n142\t\n143\t     • One name. The literature has settled on “pathwidth”, which has more than three times as many\n144\t       relevant works as the other eleven names together. Besides pathwidth, the most active community\n145\t       over 2005–2024 is the open-stacks one, and the exact search of Section 4 came from it (Section 3).\n146\t\n147\t     • A repaired search. The best exact algorithm for MOSP, the search of Chu and Stuckey (2009), is\n148\t       also a pathwidth algorithm. Two of its published dominance theorems are false, as are their later\n149\t       restatements (Chu, 2011; Fink, 2012). The correct rule was already known in the exact pathwidth\n150\t       literature, and we show that it can be tested by one bipartite matching per candidate. The repaired\n151\t       search is proved sound. Over each benchmark set it changes the total number of search nodes by at\n152\t       most 2%, and no wrong answer of the published rules on a whole instance is known (Section 4).\n153\t\n154\t     • Benchmarks re-certified, and a dataset. The repaired search re-proved all 115 benchmark optima that\n155\t       rested on the published rules, and none changed (as of 6 October 2026). We release 17,714 graphs,\n156\t       distinct up to isomorphism, from twenty benchmark collections of the twelve problems; 16,087 have\n157\t       a certified pathwidth with a layout that attains it, and so a certified value for every exact problem in\n158\t       the table (Section 5).\n159\t\n160\t\n161\t                                                              3\n162\t\fHow to read the paper. Section 2 states the twelve problems and proves what is true. Section 3 measures\n163\twhich names and communities the literature uses. Section 4 reads the exact open-stacks search as a path-\n164\twidth algorithm, finds the two false rules and proves the repaired search sound. Section 5 reports its cost and\n165\tthe dataset, and Section 6 answers the question above. Every theorem we state is machine-checked in the\n166\tLean 4 proof assistant. The reader need not read Lean: the proofs are given in the text, and Appendix A maps\n167\teach result to its formal statement. Appendix B holds the supporting material, Appendix C the bibliometric\n168\tmethod, and the data, code and proofs are described at the end of the paper (page 26).\n169\t\n170\t\n171\t2     The equivalences, proved\n172\tThis section answers the first half of our question: which rows of the Linhares–Yanasse table are true, and\n173\tin what sense. Section 1 separated two readings of “plus or minus one”. We call a row exact when it meets\n174\tthe fixed-offset reading: its value is pathwidth, or pathwidth plus one, on every input. We call it a band\n175\twhen it meets only the literal reading: its value stays within a fixed distance of pathwidth, and both ends\n176\tof that range occur. We call it false when it meets neither. The answer is that eight rows are exact, two are\n177\tbands and two are false. Section 2.1 states the problems. Section 2.2 proves the answer in four steps: the\n178\thub theorem, which ties MOSP to pathwidth; the other exact rows; the bands; and the false rows. Table 2.3\n179\tand Figure 2.1 summarise it.\n180\t     All theorems below are verified in the Lean proof assistant, unless the text says otherwise; Appendix A\n181\tlists the formal statements. Where a result is published, we cite the published proof and do not repeat it.\n182\t\n183\tNotation. Table 2.1 lists the symbols used in the rest of the paper. Graphs are finite and simple. A 0/1\n184\tmatrix M has the customers as rows and the patterns as columns; in the VLSI problems the rows are nets\n185\tand the columns gates. Row r is active at position j of a column order π if it has 1s in two columns x, y with\n186\tπ(x) ≤ j ≤ π(y), possibly x = y: its stack is open there. “M has a 1” excludes only the all-zero matrix. One\n187\tletter needs care. In this section k is the bound in each decision problem, so for pathwidth it bounds a width.\n188\tIn Section 4 k bounds the number of open stacks, which is one more than a width; we say so again there.\n189\t\n190\t2.1   The problems\n191\tEach problem is a decision problem with an instance and an integer k; its value is the least k for which the\n192\tanswer is yes. Four of the twelve carry the rest of the paper, and we define them in full. Figure 1.1 shows\n193\teach on one instance.\n194\tPathwidth pw(G). Is there a sequence of bags X1 , . . . , Xr ⊆ V covering V , with every edge inside some bag,\n195\t     Xi ∩ Xℓ ⊆ X j whenever i ≤ j ≤ ℓ, and |Xi | ≤ k + 1? (Kinnersley, 1992, p. 346). The condition on\n196\t     Xi ∩ Xℓ is the interval property: the bags that contain a vertex are consecutive.\n197\tVertex separation vs(G). Is there a layout L such that for every i < n at most k vertices u with L(u) ≤ i\n198\t     have a neighbour v with L(v) > i? (Kinnersley, 1992, p. 346).\n199\tMOSP Z(M). Given a 0/1 matrix M of customers by patterns, is there an order of the patterns with at most\n200\t   k customers active at every position? (Linhares and Yanasse, 2002, eqs. (1)–(2)).\n201\tGate matrix layout t(M). Given a net–gate matrix, is there an order of the gates and an assignment of nets\n202\t     to k tracks such that nets active at a common position get different tracks? (Möhring, 1990, p. 18);\n203\t     (Wing et al., 1985, Problem 1).\n204\t   The other eight problems are needed only to read the table. Table 2.2 gives each in one line; Ap-\n205\tpendix B.1 gives the formal definitions, with the sources.\n206\t\n207\t                                                      4\n208\t\fTable 2.1: Notation. Each symbol has one meaning throughout the paper; the symbols of the search are defined in\n209\tSection 4.1.\n210\t\n211\tSymbol                     Meaning\n212\tG = (V, E), n = |V |       a graph, its vertices and edges; in Section 4 the vertices are the customers, V = C\n213\tL                          a layout, a bijection V → {1, . . . , n}\n214\tX1 , . . . , Xr            the bags of a path decomposition\n215\tM; C; P                    a 0/1 matrix; its rows (customers, nets); its columns (patterns, gates)\n216\tGM                         the MOSP graph: vertex set C, two customers adjacent when some pattern has a 1 in both\n217\t                           rows; each pattern becomes a clique\n218\tH                          the net graph of a circuit: two nets adjacent when they share a gate\n219\tπ                          an order of the columns\n220\tZ(M), t(M)                 the MOSP value (fewest open stacks) and the gate matrix layout value (fewest tracks)\n221\tpw, vs                     pathwidth and vertex separation\n222\tθ , ns, mns, es, pes, ν,   the values of the other problems (Table 2.2)\n223\tsb, cw, mcw, VSG,\n224\tpla\n225\tk                          the bound of a decision problem; in Section 4, a bound on open stacks, so width k − 1\n226\tw                          a width, as in “pathwidth w”\n227\tN[c]                       the closed neighbourhood of c: c and its neighbours\n228\t∂ T , b(T )                the border of a vertex set T , the vertices outside T with a neighbour in T ; b(T ) = |∂ T |\n229\t\n230\t\n231\t\n232\t\n233\tTable 2.2: The other eight problems of the Linhares–Yanasse table look unrelated (two circuit layouts, two search\n234\tgames and four graph problems), yet each asks for the least k that is possible. One line each. Formal definitions are in\n235\tAppendix B.1.\n236\t\n237\tProblem                       In one line\n238\tOne-dimensional logic         Order the gates of a circuit so that the intervals spanned by the nets overlap at most k at\n239\t                              any point. Its graph is the net graph H, which joins two nets that share a gate. A\n240\t                              boundary variant pins two gates to the ends.\n241\tPLA folding pla(M)            Gate matrix layout in at most k tracks, with at most two nets on a track. With no limit per\n242\t                              track (multiple folding) it is gate matrix layout itself.\n243\tInterval thickness θ (G)      Add edges to G to make an interval graph whose largest clique has at most k vertices.\n244\tNode search ns(G)             Searchers are placed on and removed from vertices. An edge is cleared when both its\n245\t                              ends hold a searcher, and becomes contaminated again if a searcher-free path joins it to a\n246\t                              contaminated edge. Clear every edge with at most k searchers at once. mns(G) is the\n247\t                              same with no recontamination allowed.\n248\tEdge search es(G)             Node search with a third move: sliding a searcher along an edge clears it. pes(G) is the\n249\t                              same over progressive strategies, which never recontaminate an edge.\n250\tNarrowness ν(G)               Add the vertices one at a time in some order, and drop each as soon as all its neighbours\n251\t                              have been added. Never hold more than k.\n252\tSplit bandwidth sb(G)         Repeatedly split a vertex into an edge, sharing its neighbours between the two ends, so\n253\t                              that the result has a layout in which adjacent vertices are at most k apart (its bandwidth).\n254\tEdge separation               The table’s reference (Lengauer, 1981) supports three readings: cutwidth cw(G), the\n255\t                              most edges crossing a gap of a layout; modified cutwidth mcw(G), the most edges\n256\t                              passing strictly over a vertex; and Lengauer’s vertex separator game VSG(G), a pebbling\n257\t                              game that counts the unpebbled vertices adjacent to pebbled ones.\n258\t\n259\t\n260\t\n261\t\n262\t                                                             5\n263\t\fTable 2.3: The Linhares–Yanasse table, settled. Eight rows are exact, two are bands and two are false, and every true\n264\trelation is a relation to pathwidth. Relation: what is true and proved in Lean, on the graph the problem lives on (the\n265\tMOSP graph or the net graph H for the matrix problems, the input graph otherwise). Published source: the published\n266\twork the row rests on; the Lean proofs are listed in Appendix A. Status: exact, a fixed offset; band, an inequality\n267\twhose ends both occur; false, the table’s claim fails, with a counterexample family proved in Lean.\n268\t\n269\tProblem                 Published source               Relation                                               Status\n270\tMOSP                    Yanasse (1997a);               Z(M) = pw(GM ) + 1 (M has a 1)                         exact\n271\t                        Fellows and Langston\n272\t                        (1989); Linhares and\n273\t                        Yanasse (2002)\n274\tGate matrix layout      Möhring (1990)                 t(M) = Z(M) = pw(GM ) + 1                              exact\n275\tOne-dim. logic          Ohtsuki et al. (1979)          tracks = θ (H) = pw(H) + 1; boundary                   exact\n276\t                                                       variant ≥ pw(H) + 1\n277\tPLA folding             Möhring (1990)                 max(pw + 1, ⌈|N|/2⌉) ≤ pla, gap unbounded;             false; variant exact\n278\t                                                       multiple folding = pw + 1\n279\tInterval thickness      Möhring (1990);                θ = pw + 1 (V ̸= /0)                                   exact\n280\t                        Kirousis and\n281\t                        Papadimitriou (1985)\n282\tNode search             Kirousis and                   ns = mns = vs + 1 = pw + 1 = θ (E ̸= /0)               exact\n283\t                        Papadimitriou (1985,\n284\t                        1986); Bienstock and\n285\t                        Seymour (1991)\n286\tEdge search             Ellis et al. (1994);           vs ≤ es ≤ vs + 2, also for pes                         band\n287\t                        Kirousis and\n288\t                        Papadimitriou (1986)\n289\tNarrowness              Kornai and Tuza (1992)         ν = pw + 1 (V ̸= /0)                                   exact\n290\tSplit bandwidth         Fomin (1998)                   pw ≤ sb ≤ pw + 1                                       band\n291\tPathwidth               Kinnersley (1992)              the reference                                          exact\n292\tEdge separationa        Lengauer (1981)                cw, mcw unbounded against pw (stars);                  false; VSG exact\n293\t                                                       VSG = max(1, vs)\n294\tVertex separation       Kinnersley (1992)              vs = pw                                                exact\n295\ta Also misattributed: what Lengauer (1981) proves exactly is the vertex separator game, which is the table’s vertex separation row.\n296\t\n297\t\n298\t\n299\t\n300\t2.2    What is true\n301\tTable 2.3 gives the answer row by row, and Figure 2.1 draws it as a graph around pathwidth. The rest of this\n302\tsubsection proves it.\n303\t\n304\tThe hub. Two theorems tie the table to pathwidth. The first joins the two graph rows, vertex separation\n305\tand pathwidth. The second joins MOSP to pathwidth, and through it all four matrix problems. The second\n306\trests on one lemma about path decompositions. For a vertex v, let first(v) and last(v) be the least and greatest\n307\tindices of bags containing v; by the interval property, v ∈ Xi exactly when first(v) ≤ i ≤ last(v).\n308\tLemma 2.1 (cliques sit in a bag). In a path decomposition of G, every clique of G lies inside one bag (Ap-\n309\tpendix A, A.1).\n310\tProof. The bags containing a vertex u of the clique form the interval [first(u), last(u)]. Two vertices of the\n311\tclique are adjacent, so their edge lies in a bag and their intervals meet. Pairwise intersecting intervals of a\n312\tline have a common point (the Helly property): let a be the largest left end; every right end is at least every\n313\tleft end, so at least a, and a lies in all of them. So all the intervals share an index i, and the clique lies in\n314\tXi .\n315\t\n316\t                                                                  6\n317\t\f                  ✗ gap unbounded\n318\t\n319\t\n320\t                                    =t\n321\t                                             t(M)               =Z\n322\t  simple PLA             multiple                                      Z(M)                 VSG\n323\t    folding              folding          gate matrix                                   vertex game\n324\t                                                                       MOSP\n325\t                                            layout\n326\t\n327\t\n328\t                                                                 Z = pw + 1             = max(1, vs)\n329\t\n330\t\n331\t\n332\t    tracks      = θ(H)         θ(G)      θ = pw + 1                           vs = pw     vs(G)        ≤ vs + 2      pes(G)\n333\t                                                           pw(G)\n334\t   one-dim.                  interval                                                     vertex                   progressive\n335\t     logic                                               pathwidth\n336\t                            thickness                                                   separation                 edge search\n337\t\n338\t\n339\t                                                                ✗ unbounded               = vs + 1\n340\t                                                   ν = pw + 1     on stars                              ≤ vs + 2         es = pes?\n341\t                                pw ≤ sb ≤ pw + 1\n342\t\n343\t                      sb(G)                                                               mns(G)\n344\t                                             ν(G)                     cw, mcw                                         es(G)\n345\t                       split                                          cutwidth           monotone\n346\t                                          narrowness                                                               edge search\n347\t                    bandwidth                                                           node search\n348\t\n349\t\n350\t                                                                                               ns = mns\n351\t\n352\t\n353\t                                                                                           ns(G)\n354\t                                                                                        node search\n355\t\n356\t\n357\t\n358\tFigure 2.1: Pathwidth is the hub of the table: every exact relation is one or two steps from it, and the only failures\n359\tare the two false claims, in dark red. Solid edges are exact relations and dashed edges bands, both proved in Lean; the\n360\tdotted edge is LaPaugh’s es = pes, the one relation not proved, which no row needs. Each false claim is refuted by\n361\ta counterexample family proved in Lean. The four matrix problems (top left) live on the MOSP graph GM or the net\n362\tgraph H. Hypotheses are omitted: M has a 1, V ̸= /0, or G has an edge, as in Table 2.3. Two edges that follow from the\n363\tothers, mns = θ and ns = vs + 1, are not drawn.\n364\t\n365\t\n366\t\n367\t\n368\t                                                                  7\n369\t\f    The first theorem is known. It says the two graph rows of the table are the same quantity.\n370\t\n371\tTheorem 2.2 (vertex separation is pathwidth; known). For every graph, vs(G) = pw(G) (Appendix A, A.2).\n372\t\n373\t    This is Theorem 3.1 of Kinnersley (1992). Both directions are constructions. From a layout of separation\n374\tk, each bag is the set counted at a cut together with the next vertex. From a decomposition, ordering the\n375\tvertices by first gives a layout of the same width.\n376\t    The second theorem is the one the rest of the paper uses. In Figure 1.1(d), the sets of customers active\n377\tat each step of the good order are the bags of a path decomposition of the MOSP graph. The theorem says\n378\tthat this always works, in both directions.\n379\t\n380\tTheorem 2.3 (MOSP is pathwidth plus one). If M has a 1, then Z(M) = pw(GM ) + 1 (Appendix A, A.3).\n381\t\n382\tProof. (≤) Take a decomposition of GM of width k. The rows of each column p form a clique, so by\n383\tLemma 2.1 they lie in a bag Xβ (p) . Order the columns by β (p). If row r is active at the position of column p,\n384\tit has columns x, y with β (x) ≤ β (p) ≤ β (y), and r ∈ Xβ (x) ∩ Xβ (y) , so the interval property gives r ∈ Xβ (p) .\n385\tAt most k + 1 rows are active there.\n386\t    (≥) Take an order with at most Z = Z(M) active rows at every position. The active sets, with a singleton\n387\tbag for each row with no 1, form a path decomposition: rows sharing a column are both active at its position,\n388\tand a row is active on an interval of positions. Its width is Z − 1, using Z ≥ 1.\n389\t\n390\t    For the table, this settles the MOSP row as exact, with offset one, on the MOSP graph. Read as a cutting\n391\tproblem, Theorem 2.3 is Proposition 5 of Yanasse (1997a), which turns each pattern into a clique on its\n392\tpiece types. Read as a layout problem, it is Theorem 7 of Fellows and Langston (1989), with the column-\n393\texpansion Lemma 4.1 of Fellows and Langston (1987). Linhares and Yanasse (2002, Prop. 2) identify\n394\tMOSP with gate matrix layout. The proof above needs no assumption that the instance is reduced.\n395\t    The graph matters. The MOSP literature also uses a second graph, with the patterns as vertices, joined\n396\twhen they share a customer, to split an instance into independent parts (Yanasse and Senne, 2010). It is\n397\tnatural to expect its pathwidth to measure the open stacks as well; it does not, in either direction. One\n398\tpattern shared by four customers who each have a private pattern has Z = 4, while its pattern graph is the\n399\tstar K1,4 , of pathwidth 1 (Appendix A, A.4). A single customer with many patterns errs the other way.\n400\t\n401\tThe exact core. The other exact rows attach to Theorems 2.2 and 2.3 by published arguments. We do\n402\tnot reprove them on paper; each item below cites the published proof, and the Lean proofs are listed in\n403\tAppendix A. We call the problems that equal pathwidth or pathwidth plus one on every input the exact core:\n404\tthe eight exact rows, with multiple folding and the vertex separator game.\n405\t\n406\tTheorem 2.4 (the exact core).\n407\t\n408\t   1. Gate matrix layout is MOSP on the same matrix: if M has a 1, t(M) = Z(M) = pw(GM ) + 1. For a\n409\t      fixed gate order the fewest tracks equals the most nets active at one position, by the left-edge algorithm\n410\t      (Möhring, 1990, p. 31) (Appendix A, A.5).\n411\t\n412\t   2. Interval thickness: if V ̸= /0, θ (G) = pw(G) + 1 (Möhring, 1990, Prop. 3.5) (Appendix A, A.6).\n413\t\n414\t   3. One-dimensional logic: if every net lies on a gate, the fewest tracks is θ (H), and if some gate connects\n415\t      a net it is pw(H) + 1 (Ohtsuki et al., 1979, Thm 3) (Appendix A, A.7).\n416\t\n417\t   4. Narrowness: if V ̸= /0, ν(G) = pw(G) + 1, and for each order σ , ν(σ ) = vs(σ rev ) + 1 (Kornai and\n418\t      Tuza, 1992, Prop. 3.1) (Appendix A, A.8).\n419\t\n420\t\n421\t\n422\t                                                         8\n423\t\f   5. Node search: for every graph ns(G) = mns(G), and if E ̸= /0, ns(G) = vs(G) + 1 = pw(G) + 1 = θ (G)\n424\t      (Kirousis and Papadimitriou, 1985, Thm); (Kirousis and Papadimitriou, 1986, Thms 2.3, 4.1) (Ap-\n425\t      pendix A, A.9).\n426\t\n427\t   6. Multiple folding: the fewest tracks is t(M) = pw(GM ) + 1 (Möhring, 1990, Thm 3.14) (Appendix A,\n428\t      A.10).\n429\t\n430\t   7. Lengauer’s vertex separator game: VSG(G) = max(1, vs(G)) (Lengauer, 1981, Thm 4) (Appendix A,\n431\t      A.11).\n432\t\n433\t    For the table, this makes the gate matrix layout, one-dimensional logic, interval thickness, node search\n434\tand narrowness rows exact, and gives an exact variant of each false row. A single certified pathwidth\n435\ttherefore answers every problem in the exact core; Section 5.4 uses this.\n436\t\n437\tBands.    Two rows are not fixed offsets of pathwidth, but stay within a fixed distance of it.\n438\t\n439\tTheorem 2.5 (bands). For every graph, pw(G) ≤ sb(G) ≤ pw(G) + 1 (Fomin, 1998, Thm 8), and vs(G) ≤\n440\tes(G) ≤ vs(G) + 2, and the same for pes (Ellis et al., 1994, Thm 2.1) (Appendix A, A.12).\n441\t\n442\t     Every end of both bands occurs. K2 has sb = pw = 1 and es = vs = 1; the star K1,3 has pw = 1 and\n443\tsb = 2, and vs = 1 with es = 2; and K3,3 has vs = 3 with es = 5. These values are computed by brute force\n444\tfrom each quantity’s definition, not proved in Lean.1 Fomin (1998) assumes a connected graph with at least\n445\ttwo vertices; the Lean proof needs neither. LaPaugh’s equality es = pes (LaPaugh, 1993) is the one relation\n446\twe state and do not prove, and no row needs it.\n447\t     For the table, this means the following. Relative to the number of open stacks Z = pw + 1, split band-\n448\twidth lies in [Z − 1, Z] and edge search in [Z − 1, Z + 1]. Both stay within one of Z, so they meet the\n449\tliteral reading of “plus or minus one”. Since both ends occur, neither is a fixed offset, so neither meets the\n450\tfixed-offset reading.\n451\t\n452\tFalse rows.     Two rows fail even the literal reading: no constant bounds their distance from pathwidth.\n453\t\n454\tTheorem 2.6 (two rows are false).\n455\t\n456\t   1. PLA folding: for a matrix with a 1, max(pw(GM ) + 1, ⌈|N|/2⌉) ≤ pla(M), and the gap to pw + 1 is\n457\t      unbounded. The 5 × 5 identity needs 3 tracks while pw(GM ) + 1 = 1, and the path Pn as a matrix,\n458\t      one gate per edge, has t = 2 and pla ≥ ⌈n/2⌉, so the gap is unbounded on connected instances too\n459\t      (Möhring, 1990, Prop. 3.15) (Appendix A, A.13).\n460\t\n461\t   2. Edge separation read as cutwidth: pw ≤ cw always, but cw(K1,n ) ≥ n/2 and mcw(K1,n ) ≥ n/2 − 1\n462\t      while pw(K1,n ) = 1; so neither cutwidth reading is within a constant of pathwidth (Appendix A, A.14).\n463\t\n464\t    For the table, these rows are false as stated. Each has an exact variant (Theorem 2.4): the PLA claim\n465\tholds for multiple folding, and the edge-separation reference proves exactly the vertex separator game.\n466\t    Several published equalities need a nondegeneracy hypothesis, and one published proof has a gap that\n467\twe repair; Appendix B.2 gives the details. Appendix B.3 adds a thirteenth problem, outside the table, to the\n468\texact core.\n469\t  1 sb(K ) ≥ 2 is also argued by hand: splits of a tree are trees and keep at least three leaves.\n470\t        1,3\n471\t\n472\t\n473\t\n474\t\n475\t                                                                 9\n476\t\fSummary. Eight of the twelve rows of the Linhares–Yanasse table are exact, counting the pathwidth row\n477\titself, which is the reference. The other seven are pathwidth, or pathwidth plus one, exactly: MOSP, gate\n478\tmatrix layout, one-dimensional logic, interval thickness, node search, narrowness and vertex separation. So\n479\tare two variants of the false rows, multiple folding and Lengauer’s vertex separator game. Split bandwidth\n480\tand edge search are bands of width one and two, within one of the number of open stacks but not at a fixed\n481\toffset. Simple PLA folding and edge separation are false as stated. One theorem is stated and not proved,\n482\tLaPaugh’s es = pes, and no row needs it. One of the table’s references, Kashiwabara and Fujisawa (1979),\n483\tis not held; its row is proved from Möhring (1990) and Kirousis and Papadimitriou (1985).\n484\t\n485\t\n486\t3    The names and the communities\n487\tSection 2 showed that eight of the twelve rows of the Linhares–Yanasse table are one quantity. A result\n488\tproved under any of their names therefore holds under all of them. But a reader who searches under one\n489\tname will not find a result published under another. This section asks two questions about the literature.\n490\tWhich name does it use for the quantity? And, besides that name, which community has been most active?\n491\tThe answers are pathwidth, by far (Figure 3.1), and open stacks (Table 3.1). The second answer explains\n492\twhere the best exact algorithm for the family came from, and leads to Section 4.\n493\t\n494\tHow we counted. We searched OpenAlex (Priem et al., 2022) on 29 September 2026 for each problem’s\n495\tname and its common variants, in titles and abstracts, within Computer Science, Mathematics, Engineering\n496\tand Decision Sciences. A search hit need not use the name in the table’s sense: “narrowness” matches term\n497\trewriting, and “path-width” matches roads and tool paths. So we read the 2,271 hits and kept the 1,593 that\n498\tare about one of the twelve problems. The 700 hits for the eleven smaller names were judged one by one,\n499\tby one annotator with model assistance; the 1,571 pathwidth hits were filtered by a stated rule, checked on\n500\tsamples. A mechanical phrase rule agrees with the judgements on 78% of the 700 and leads to the same\n501\tconclusions. The counts are lower bounds, since a work can study a problem without naming it in its abstract.\n502\tAppendix C gives the method in full, the robustness check and the counts per name.\n503\t\n504\t            pathwidth                                                                                          1,213\n505\t    gate matrix layout                 110\n506\t           PLA folding            68\n507\t                 MOSP             58\n508\t    vertex separation             55\n509\t          edge search            38\n510\t          node search            34\n511\tone-dimensional logic        11\n512\t    interval thickness       6\n513\t                                                                                          discipline in the 2002 table\n514\t      edge separation 0 (of 21 search hits, none relevant)                                      operations research\n515\t           narrowness 0 (of 35 search hits, none relevant)                                      VLSI design\n516\t       split bandwidth 0 (of 35 search hits, none relevant)                                     graph theory\n517\t\n518\t                         0               200       400             600        800          1000           1200\n519\t                                               relevant works using the name in title or abstract\n520\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d20ab00f-ced5-4701-8f82-053c98f1df8f","uuid":"e884acc0-b697-4c19-a3f3-9b45c7c78fcd","timestamp":"2026-10-06T20:14:39.768Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/main.txt","content":"                                     The pathwidth complex\n                          Alexandre Linhares                 Horacio Hideki Yanasse\n                           affiliation to be added              affiliation to be added\n\n                                             Draft, 6 October 2026\n\n\n                                                     Abstract\n      The minimization of open stacks problem (MOSP) asks for the order in which to make a set of patterns\n      so that the fewest customer orders are open at once. Linhares and Yanasse (2002) listed twelve problems\n      from operations research, VLSI design and graph theory, MOSP among them, as equal to it “plus or\n      minus one”, and gave no proofs. We ask which of these equalities are true, and what follows when results,\n      algorithms and benchmarks are moved between the twelve problems through pathwidth, the quantity that\n      connects them. We find four things. First, eight of the twelve rows are exact, counting pathwidth itself:\n      each equals pathwidth or pathwidth plus one. Two lie in bands one and two wide, and two are false as\n      stated; every relation is proved, and machine-checked in Lean. Second, the literature has settled on the\n      name pathwidth, used more than three times as often as the other eleven names together, and the three\n      communities rarely cite one another. Third, the best exact algorithm for MOSP, the search of Chu and\n      Stuckey (2009), is a pathwidth algorithm, and two of its published dominance theorems are false. The\n      correct rule was already known for pathwidth; we show that it is tested by one bipartite matching per\n      candidate, and prove the repaired search sound. In our implementation it costs at most 2% more search\n      nodes on each benchmark set, and it re-proved all 115 benchmark optima that rested on the published\n      rules, changing none. Fourth, we release 17,714 benchmark graphs, distinct up to isomorphism, from\n      twenty collections, 16,087 of them with a certified pathwidth and a layout that attains it.\n\n\n1    Introduction\nA factory makes a set of patterns, one at a time, and each customer’s order needs some of them. A customer’s\nstack opens when the first pattern the customer needs is made, and it closes when the last one is made. The\nminimization of open stacks problem (MOSP) asks for the order of the patterns that keeps the fewest stacks\nopen at any one time. In the cutting industry, where the problem arose, the patterns are cutting patterns and\nthe customers are piece types (Yanasse, 1997b). Figure 1.1(a) gives a small instance. Six customers, a to f ,\nneed four patterns: P1 = {a, b}, P2 = {b, c, d}, P3 = {c, d, e} and P4 = {e, f }. Made in order A, P1 P2 P3 P4 ,\nthe patterns keep at most 3 stacks open. Made in order B, P1 P3 P4 P2 , they keep 5 open, because customer b\nis served first but waits for P2 , which comes last. Of the 24 orders, 2 reach 3 (order A and its reverse), 16\ngive 4 and 6 give 5. MOSP is NP-hard (Linhares, 2001; Linhares and Yanasse, 2002).\n    The same matrix is a problem in VLSI design. In a gate matrix layout the patterns are gates, drawn as\nvertical lines in some order, and the customers are nets. Each net is a horizontal wire from the first gate it\nconnects to the last, and wires that overlap must lie on different horizontal tracks. The task is to order the\ngates so that the fewest tracks suffice. Figure 1.1(b) and (c) draw the two orders. The wires are the same;\nonly the gate order changes which wires overlap. Order A packs into 3 tracks and order B needs 5. The\nnumber of tracks a gate order needs is its density, the most wires crossing any one gate, and that is exactly\nthe number of open stacks there. So the two problems compute the same number from the same matrix\n(Linhares and Yanasse, 2002, Proposition 2).\n\n\n                                                         1\n\f(a)                              (b)                                   (c)                                 (d)\n      P1     P2    P3     P4           P1       P2       P3       P4           P1       P3       P4   P2\n                                   a        c                 f            a        c\n a     1      0     0      0                                                                                             c\n                                  b                  e                     b\n b     1      1     0      0                                                                                a     b               e   f\n                                            d                                       d                                    d\n  c    0      1     1      0\n                                                                                    e\n d     0      1     1      0                3 tracks\n                                                                                             f                         {a, b}\n e     0      0     1      1\n                                                                                                                      {b, c, d}\n  f    0      0     0      1                                                        5 tracks\n                                                                                                                      {c, d, e}\n A     2      3     3      2\n                                                                                                                       {e, f}\n B     2      4     5      3\n      open stacks per position                                                                                   bags of A: width 2\n\n\nFigure 1.1: One instance, three readings, one number. (a) Six customers and four patterns, with each customer’s open\nstack shaded in order A; order A keeps at most 3 stacks open and order B at most 5, and only 2 of the 24 orders reach\n3. (b, c) The same matrix as a gate matrix layout: the same two orders need 3 and 5 tracks (the shaded gate is where\nthe density peaks). (d) The same matrix as a graph, one clique per pattern: the open sets of order A are the bags of a\npath decomposition of width 2, so the optimum is the pathwidth plus one (Theorem 2.3).\n\n\n     The same matrix is also a graph. Make each customer a vertex, and join two customers when some\npattern needs both, so that each pattern becomes a clique (Figure 1.1(d)). Write down, for order A, the set\nof open stacks at each position: {a, b}, {b, c, d}, {c, d, e}, {e, f }. These sets cover every vertex and every\nedge, and each customer appears in a run of consecutive sets. In graph theory such a sequence is a path\ndecomposition, its sets are bags, and its width is the size of its largest bag minus one. The least width over\nall path decompositions is the pathwidth of the graph, here 2. The construction works in both directions, so\nthe fewest open stacks is always the pathwidth plus one (Theorem 2.3).\n     Linhares and Yanasse (2002) collected twelve such problems, from operations research, VLSI design\nand graph theory, in the table that Table 1.1 reproduces. Each computes, on corresponding inputs, “a function\n f (Π) that is either equal to the number of open stacks or closely related to it (plus or minus one)” (Linhares\nand Yanasse, 2002, p. 1764). The table gives a reference for each problem, but no proofs and no graph of\nwhich problem reduces to which. “Plus or minus one” has two readings. Under the literal reading, f is\nwithin one of the number of open stacks on every input. Under the fixed-offset reading, f equals the number\nof open stacks plus one constant, the same on every input. Only the second makes solving one problem the\nsame as solving another. We call the twelve the pathwidth complex, because pathwidth is both the name\nthe literature uses most (Section 3) and the quantity through which every true relation in the table is proved\n(Section 2). This paper asks: which of the equalities in the Linhares–Yanasse table are true, and what follows\nwhen results, algorithms and benchmarks are moved between the twelve problems through pathwidth?\n     The table has a history that explains why the question is still open. It was first printed in the first author’s\ndoctoral thesis (Linhares, 2001, Table 2.1, p. 13), supervised by the second author, which proved MOSP NP-\nhard and equal to gate matrix layout. Its rows were collected over the years of the doctorate, while reading\nthe literature of each field. Each came with a reference and nothing else. No row’s relation to open stacks\nwas checked, no published proof was restated, and no graph said which problem reduces to which. The\ntable appeared again, row for row, as Table 1 of Linhares and Yanasse (2002), and it has been read since as\na statement of equivalence. Garcia de la Banda and Stuckey (2007), for instance, credit the speed of their\nsearch in this journal to “the fact that the problem is fixed parameter tractable (Linhares and Yanasse 2002)”.\nSo the table is already used to justify algorithms, and whether it is true matters. If it is true, every result\nabout any one of the twelve problems transfers to all the others. Looking back, the table was the thesis’s\nmost important finding, and this article does what the thesis did not: it states each problem precisely, proves\n\n                                                                       2\n\fTable 1.1: Twelve problems from three fields, published as one family: eight rows are exact, counting the pathwidth\nrow itself; two are bands; two are false as the table states them. The Linhares–Yanasse table, reproduced from Linhares\nand Yanasse (2002, Table 1, p. 1764) with its row order, wording and references as printed, and its caption: “Display\nof a family of equivalent problems studied independently on the literature”. The shaded column is ours. It gives the\nrelation to pathwidth (pw) that is true, proved in Section 2 (Table 2.3).\n\nProblem                      Discipline             References                                   This paper\nMOSP                         Operations research    Yanasse (1997b); Fink and Voß (1999)         exact: pw + 1\nGate matrix layout           VLSI design            Möhring (1990); Wing et al. (1985)           exact: pw + 1\nOne-dimensional logic        VLSI design            Ohtsuki et al. (1979)                        exact: pw + 1\nPLA folding                  VLSI design            Möhring (1990)                               falsea\nInterval thickness           Graph theory           Kashiwabara and Fujisawa (1979)              exact: pw + 1\nNode search game             Graph theory           Kirousis and Papadimitriou (1985)            exact: pw + 1\nEdge search game             Graph theory           Kirousis and Papadimitriou (1986)            band: pw to pw + 2\nNarrowness                   Graph theory           Kornai and Tuza (1992)                       exact: pw + 1\nSplit bandwidth              Graph theory           Fomin (1998)                                 band: pw to pw + 1\nGraph path-width             Graph theory           Kinnersley (1992)                            exact: pw (reference)\nEdge separation              Graph theory           Lengauer (1981)                              falseb\nVertex separation            Graph theory           Kinnersley (1992)                            exact: pw\na Exact (pw + 1) for multiple folding, with any number of nets per track. b As cutwidth; the vertex separator game of the same\n\nreference is exact (pw, for graphs with an edge).\n\n\nwhat is true and refutes what is not. The communities that could have checked the table rarely read each\nother: of 844 works that cite the table’s papers, only six cite both an open-stacks paper and a graph-theory\npaper (Section 3).\n\nWhat we find.\n\n     • The table, settled. Eight of the twelve rows are exact, the pathwidth row itself counted as one of them:\n       the other seven equal pathwidth or pathwidth plus one on every input, outside degenerate cases such\n       as an empty graph. Two rows, split bandwidth and edge search, lie in bands one and two wide. Two\n       rows, PLA folding and edge separation, are false as stated, with counterexamples. A variant of each\n       false row is exact (Section 2).\n\n     • One name. The literature has settled on “pathwidth”, which has more than three times as many\n       relevant works as the other eleven names together. Besides pathwidth, the most active community\n       over 2005–2024 is the open-stacks one, and the exact search of Section 4 came from it (Section 3).\n\n     • A repaired search. The best exact algorithm for MOSP, the search of Chu and Stuckey (2009), is\n       also a pathwidth algorithm. Two of its published dominance theorems are false, as are their later\n       restatements (Chu, 2011; Fink, 2012). The correct rule was already known in the exact pathwidth\n       literature, and we show that it can be tested by one bipartite matching per candidate. The repaired\n       search is proved sound. Over each benchmark set it changes the total number of search nodes by at\n       most 2%, and no wrong answer of the published rules on a whole instance is known (Section 4).\n\n     • Benchmarks re-certified, and a dataset. The repaired search re-proved all 115 benchmark optima that\n       rested on the published rules, and none changed (as of 6 October 2026). We release 17,714 graphs,\n       distinct up to isomorphism, from twenty benchmark collections of the twelve problems; 16,087 have\n       a certified pathwidth with a layout that attains it, and so a certified value for every exact problem in\n       the table (Section 5).\n\n\n                                                              3\n\fHow to read the paper. Section 2 states the twelve problems and proves what is true. Section 3 measures\nwhich names and communities the literature uses. Section 4 reads the exact open-stacks search as a path-\nwidth algorithm, finds the two false rules and proves the repaired search sound. Section 5 reports its cost and\nthe dataset, and Section 6 answers the question above. Every theorem we state is machine-checked in the\nLean 4 proof assistant. The reader need not read Lean: the proofs are given in the text, and Appendix A maps\neach result to its formal statement. Appendix B holds the supporting material, Appendix C the bibliometric\nmethod, and the data, code and proofs are described at the end of the paper (page 26).\n\n\n2     The equivalences, proved\nThis section answers the first half of our question: which rows of the Linhares–Yanasse table are true, and\nin what sense. Section 1 separated two readings of “plus or minus one”. We call a row exact when it meets\nthe fixed-offset reading: its value is pathwidth, or pathwidth plus one, on every input. We call it a band\nwhen it meets only the literal reading: its value stays within a fixed distance of pathwidth, and both ends\nof that range occur. We call it false when it meets neither. The answer is that eight rows are exact, two are\nbands and two are false. Section 2.1 states the problems. Section 2.2 proves the answer in four steps: the\nhub theorem, which ties MOSP to pathwidth; the other exact rows; the bands; and the false rows. Table 2.3\nand Figure 2.1 summarise it.\n     All theorems below are verified in the Lean proof assistant, unless the text says otherwise; Appendix A\nlists the formal statements. Where a result is published, we cite the published proof and do not repeat it.\n\nNotation. Table 2.1 lists the symbols used in the rest of the paper. Graphs are finite and simple. A 0/1\nmatrix M has the customers as rows and the patterns as columns; in the VLSI problems the rows are nets\nand the columns gates. Row r is active at position j of a column order π if it has 1s in two columns x, y with\nπ(x) ≤ j ≤ π(y), possibly x = y: its stack is open there. “M has a 1” excludes only the all-zero matrix. One\nletter needs care. In this section k is the bound in each decision problem, so for pathwidth it bounds a width.\nIn Section 4 k bounds the number of open stacks, which is one more than a width; we say so again there.\n\n2.1   The problems\nEach problem is a decision problem with an instance and an integer k; its value is the least k for which the\nanswer is yes. Four of the twelve carry the rest of the paper, and we define them in full. Figure 1.1 shows\neach on one instance.\nPathwidth pw(G). Is there a sequence of bags X1 , . . . , Xr ⊆ V covering V , with every edge inside some bag,\n     Xi ∩ Xℓ ⊆ X j whenever i ≤ j ≤ ℓ, and |Xi | ≤ k + 1? (Kinnersley, 1992, p. 346). The condition on\n     Xi ∩ Xℓ is the interval property: the bags that contain a vertex are consecutive.\nVertex separation vs(G). Is there a layout L such that for every i < n at most k vertices u with L(u) ≤ i\n     have a neighbour v with L(v) > i? (Kinnersley, 1992, p. 346).\nMOSP Z(M). Given a 0/1 matrix M of customers by patterns, is there an order of the patterns with at most\n   k customers active at every position? (Linhares and Yanasse, 2002, eqs. (1)–(2)).\nGate matrix layout t(M). Given a net–gate matrix, is there an order of the gates and an assignment of nets\n     to k tracks such that nets active at a common position get different tracks? (Möhring, 1990, p. 18);\n     (Wing et al., 1985, Problem 1).\n   The other eight problems are needed only to read the table. Table 2.2 gives each in one line; Ap-\npendix B.1 gives the formal definitions, with the sources.\n\n                                                      4\n\fTable 2.1: Notation. Each symbol has one meaning throughout the paper; the symbols of the search are defined in\nSection 4.1.\n\nSymbol                     Meaning\nG = (V, E), n = |V |       a graph, its vertices and edges; in Section 4 the vertices are the customers, V = C\nL                          a layout, a bijection V → {1, . . . , n}\nX1 , . . . , Xr            the bags of a path decomposition\nM; C; P                    a 0/1 matrix; its rows (customers, nets); its columns (patterns, gates)\nGM                         the MOSP graph: vertex set C, two customers adjacent when some pattern has a 1 in both\n                           rows; each pattern becomes a clique\nH                          the net graph of a circuit: two nets adjacent when they share a gate\nπ                          an order of the columns\nZ(M), t(M)                 the MOSP value (fewest open stacks) and the gate matrix layout value (fewest tracks)\npw, vs                     pathwidth and vertex separation\nθ , ns, mns, es, pes, ν,   the values of the other problems (Table 2.2)\nsb, cw, mcw, VSG,\npla\nk                          the bound of a decision problem; in Section 4, a bound on open stacks, so width k − 1\nw                          a width, as in “pathwidth w”\nN[c]                       the closed neighbourhood of c: c and its neighbours\n∂ T , b(T )                the border of a vertex set T , the vertices outside T with a neighbour in T ; b(T ) = |∂ T |\n\n\n\n\nTable 2.2: The other eight problems of the Linhares–Yanasse table look unrelated (two circuit layouts, two search\ngames and four graph problems), yet each asks for the least k that is possible. One line each. Formal definitions are in\nAppendix B.1.\n\nProblem                       In one line\nOne-dimensional logic         Order the gates of a circuit so that the intervals spanned by the nets overlap at most k at\n                              any point. Its graph is the net graph H, which joins two nets that share a gate. A\n                              boundary variant pins two gates to the ends.\nPLA folding pla(M)            Gate matrix layout in at most k tracks, with at most two nets on a track. With no limit per\n                              track (multiple folding) it is gate matrix layout itself.\nInterval thickness θ (G)      Add edges to G to make an interval graph whose largest clique has at most k vertices.\nNode search ns(G)             Searchers are placed on and removed from vertices. An edge is cleared when both its\n                              ends hold a searcher, and becomes contaminated again if a searcher-free path joins it to a\n                              contaminated edge. Clear every edge with at most k searchers at once. mns(G) is the\n                              same with no recontamination allowed.\nEdge search es(G)             Node search with a third move: sliding a searcher along an edge clears it. pes(G) is the\n                              same over progressive strategies, which never recontaminate an edge.\nNarrowness ν(G)               Add the vertices one at a time in some order, and drop each as soon as all its neighbours\n                              have been added. Never hold more than k.\nSplit bandwidth sb(G)         Repeatedly split a vertex into an edge, sharing its neighbours between the two ends, so\n                              that the result has a layout in which adjacent vertices are at most k apart (its bandwidth).\nEdge separation               The table’s reference (Lengauer, 1981) supports three readings: cutwidth cw(G), the\n                              most edges crossing a gap of a layout; modified cutwidth mcw(G), the most edges\n                              passing strictly over a vertex; and Lengauer’s vertex separator game VSG(G), a pebbling\n                              game that counts the unpebbled vertices adjacent to pebbled ones.\n\n\n\n\n                                                             5\n\fTable 2.3: The Linhares–Yanasse table, settled. Eight rows are exact, two are bands and two are false, and every true\nrelation is a relation to pathwidth. Relation: what is true and proved in Lean, on the graph the problem lives on (the\nMOSP graph or the net graph H for the matrix problems, the input graph otherwise). Published source: the published\nwork the row rests on; the Lean proofs are listed in Appendix A. Status: exact, a fixed offset; band, an inequality\nwhose ends both occur; false, the table’s claim fails, with a counterexample family proved in Lean.\n\nProblem                 Published source               Relation                                               Status\nMOSP                    Yanasse (1997a);               Z(M) = pw(GM ) + 1 (M has a 1)                         exact\n                        Fellows and Langston\n                        (1989); Linhares and\n                        Yanasse (2002)\nGate matrix layout      Möhring (1990)                 t(M) = Z(M) = pw(GM ) + 1                              exact\nOne-dim. logic          Ohtsuki et al. (1979)          tracks = θ (H) = pw(H) + 1; boundary                   exact\n                                                       variant ≥ pw(H) + 1\nPLA folding             Möhring (1990)                 max(pw + 1, ⌈|N|/2⌉) ≤ pla, gap unbounded;             false; variant exact\n                                                       multiple folding = pw + 1\nInterval thickness      Möhring (1990);                θ = pw + 1 (V ̸= /0)                                   exact\n                        Kirousis and\n                        Papadimitriou (1985)\nNode search             Kirousis and                   ns = mns = vs + 1 = pw + 1 = θ (E ̸= /0)               exact\n                        Papadimitriou (1985,\n                        1986); Bienstock and\n                        Seymour (1991)\nEdge search             Ellis et al. (1994);           vs ≤ es ≤ vs + 2, also for pes                         band\n                        Kirousis and\n                        Papadimitriou (1986)\nNarrowness              Kornai and Tuza (1992)         ν = pw + 1 (V ̸= /0)                                   exact\nSplit bandwidth         Fomin (1998)                   pw ≤ sb ≤ pw + 1                                       band\nPathwidth               Kinnersley (1992)              the reference                                          exact\nEdge separationa        Lengauer (1981)                cw, mcw unbounded against pw (stars);                  false; VSG exact\n                                                       VSG = max(1, vs)\nVertex separation       Kinnersley (1992)              vs = pw                                                exact\na Also misattributed: what Lengauer (1981) proves exactly is the vertex separator game, which is the table’s vertex separation row.\n\n\n\n\n2.2    What is true\nTable 2.3 gives the answer row by row, and Figure 2.1 draws it as a graph around pathwidth. The rest of this\nsubsection proves it.\n\nThe hub. Two theorems tie the table to pathwidth. The first joins the two graph rows, vertex separation\nand pathwidth. The second joins MOSP to pathwidth, and through it all four matrix problems. The second\nrests on one lemma about path decompositions. For a vertex v, let first(v) and last(v) be the least and greatest\nindices of bags containing v; by the interval property, v ∈ Xi exactly when first(v) ≤ i ≤ last(v).\nLemma 2.1 (cliques sit in a bag). In a path decomposition of G, every clique of G lies inside one bag (Ap-\npendix A, A.1).\nProof. The bags containing a vertex u of the clique form the interval [first(u), last(u)]. Two vertices of the\nclique are adjacent, so their edge lies in a bag and their intervals meet. Pairwise intersecting intervals of a\nline have a common point (the Helly property): let a be the largest left end; every right end is at least every\nleft end, so at least a, and a lies in all of them. So all the intervals share an index i, and the clique lies in\nXi .\n\n                                                                  6\n\f                  ✗ gap unbounded\n\n\n                                    =t\n                                             t(M)               =Z\n  simple PLA             multiple                                      Z(M)                 VSG\n    folding              folding          gate matrix                                   vertex game\n                                                                       MOSP\n                                            layout\n\n\n                                                                 Z = pw + 1             = max(1, vs)\n\n\n\n    tracks      = θ(H)         θ(G)      θ = pw + 1                           vs = pw     vs(G)        ≤ vs + 2      pes(G)\n                                                           pw(G)\n   one-dim.                  interval                                                     vertex                   progressive\n     logic                                               pathwidth\n                            thickness                                                   separation                 edge search\n\n\n                                                                ✗ unbounded               = vs + 1\n                                                   ν = pw + 1     on stars                              ≤ vs + 2         es = pes?\n                                pw ≤ sb ≤ pw + 1\n\n                      sb(G)                                                               mns(G)\n                                             ν(G)                     cw, mcw                                         es(G)\n                       split                                          cutwidth           monotone\n                                          narrowness                                                               edge search\n                    bandwidth                                                           node search\n\n\n                                                                                               ns = mns\n\n\n                                                                                           ns(G)\n                                                                                        node search\n\n\n\nFigure 2.1: Pathwidth is the hub of the table: every exact relation is one or two steps from it, and the only failures\nare the two false claims, in dark red. Solid edges are exact relations and dashed edges bands, both proved in Lean; the\ndotted edge is LaPaugh’s es = pes, the one relation not proved, which no row needs. Each false claim is refuted by\na counterexample family proved in Lean. The four matrix problems (top left) live on the MOSP graph GM or the net\ngraph H. Hypotheses are omitted: M has a 1, V ̸= /0, or G has an edge, as in Table 2.3. Two edges that follow from the\nothers, mns = θ and ns = vs + 1, are not drawn.\n\n\n\n\n                                                                  7\n\f    The first theorem is known. It says the two graph rows of the table are the same quantity.\n\nTheorem 2.2 (vertex separation is pathwidth; known). For every graph, vs(G) = pw(G) (Appendix A, A.2).\n\n    This is Theorem 3.1 of Kinnersley (1992). Both directions are constructions. From a layout of separation\nk, each bag is the set counted at a cut together with the next vertex. From a decomposition, ordering the\nvertices by first gives a layout of the same width.\n    The second theorem is the one the rest of the paper uses. In Figure 1.1(d), the sets of customers active\nat each step of the good order are the bags of a path decomposition of the MOSP graph. The theorem says\nthat this always works, in both directions.\n\nTheorem 2.3 (MOSP is pathwidth plus one). If M has a 1, then Z(M) = pw(GM ) + 1 (Appendix A, A.3).\n\nProof. (≤) Take a decomposition of GM of width k. The rows of each column p form a clique, so by\nLemma 2.1 they lie in a bag Xβ (p) . Order the columns by β (p). If row r is active at the position of column p,\nit has columns x, y with β (x) ≤ β (p) ≤ β (y), and r ∈ Xβ (x) ∩ Xβ (y) , so the interval property gives r ∈ Xβ (p) .\nAt most k + 1 rows are active there.\n    (≥) Take an order with at most Z = Z(M) active rows at every position. The active sets, with a singleton\nbag for each row with no 1, form a path decomposition: rows sharing a column are both active at its position,\nand a row is active on an interval of positions. Its width is Z − 1, using Z ≥ 1.\n\n    For the table, this settles the MOSP row as exact, with offset one, on the MOSP graph. Read as a cutting\nproblem, Theorem 2.3 is Proposition 5 of Yanasse (1997a), which turns each pattern into a clique on its\npiece types. Read as a layout problem, it is Theorem 7 of Fellows and Langston (1989), with the column-\nexpansion Lemma 4.1 of Fellows and Langston (1987). Linhares and Yanasse (2002, Prop. 2) identify\nMOSP with gate matrix layout. The proof above needs no assumption that the instance is reduced.\n    The graph matters. The MOSP literature also uses a second graph, with the patterns as vertices, joined\nwhen they share a customer, to split an instance into independent parts (Yanasse and Senne, 2010). It is\nnatural to expect its pathwidth to measure the open stacks as well; it does not, in either direction. One\npattern shared by four customers who each have a private pattern has Z = 4, while its pattern graph is the\nstar K1,4 , of pathwidth 1 (Appendix A, A.4). A single customer with many patterns errs the other way.\n\nThe exact core. The other exact rows attach to Theorems 2.2 and 2.3 by published arguments. We do\nnot reprove them on paper; each item below cites the published proof, and the Lean proofs are listed in\nAppendix A. We call the problems that equal pathwidth or pathwidth plus one on every input the exact core:\nthe eight exact rows, with multiple folding and the vertex separator game.\n\nTheorem 2.4 (the exact core).\n\n   1. Gate matrix layout is MOSP on the same matrix: if M has a 1, t(M) = Z(M) = pw(GM ) + 1. For a\n      fixed gate order the fewest tracks equals the most nets active at one position, by the left-edge algorithm\n      (Möhring, 1990, p. 31) (Appendix A, A.5).\n\n   2. Interval thickness: if V ̸= /0, θ (G) = pw(G) + 1 (Möhring, 1990, Prop. 3.5) (Appendix A, A.6).\n\n   3. One-dimensional logic: if every net lies on a gate, the fewest tracks is θ (H), and if some gate connects\n      a net it is pw(H) + 1 (Ohtsuki et al., 1979, Thm 3) (Appendix A, A.7).\n\n   4. Narrowness: if V ̸= /0, ν(G) = pw(G) + 1, and for each order σ , ν(σ ) = vs(σ rev ) + 1 (Kornai and\n      Tuza, 1992, Prop. 3.1) (Appendix A, A.8).\n\n\n\n                                                         8\n\f   5. Node search: for every graph ns(G) = mns(G), and if E ̸= /0, ns(G) = vs(G) + 1 = pw(G) + 1 = θ (G)\n      (Kirousis and Papadimitriou, 1985, Thm); (Kirousis and Papadimitriou, 1986, Thms 2.3, 4.1) (Ap-\n      pendix A, A.9).\n\n   6. Multiple folding: the fewest tracks is t(M) = pw(GM ) + 1 (Möhring, 1990, Thm 3.14) (Appendix A,\n      A.10).\n\n   7. Lengauer’s vertex separator game: VSG(G) = max(1, vs(G)) (Lengauer, 1981, Thm 4) (Appendix A,\n      A.11).\n\n    For the table, this makes the gate matrix layout, one-dimensional logic, interval thickness, node search\nand narrowness rows exact, and gives an exact variant of each false row. A single certified pathwidth\ntherefore answers every problem in the exact core; Section 5.4 uses this.\n\nBands.    Two rows are not fixed offsets of pathwidth, but stay within a fixed distance of it.\n\nTheorem 2.5 (bands). For every graph, pw(G) ≤ sb(G) ≤ pw(G) + 1 (Fomin, 1998, Thm 8), and vs(G) ≤\nes(G) ≤ vs(G) + 2, and the same for pes (Ellis et al., 1994, Thm 2.1) (Appendix A, A.12).\n\n     Every end of both bands occurs. K2 has sb = pw = 1 and es = vs = 1; the star K1,3 has pw = 1 and\nsb = 2, and vs = 1 with es = 2; and K3,3 has vs = 3 with es = 5. These values are computed by brute force\nfrom each quantity’s definition, not proved in Lean.1 Fomin (1998) assumes a connected graph with at least\ntwo vertices; the Lean proof needs neither. LaPaugh’s equality es = pes (LaPaugh, 1993) is the one relation\nwe state and do not prove, and no row needs it.\n     For the table, this means the following. Relative to the number of open stacks Z = pw + 1, split band-\nwidth lies in [Z − 1, Z] and edge search in [Z − 1, Z + 1]. Both stay within one of Z, so they meet the\nliteral reading of “plus or minus one”. Since both ends occur, neither is a fixed offset, so neither meets the\nfixed-offset reading.\n\nFalse rows.     Two rows fail even the literal reading: no constant bounds their distance from pathwidth.\n\nTheorem 2.6 (two rows are false).\n\n   1. PLA folding: for a matrix with a 1, max(pw(GM ) + 1, ⌈|N|/2⌉) ≤ pla(M), and the gap to pw + 1 is\n      unbounded. The 5 × 5 identity needs 3 tracks while pw(GM ) + 1 = 1, and the path Pn as a matrix,\n      one gate per edge, has t = 2 and pla ≥ ⌈n/2⌉, so the gap is unbounded on connected instances too\n      (Möhring, 1990, Prop. 3.15) (Appendix A, A.13).\n\n   2. Edge separation read as cutwidth: pw ≤ cw always, but cw(K1,n ) ≥ n/2 and mcw(K1,n ) ≥ n/2 − 1\n      while pw(K1,n ) = 1; so neither cutwidth reading is within a constant of pathwidth (Appendix A, A.14).\n\n    For the table, these rows are false as stated. Each has an exact variant (Theorem 2.4): the PLA claim\nholds for multiple folding, and the edge-separation reference proves exactly the vertex separator game.\n    Several published equalities need a nondegeneracy hypothesis, and one published proof has a gap that\nwe repair; Appendix B.2 gives the details. Appendix B.3 adds a thirteenth problem, outside the table, to the\nexact core.\n  1 sb(K ) ≥ 2 is also argued by hand: splits of a tree are trees and keep at least three leaves.\n        1,3\n\n\n\n\n                                                                 9\n\fSummary. Eight of the twelve rows of the Linhares–Yanasse table are exact, counting the pathwidth row\nitself, which is the reference. The other seven are pathwidth, or pathwidth plus one, exactly: MOSP, gate\nmatrix layout, one-dimensional logic, interval thickness, node search, narrowness and vertex separation. So\nare two variants of the false rows, multiple folding and Lengauer’s vertex separator game. Split bandwidth\nand edge search are bands of width one and two, within one of the number of open stacks but not at a fixed\noffset. Simple PLA folding and edge separation are false as stated. One theorem is stated and not proved,\nLaPaugh’s es = pes, and no row needs it. One of the table’s references, Kashiwabara and Fujisawa (1979),\nis not held; its row is proved from Möhring (1990) and Kirousis and Papadimitriou (1985).\n\n\n3    The names and the communities\nSection 2 showed that eight of the twelve rows of the Linhares–Yanasse table are one quantity. A result\nproved under any of their names therefore holds under all of them. But a reader who searches under one\nname will not find a result published under another. This section asks two questions about the literature.\nWhich name does it use for the quantity? And, besides that name, which community has been most active?\nThe answers are pathwidth, by far (Figure 3.1), and open stacks (Table 3.1). The second answer explains\nwhere the best exact algorithm for the family came from, and leads to Section 4.\n\nHow we counted. We searched OpenAlex (Priem et al., 2022) on 29 September 2026 for each problem’s\nname and its common variants, in titles and abstracts, within Computer Science, Mathematics, Engineering\nand Decision Sciences. A search hit need not use the name in the table’s sense: “narrowness” matches term\nrewriting, and “path-width” matches roads and tool paths. So we read the 2,271 hits and kept the 1,593 that\nare about one of the twelve problems. The 700 hits for the eleven smaller names were judged one by one,\nby one annotator with model assistance; the 1,571 pathwidth hits were filtered by a stated rule, checked on\nsamples. A mechanical phrase rule agrees with the judgements on 78% of the 700 and leads to the same\nconclusions. The counts are lower bounds, since a work can study a problem without naming it in its abstract.\nAppendix C gives the method in full, the robustness check and the counts per name.\n\n            pathwidth                                                                                          1,213\n    gate matrix layout                 110\n           PLA folding            68\n                 MOSP             58\n    vertex separation             55\n          edge search            38\n          node search            34\none-dimensional logic        11\n    interval thickness       6\n                                                                                          discipline in the 2002 table\n      edge separation 0 (of 21 search hits, none relevant)                                      operations research\n           narrowness 0 (of 35 search hits, none relevant)                                      VLSI design\n       split bandwidth 0 (of 35 search hits, none relevant)                                     graph theory\n\n                         0               200       400             600        800          1000           1200\n                                               relevant works using the name in title or abstract\n","numLines":520,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1925}}}
