{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01S5o7B1cMkhjCJUU2iS3WiK","type":"tool_result","content":"520\t\n521\tFigure 3.1: Pathwidth has more relevant works than the other eleven names together, three times over, and three names\n522\thave none. Works using each problem’s name, in title or abstract, in the sense of the Linhares–Yanasse table; all years\n523\tto September 2026. Colour gives the discipline that the table assigns the problem.\n524\t\n525\t\n526\t\n527\t\n528\t                                                              10\n529\t\fTable 3.1: Besides pathwidth, the most active name over 2005–2024 is MOSP, although vertex separation led in 2015–\n530\t2019. Relevant works per five-year period, for the three names with the most works over 2005–2024; the next are edge\n531\tsearch (28) and node search (20).\n532\t\n533\t                    Name                 2005–09     2010–14       2015–19      2020–24     2005–24\n534\t                    Pathwidth                 114         179           267          311         871\n535\t                    MOSP                       17          17             5           12          51\n536\t                    Vertex separation           7          10            15            0          32\n537\t\n538\tTable 3.2: The open-stacks papers are read apart from graph theory. Works citing the papers that the Linhares–Yanasse\n539\ttable cites, by the discipline of the papers they cite. Of 135 works citing an operations-research paper, 6 also cite a\n540\tgraph-theory paper (4%); of 285 citing a VLSI paper, 65 do (23%). The 2002 paper itself counts as operations research.\n541\tOpenAlex, 29 September 2026; 844 citing works in all.\n542\t\n543\t                                                                . . . of which also cite\n544\t                      Works citing           All    operations research       VLSI     graph theory\n545\t                      operations research   135                         –       14               6\n546\t                      VLSI                  285                        14        –              65\n547\t                      graph theory          507                         6       65               –\n548\t\n549\t\n550\t3.1   Pathwidth has absorbed the family\n551\tFigure 3.1 gives the counts. Pathwidth has 1,213 relevant works, 21 times as many as MOSP and more than\n552\tthree times as many as the other eleven names together (380). Three names have no relevant work at all:\n553\tnarrowness, split bandwidth and edge separation. Two more are rare: interval thickness has 6 works and\n554\tone-dimensional logic 11. Pathwidth is also the only name still growing: its 311 works in 2020–2024 are\n555\tmore than the whole history of any other name (Appendix C). So anything one would want to know about\n556\talgorithms for this class of problems was most likely published under “pathwidth”.\n557\t\n558\t3.2   Besides pathwidth, open stacks\n559\tThe VLSI names belong to the 1980s: since 2005 gate matrix layout has 9 relevant works and PLA folding\n560\t6. Table 3.1 shows the three names with the most works over 2005–2024. MOSP is second, with 51, ahead\n561\tof vertex separation (32) and edge search (28). Vertex separation led only in 2015–2019. The open-stacks\n562\tcommunity, in operations research, is thus the one most active besides pathwidth.\n563\t    It has also worked largely apart from graph theory. Table 3.2 counts the 844 works that cite the papers\n564\tof the table, by the disciplines of the papers they cite. Only six works cite both an open-stacks paper and\n565\ta graph-theory paper. Two are by the table’s authors, one is open-stacks work on graph properties, and\n566\tthree are pathwidth or vertex-separation work from computer science. The VLSI papers are read with graph\n567\ttheory about five times as often.\n568\t\n569\t3.3   From an open-stacks challenge to a pathwidth solver\n570\tIt is not a coincidence that the best exact algorithm for MOSP came from the open-stacks community and\n571\tis also, on the published evidence, among the strongest exact algorithms for pathwidth. The account is in\n572\tChu and Stuckey (2009, §1). MOSP was the subject of the first Constraint Modelling Challenge (Smith\n573\tand Gent, 2005), posed in May 2005, which drew 13 entries. The winning entry, by García de la Banda\n574\tand Stuckey, combined dynamic programming with branch and bound and was an order of magnitude faster\n575\t\n576\t\n577\t                                                          11\n578\t\fthan any other (Garcia de la Banda and Stuckey, 2007). Chu and Stuckey (2009) built on it. They branched\n579\ton which customer’s stack closes next rather than on which pattern is made next, recorded the states already\n580\trefuted, added dominance rules, and became five to six orders of magnitude faster on hard instances. Their\n581\tsolver closed every open instance of the challenge within ten seconds. MOSP’s busiest decade in Table 3.1,\n582\t2005–2014, is the decade of this work.\n583\t    The customer search reads an instance only through its MOSP graph (Chu and Stuckey, 2009, p. 4), so it\n584\tdecides pathwidth (Section 4). Ported to graphs, it proves more instances than reported on the benchmarks\n585\twhere published exact pathwidth results exist, those of Coudert et al. (2016). It proves the pathwidth of\n586\t11,194 of the 11,534 Rome graphs (97.1%), against their 95.6%, both within 600 seconds per graph. It\n587\tproves all 50 VSPLIB trees, against their 30, and 39 of the 73 Harwell–Boeing graphs, against their 26.\n588\tThese are comparisons with published numbers obtained on other hardware, not a head-to-head run.\n589\t    Two consequences shape the rest of the paper. First, a result on any of the twelve problems should be\n590\tstated, and searched for, as a result on pathwidth. Section 4 does this for the customer search. It finds that\n591\tthe search’s first dominance rule is, in correct form, a theorem already known in the pathwidth literature,\n592\tand that two of its published theorems are false. Second, a benchmark for any problem in the exact core is\n593\ta pathwidth benchmark, which is how Section 5.4 builds its dataset.\n594\t\n595\t\n596\t4     An exact open-stacks search, read as a pathwidth algorithm\n597\tIf MOSP is pathwidth (Theorem 2.3), then the best exact MOSP algorithm is a pathwidth algorithm, and its\n598\trules can be checked against what the pathwidth literature knows. This section does that for the customer\n599\tsearch of Chu and Stuckey (2009), the search that Section 3 traced from the open-stacks challenge. It\n600\tsearches over the orders in which the customers’ stacks close, not over pattern sequences, and it prunes with\n601\tdominance rules. Three of the rules are stated as theorems (their Theorems 1, 2 and 3) and a fourth, the\n602\tsubset rule, in their §2. Chu and Stuckey (2009) place MOSP among “graph path-width and gate matrix\n603\tlayout”, citing Linhares and Yanasse (2002), but they do not use pathwidth in the search. Any graph is the\n604\tMOSP graph of the instance with one customer per vertex and one pattern per edge, so the search decides\n605\tpathwidth for every graph.\n606\t    The stakes are these. Two of the published dominance theorems, the definite move and the better move,\n607\tare false, and so are their restatements in Chu’s thesis (Chu, 2011) and, with a stronger premise, in Fink\n608\t(2012). Yet no wrong answer is known. No whole instance has been found on which the published rules\n609\twrongly answer “no”. And when every benchmark optimum that rested on the search was proved again with\n610\trepaired rules, no value changed (Section 5). The correct form of the definite move was already known for\n611\tpathwidth, as Tamaki’s commitment lemma, which says when an exact pathwidth search may commit to one\n612\textension of a vertex set and try no other (Section 4.6). We show that it can be tested by one small bipartite\n613\tmatching per candidate. The main result, Theorem 4.8, says informally: with both rules repaired, the search\n614\tis sound; whenever it answers that k stacks do not suffice, they do not, and the graph has pathwidth at least\n615\tk. The repair costs at most 2% more search nodes on each benchmark set (Section 5).\n616\t    Section 4.1 defines the search and shows that it decides pathwidth, in both vocabularies. Section 4.2\n617\tgives the algorithm. Sections 4.3 and 4.4 take the rules one at a time, Section 4.5 proves the repaired search\n618\tsound, and Section 4.6 places each rule in the pathwidth literature.\n619\t\n620\t4.1   The search decides pathwidth\n621\tChu and Stuckey (2009, p. 4) observe that the patterns matter only through the MOSP graph. So fix a graph\n622\tG = (C, E) whose vertices are the customers, and an integer k ≥ 0. In this section k bounds the number of\n623\topen stacks, which is one more than a width: k stacks suffice exactly when the pathwidth is at most k − 1\n624\t\n625\t\n626\t                                                     12\n627\t\f(Theorem 4.2). For a customer c write N[c] for its closed neighbourhood, the stacks that must be open when\n628\tc’s stack closes. For a set T ⊆ C of vertices, its border ∂ T is the set of vertices outside T with a neighbour\n629\tin T . For a set T of closed customers write\n630\t                 S\n631\t       O(T ) =     c∈T N[c]                                     the stacks opened once T has closed,\n632\t      fin(X) = {c : N[c] ⊆ X}                                   the customers free to close when X is open,\n633\t       cl(T ) = fin(O(T ))                                      the free closure of T ,\n634\t        b(T ) = |O(T ) \\ T | = |∂ T |                           the stacks open after T has closed,\n635\t      o(c, T ) = N[c] \\ O(T ),    open(c, T ) = |o(c, T )|      the stacks closing c would newly open.\n636\t\n637\tThe open stacks after T has closed are exactly the border of T , and this one identity carries the whole\n638\ttranslation to pathwidth.\n639\t    A move at T closes one customer c ∈ / T , at cost cost(T, c) = |O(T ∪ {c}) \\ T | = b(T ∪ {c}) + 1, the stacks\n640\topen at that moment counting c. It is playable when cost(T, c) ≤ k. Write Extk (T ) (“T extends”) when some\n641\torder of C \\ T has every step playable. On entering a node the search closes every free customer, so it stands\n642\tonly at states, sets with S = cl(S). The child of S by c is S · c = cl(S ∪ {c}). The search’s predicate Solk (S)\n643\tholds when S = C, or when some playable c has Solk (S · c) (Appendix A, A.15). Every node the search visits\n644\tsatisfies the invariant b(S) ≤ k.\n645\t\n646\tAn example. Take the graph of Figure 1.1(d), with k = 3. At T = {a} we have O(T ) = {a, b}, so one\n647\tstack, b, is open: b(T ) = 1 and ∂ T = {b}. No other customer is free, since N[b] contains c and d, so T is\n648\ta state. Closing b newly opens o(b, T ) = {c, d} and costs cost(T, b) = 3, the stacks b, c and d; the move is\n649\tplayable. At {a, b, c} every neighbour of d is open, so d is free, and the child {a, b} · c is {a, b, c, d}. The\n650\tclosing order a, b, c, e (with d and f closed free) costs 2, 3, 3, 2, the profile of order A in Figure 1.1(a).\n651\t\n652\tLemma 4.1 (free moves never hurt). If b(T ) ≤ k, then Extk (T ) ⇐⇒ Solk (cl(T )) (Appendix A, A.16).\n653\t\n654\t    Closing a customer whose neighbourhood is already open opens nothing, never makes a later step dearer,\n655\tand leaves one fewer stack open. The hypothesis is needed: closing the free customers one by one costs\n656\tb(T ) at each step.\n657\t\n658\tTheorem 4.2 (the search decides pathwidth). For nonempty C, Solk ( /0) ⇐⇒ pw(G) + 1 ≤ k. On the MOSP\n659\tgraph of an instance M that has a 1, Solk ( /0) ⇐⇒ Z(M) ≤ k (Appendix A, A.17).\n660\t\n661\tProof. For a closing order c1 , . . . , cn with prefixes Si , the i-th step costs 1 + |∂ Si |. Reverse the order to get\n662\ta layout. Its first n − i vertices are C \\ Si , and those with a neighbour among the remaining ones are exactly\n663\t∂ Si . So the cost of the closing order is the vertex separation of the reversed layout plus one, and Lemma 4.1\n664\tat the root and Theorem 2.2 finish the proof.\n665\t\n666\t   So a refutation, ¬Solk ( /0), may only mean pw(G) > k − 1. The rest of the section asks whether the\n667\tpruning rules preserve that meaning.\n668\t\n669\tIn pathwidth language. The exact pathwidth literature writes N(T ) for the border and d(T ) for its size\n670\t(Kobayashi et al., 2014, p. 390); we keep ∂ T and b(T ). A vertex sequence is w-feasible when every prefix\n671\thas border at most w, and vs(G) is the least w for which some permutation is w-feasible. Table 4.1 translates\n672\teach notion of the search into this vocabulary. Read it row by row: every ingredient of the customer search\n673\tis a known ingredient of an exact pathwidth algorithm, and the last two rows are the rules of Section 4.2.\n674\t\n675\t\n676\t\n677\t\n678\t                                                          13\n679\t\fTable 4.1: The customer search is, ingredient by ingredient, an exact pathwidth algorithm: each notion of the search\n680\tbeside its name in the pathwidth literature. The memo and the definite move are described in Section 4.2.\n681\t\n682\tCustomer search                          Layout\n683\tclosing order from /0                    vertex sequence, read forwards\n684\topen stacks b(T )                        border size |∂ T |\n685\tcost(T, c)                               |∂ (T ∪ {c})| + 1\n686\tSolk ( /0)                               vs(G) ≤ k − 1\n687\tfree closure cl(T )                      full set of T (Suchan and Villanger, 2009, p. 328)\n688\tmemo                                     failure table\n689\tdefinite move, repaired                  commitment to the child\n690\t\n691\tAlgorithm 4.1 The customer search (Chu and Stuckey, 2009) is a plain depth-first search; all its pruning\n692\tsits in the memo (lines 4 and 12), the filter (line 6) and the old moves (line 9). It decides Solk (S) for a graph\n693\tG = (C, E) and a stack bound k, and is called as S EARCH( /0, /0). Q is the set of old moves the node inherits.\n694\t 1    S EARCH(S, Q):\n695\t 2       S ← cl(S)                                                            free moves (Lemma 4.1)\n696\t 3       if S = C then return true\n697\t 4       if S is in the memo then return false                                memo\n698\t 5       A ← the c ∈  / S ∪ Q with cost(S, c) ≤ k, in index order             cost cut\n699\t 6       L ← filter of A: definite move, then subset rule, then better move   Sections 4.3–4.4\n700\t 7       seen ← Q \\ S\n701\t 8       for each c ∈ L:\n702\t 9          Q′ ← {q ∈ seen : cost(S ∪ {q}, c) ≤ k}                            old moves\n703\t10          if S EARCH(S ∪ {c}, Q′ ) then return true\n704\t11          seen ← seen ∪ {c}\n705\t12       add S to the memo\n706\t13       return false\n707\t\n708\t\n709\t4.2     The algorithm\n710\tAlgorithm 4.1 gives the customer search. It is a depth-first search over states. At each node it closes the\n711\tfree customers, consults a table of states already refuted, keeps the playable candidates, filters them with the\n712\tdominance rules and recurses on the survivors.\n713\t    Line 2 is safe by Lemma 4.1, and the cost cut of line 5 is exact, since an unplayable move is not a move\n714\tof Solk . The rest is pruning, and each piece has a one-sentence intuition.\n715\t\n716\t      • The memo (lines 4 and 12): whether a state can be finished does not depend on how it was reached, so\n717\t        a state refuted once is refused when met again.\n718\t\n719\t      • Old moves (lines 7–11): a sibling whose subtree failed stays failed further down, as long as closing it\n720\t        first would have kept each later step within the bound.\n721\t\n722\t      • The definite move: if closing q now leaves no more stacks open than before, it seems safe to close q at\n723\t        once and try nothing else.\n724\t\n725\t      • The subset rule: if d newly opens a subset of what r newly opens, then d is at least as good a move as\n726\t        r.\n727\t\n728\t      • The better move: if q would be a definite move right after r, then closing q before r is at least as good\n729\t        as closing r first.\n730\t\n731\t\n732\t                                                           14\n733\t\fEach of the three filtering rules discards a move r by citing a covering, a kept move c with Solk (S · r) ⇒\n734\tSolk (S · c). The filter runs the definite move, then the subset rule, then the better move, each once. If the\n735\tdefinite move fires, it keeps its customer alone.\n736\t    This loop is the decision procedure of Kobayashi et al. (2014, Algorithm 1, p. 393), which asks whether\n737\ta vertex set extends to a w-feasible permutation. It has four steps: replace the set by a committed extension,\n738\tanswer from a failure table or at the full vertex set, recurse on each feasible one-vertex extension, and record\n739\tfailures. The customer search is that loop with w = k − 1. The free closure and the definite move play the\n740\tpart of the commitment, the memo is the failure table, and the cost cut is the feasibility test. Coudert et al.\n741\t(2014, §3.2) has the same three parts in a branch and bound. Neither line cites the other in the papers we\n742\thold: Chu and Stuckey (2009) predates the commitments and both 2014 papers, and none of the pathwidth\n743\tpapers cites it. This is the isolation measured in Table 3.2, at the level of one algorithm.\n744\t\n745\t4.3   The definite move is false as published, and its repair is a matching\n746\tThe definite move is the strongest of the rules, since when it fires it discards every other candidate. Its\n747\tpremise looks like a safe accounting: closing q opens some new stacks and lets some customers close, and\n748\tif it lets at least as many close as it opens, nothing seems lost by closing it first. Chu and Stuckey (2009,\n749\t§3.1, p. 6) state:\n750\t\n751\t      Theorem 1. Suppose S ++ [q] is playable and close(q, S) ≥ open(q, S), then if U′ = S ++ R is\n752\t      a solution, there exists a solution U = S ++ [q] ++ R′ .\n753\t\n754\tHere ++ is concatenation of closing sequences, R and R′ are closing sequences, a solution is a complete\n755\tclosing order with every step playable, and close(q, S) counts the customers d with o(d, S) ⊆ o(q, S) (p. 4).\n756\tThe rule keeps q alone. We read d as ranging over customers not yet closed, as our implementation does;\n757\tread literally, closed customers count too, which weakens the premise, and both readings are refuted below.\n758\tChu (2011, Thm 6.3.6, p. 142) restates the theorem with the same premise and proof, reworded for k stacks.\n759\tWrite X = S · q. The first lemma confirms that the premise says what the intuition says.\n760\t\n761\tLemma 4.3 (what the premise says). close(q, S) = |X \\ S|. Hence the premise holds iff b(X) ≤ b(S): the\n762\tchild has no more open stacks than the parent (Appendix A, A.24).\n763\t\n764\t   The premise compares only the two ends, S and X. The rule fails because a set between them can be\n765\tcheaper still. Figure 4.1(a) shows the graph on which it fails, and panel (b) the cheaper set between.\n766\t\n767\tCounterexample 4.4. Let G be the graph of Figure 4.1, on customers 0, . . . , 13 with the 26 edges\n768\t\n769\t                  0–3, 0–4, 0–7, 0–11, 1–2, 1–5, 1–6, 2–3, 2–4, 5–9, 5–11, 5–12, 6–8,\n770\t          6–13, 7–9, 7–10, 7–13, 8–9, 8–10, 8–11, 9–10, 9–11, 10–11, 10–12, 10–13, 12–13,\n771\t\n772\twith S = {2}, k = 6 and q = 0. Then O(S) = {1, 2, 3, 4}, o(0, S) = {0, 7, 11} and cost(S, 0) = 6, so 0 is\n773\tplayable. The customers with o(d, S) ⊆ {0, 7, 11} are 0, 3 and 4, so close(0, S) = 3 ≥ open(0, S), and 0\n774\tcomes first in index order, so the rule keeps only 0. A solution exists from S: the order\n775\t\n776\t                                      1, 3, 4, 6, 12, 13, 0, 5, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11\n777\t\n778\tcosts at most 6. None exists from the child X = {0, 2, 3, 4}: only seven sets are reachable from X by moves\n779\tof cost at most 6, and none is C (Appendix A, A.25).\n780\t\n781\t   The published implication, as a universal statement, is refuted in Lean under both readings of close,\n782\tand so is the thesis’s Theorem 6.3.6 (Appendix A, A.26). Fink (2012, pp. 27–28) restates the rule with a\n783\t\n784\t                                                        15\n785\t\f      (a) S = {2}: the definite move keeps 0                        (b) B = {2, 3, 4}: closing 3 and 4 first\n786\t\n787\t                                          13                                                                13\n788\t             6                                                              6\n789\t\n790\t\n791\t\n792\t       1           3                  7                               1             3                   7\n793\t                                                   10                                                                10\n794\t             2              0                  8                            2                 0                  8\n795\t\n796\t                   4                 11                                             4                11\n797\t\n798\t\n799\t                                           9                                                                  9\n800\t                       5                                                                5\n801\t\n802\t                                 12                                                                12\n803\t\n804\t\n805\t         closing 0 opens 0, 7, 11 and closes 0, 3, 4:                b(B) = 2 (open 0, 1) < 3 = b(X) (open 1, 7, 11),\n806\t        open = close = 3; 3 and 4 need only stack 0                  X = {0, 2, 3, 4}: the repaired rule does not fire\n807\t\n808\t                        closed            customer 0, the move kept                         other customers\n809\t                        open stack        (a) new stacks of 0; (b) open at X only\n810\t\n811\t\n812\tFigure 4.1: The definite move fails because a set between parent and child is cheaper than both. (a) The graph of\n813\tCounterexample 4.4 at the state where the rule fires. Customer 2 is closed; 1, 3 and 4 are open. Moving 0 opens the\n814\tnew stacks 0, 7 and 11 and closes 0, 3 and 4, so open = close = 3 and the rule keeps 0 alone. But 3 and 4 have the\n815\tsingle new stack 0 between them: closing them first gains two stacks for the price of one, which a solution starting\n816\twith 0 cannot match. (b) The intermediate set B = {2, 3, 4} leaves two stacks open, fewer than the three of the child\n817\tX = {0, 2, 3, 4}, so the repaired rule of this section does not fire.\n818\t\n819\t\n820\tstronger premise, counting only dominated customers of smaller index than q. The graph above refutes it\n821\tunder no labelling, but a third twin of 3 and 4 gives a 15-customer graph that does (Appendix A, A.27). The\n822\tpublished proof moves q to the front of a solution and counts, after each prefix, the stacks q opens against\n823\tthe customers it lets close. But customers that the solution has already closed no longer count as closed by\n824\tq, while the stacks q opens may still be new. Appendix B.4 gives the full analysis, and Appendix B.5 the\n825\tsearch for smaller counterexamples.\n826\t\n827\tThe repair. The fix is to require the comparison at every set between the two ends, not only at S. Call\n828\tq hereditarily definite at S when b(X) ≤ b(B) for every B with S ⊆ B ⊆ X and q ∈  / B. By Lemma 4.3, the\n829\tpublished premise is the case B = S. In the counterexample the repair fails at B = {2, 3, 4} (Figure 4.1(b)),\n830\twhere b(B) = 2 < 3 = b(X) (Appendix A, A.28). The proof that the repair is sound needs one inequality.\n831\t\n832\tLemma 4.5 (open stacks are submodular). For all A, B ⊆ C, b(A ∪ B) + b(A ∩ B) ≤ b(A) + b(B) (Appendix A,\n833\tA.29).\n834\t\n835\tProof. b(T ) = |O(T )| − |T |, O(A ∪ B) = O(A) ∪ O(B) and O(A ∩ B) ⊆ O(A) ∩ O(B); the customers them-\n836\tselves balance exactly.\n837\t\n838\tTheorem 4.6 (the repaired definite move is sound). If q ∈      / S is hereditarily definite at S, then Extk (S) ⇒\n839\tExtk (X). At a state with the invariant, Solk (S) ⇒ Solk (S · q) (Appendix A, A.30).\n840\t\n841\tProof. Take an order from S of cost at most k with prefixes Ti , in which q is the j-th step, and follow it from\n842\tX, skipping customers already in X. A step i < j closes ci at Ti−1 ∪ X. We have S ⊆ Ti ∩ X ⊆ X and q ∈       / Ti ,\n843\tso the hypothesis gives b(X) ≤ b(Ti ∩ X), and Lemma 4.5 gives b(Ti ∪ X) ≤ b(Ti ): the step costs no more\n844\t\n845\t                                                            16\n846\t\fthan the original. After step j − 1 the closed set contains T j with the same opened set, and the rest of the\n847\torder applies. The search’s form follows by Lemma 4.1.\n848\t\n849\t     This theorem is not new. The hereditary premise is the commitment condition of Tamaki, and the proof\n850\tis the proof of the commitment lemma, Kitsunai et al. (2016, Lemma 1, p. 142); Section 4.6 makes the\n851\tcorrespondence exact.\n852\t\n853\tThe matching test. The repaired rule seems to require checking exponentially many intermediate sets. It\n854\tdoes not, and this cheap test is the algorithmic contribution of the section; we did not find it in the papers\n855\twe hold. In words: the rule is safe exactly when all but one of the new stacks can be paired with a different\n856\tfreed customer who needed it. In the counterexample, closing 0 opens three new stacks and frees 3 and 4,\n857\tboth of whom needed only stack 0: one pairing where two are required.\n858\t\n859\tTheorem 4.7 (the repair is a matching condition). Let Y = X \\ (S ∪ {q}), and join each d ∈ Y to the stacks\n860\tof o(d, S) ⊆ o(q, S). Then q is hereditarily definite at S iff this bipartite graph has a matching with at least\n861\topen(q, S) − 1 edges (Appendix A, A.31).\n862\t\n863\tProof. The sets of the repair are B = S ∪ D, D ⊆ Y , and b(X) ≤ b(S ∪ D) iff |D| − |o(D)| ≤ δ with δ =\n864\t|Y | + 1 − open(q, S), where o(D) is the union of the o(d, S). By the deficiency form of Hall’s theorem the\n865\tlargest matching has size |Y | − maxD (|D| − |o(D)|), which is at least |Y | − δ = open(q, S) − 1 exactly when\n866\tthe repair holds.\n867\t\n868\t    The bipartite graph has at most close − 1 customers on one side and open stacks on the other, and the\n869\tmatching can stop at open − 1 edges. Every q with open(q, S) ≤ 1 passes at once, so the published rule is\n870\tright there (Appendix A, A.32).\n871\t\n872\t4.4   The other rules\n873\tEach of the remaining rules is quoted, proved sound in the form used, and shown to need the test or tie-break\n874\tit carries.\n875\t\n876\tThe subset rule. “If o(ci , S) ⊆ o(c j , S) and i < j . . . move j can be removed” (Chu and Stuckey, 2009,\n877\t§2, p. 5). As implemented, d dominates r when o(d, S) ⊆ o(r, S) and the inclusion is strict or d < r. Every\n878\tdominated playable r is dropped, with a fallback that keeps A if all would go. The covering is sound for\n879\tevery set S: if o(d, S) ⊆ o(r, S) and r is playable, then d is playable and Solk (S · r) ⇒ Solk (S · d), because\n880\td is free after r and closing d then r reaches the same state at no greater cost. With the index tie-break,\n881\tdomination is a strict order, so an undominated move with a solution survives, and the fallback never fires\n882\twhen a solution exists (Appendix A, A.33). The tie-break is necessary: without it, two customers with equal\n883\tnew stacks dominate each other and both go, which loses a solution on a 7-customer graph (Appendix A,\n884\tA.34).\n885\t\n886\tThe better move. Chu and Stuckey (2009, §3.2, p. 6): “Suppose S ++ [q] and S ++ [r, q] are playable and\n887\tclose(q, S ∪ {r}) ≥ open(q, S ∪ {r}) then if U′ = S ++ [r] ++ R is a solution there exists a solution U = S ++\n888\t[q] ++ R′ .” Its premise is the definite move’s premise for q at the child cl(S ∪ {r}), together with playability\n889\tof r then q, and its conclusion swaps q and r (Appendix A, A.35). The swap is sound, because both orders\n890\tend at the same state and neither step overflows. The appeal to the definite move is not.\n891\t    Counterexample. On the graph of Counterexample 4.4 at the root with k = 6, the definite move does\n892\tnot fire, customers 0 and 2 both survive the subset rule, and the premise holds for r = 2, q = 0, so the rule\n893\tdrops 2 citing 0. But {2} has a solution and {0} has none (Appendix A, A.36). At that node another kept\n894\t\n895\t                                                       17\n896\t\fcustomer still has a solution, so this is a false link and not a lost node; no node where the better move loses\n897\tthe last solution has been found. Chu (2011, Thm 6.3.8, p. 143) states the better move with a different\n898\tpremise, close(q, S) ≥ open(q, S ∪ {r}), and proves it through his Theorem 6.3.6. The same graph refutes it\n899\tat S = {2}, r = 3, q = 0 (Appendix A, A.37).\n900\t    Repair and test. Cite q for r only if q is hereditarily definite at cl(S ∪ {r}), which is one matching at the\n901\tchild (Theorem 4.7); then the swap argument goes through (Appendix A, A.38).\n902\t    Two requirements. The close count in the premise must not include the customers that r finishes on its\n903\town; counting them makes the rule’s own conclusion false on a 12-customer graph. And the rules must run\n904\tin the order definite, subset, better, each citing only customers still standing. If the better move runs first,\n905\tthe subset rule can cite a customer the better move has already dropped. An 8-customer graph then loses\n906\ta node through a cycle of links that are each true (Appendix A, A.39). The second is an instance of the\n907\tgeneral observation of Beck et al. (2025, Prop. 3, Example 6) that mutually dominating transitions must be\n908\ttie-broken.\n909\t\n910\tThe old move. Chu and Stuckey (2009, Thm 3, p. 7) let a child inherit a sibling q whose subtree failed,\n911\tprovided that inserting q before each later step keeps that step playable (line 9 of Algorithm 4.1). The\n912\trule is sound as implemented: if cost(S ∪ {q}, c) ≤ k, then Solk (cl({q} ∪ S · c)) ⇒ Solk (S · q), so a refuted\n913\tsibling stays refuted along any path whose steps pass the test (Appendix A, A.40). The test is necessary; the\n914\tsmallest graph that shows it is the path on five vertices (Appendix A, A.41).\n915\t\n916\tThe memo. Whether a state with b(S) ≤ k has a solution depends on the state alone, not on the path that\n917\treached it (Appendix A, A.42). One concern is that the memo and the old move might not combine: a\n918\trefutation obtained with inherited old moves seems to depend on the ancestors, and one implementation of\n919\tthe search declines to run both for that reason. A node taken on its own, with an old move that is not in fact\n920\trefuted, can indeed record a solvable state (Appendix A, A.43). Theorem B.2 (Appendix B.7) shows that\n921\tthis never happens in a real run, because every old move is a refutation completed earlier in the same run.\n922\t\n923\t4.5   The repaired search is sound\n924\tWhat a rule must satisfy. At a state S with old moves Q, the cost cut keeps the playable candidates A and\n925\tthe filter keeps a sublist L ⊆ A. The filter is node-sound at (S, Q) if, whenever every q ∈ Q has ¬Solk (S · q),\n926\tSolk (S) implies Solk (S · c) for some c ∈ L (Appendix A, A.44). In terms of coverings, a node is sound\n927\twhen two things hold. First, every link a rule cites is a true covering. Second, every chain of links from\n928\ta discarded candidate ends in L or in Q. The second condition can fail through a cycle even when every\n929\tlink is true, which is why the order of the rules matters. Proposition B.1 shows that the repaired filter is\n930\tnode-sound, and Theorem B.2 that node soundness, the memo and the old move together give sound runs;\n931\tboth are in Appendix B.7. Together they give the main theorem.\n932\t\n933\tTheorem 4.8 (the repaired search is sound). Run the customer search with free moves, the memo, old moves\n934\tand the filter definite → subset → better. Let the definite move fire only on a hereditarily definite customer,\n935\tand the better move cite only under the repaired premise. If the search refutes k from the root, then no\n936\tclosing order costs at most k, pw(G) > k − 1, and on the MOSP graph of an instance M that has a 1,\n937\tZ(M) > k (Appendix A, A.45).\n938\t\n939\t    In words: each rule throws away only moves that a kept move is proved to be at least as good as, and the\n940\tmemo and the old moves remember only failures that really happened earlier in the same search. So when\n941\tthe search says “no”, no closing order fits in k stacks.\n942\t\n943\t\n944\t\n945\t                                                       18\n946\t\fWhat is proved and what is tested. The theorem is about the search as specified; that our C and Python\n947\timplementations run it is checked, not proved. Three checks link them:\n948\t      • a transcription of the Lean filter agreed with the implemented filter on 16,244,090 node checks over\n949\t        graphs on 1 to 17 vertices;\n950\t      • the matching agreed with the hereditary premise on 36,939,226 candidates;\n951\t      • a search written from the theorems agreed with both implementations and an exact oracle on 17,431,232\n952\t        runs over 52,592 graphs on 1 to 17 vertices.\n953\tSection 5.3 adds a certificate checker for individual refutations.\n954\t\n955\tThe published rules. Under the published rules the filter is not node-sound (Appendix A, A.46). In a\n956\tcheck of 570,206 runs with memo and old move, it lost the last solution at a node in 58 runs, all on graphs\n957\tbuilt around Counterexample 4.4 and all at k equal to the optimum. Every one found a solution through\n958\tanother branch and answered correctly. No whole instance on which the published rules wrongly answer\n959\t“no” is known.\n960\t\n961\t4.6     The rules in pathwidth language\n962\tThe definite move has an exact counterpart in the pathwidth literature, and this subsection makes the corre-\n963\tspondence precise. Kitsunai et al. (2016, p. 142), stating Tamaki’s notion, call an extension τ of a vertex\n964\tsequence σ committable when |∂ X ′ | ≥ |∂V (τ)| for every X ′ with V (σ ) ⊆ X ′ ⊆ V (τ). Their Lemma 1 says\n965\tthat if σ extends to a feasible permutation, so does τ.\n966\tLemma 4.9. For q ∈   / S and X = cl(S ∪ {q}): the published premise holds iff b(X) ≤ b(S), the commitment\n967\tcondition at the single set X ′ = S; and the repaired premise holds iff X is a commitment from S, the condition\n968\tat every X ′ between them (Appendix A, A.47).\n969\t    So Theorem 4.6 is the commitment lemma at the child, and the published definite move is a commitment\n970\twhose interior condition was dropped. In Counterexample 4.4 the endpoint test passes with b(X) = 3 = b(S),\n971\twhile the interior set {2, 3, 4} has border 2 (Appendix A, A.48). The published premise is not wasted: by\n972\tKitsunai et al. (2016, Lemma 10, Cor. 2, pp. 148–149) it guarantees a commitment to the least-border\n973\tset between S and X, which need not contain q (Appendix A, A.49). In the counterexample that set is\n974\t{2, 3, 4} (Appendix A, A.50). Committing there when the matching fails is a second repair that gives up no\n975\tpruning; we have not built it.\n976\t    A commitment of depth one, S to S ∪ {q}, holds iff open(q, S) ≤ 1 (Appendix A, A.51). The greedy step\n977\tof Coudert et al. (2014, Lemma 3, p. 49) is exactly that condition (Appendix A, A.52), and the push rule\n978\tof Suchan and Villanger (2009, Rule 1, p. 330) is a case of it. So every published pathwidth rule of this\n979\tkind lies in the region open ≤ 1, where the published definite move is right; Kobayashi et al. (2014, p. 388,\n980\tTable 2) report depth-one commitments as “extremely effective”. Chu and Stuckey’s definite move reaches\n981\tinto open ≥ 2, and that is where it fails. For the cost of the test, Kitsunai et al. (2016, Cor. 2) find the least\n982\tborder between the ends of a given extension by a minimum s–t separator; Theorem 4.7 is that computation\n983\tfor the single target X, where it becomes a bipartite matching.\n984\t    Table 4.2 sums up, and its rows are grouped by what they say. The published definite and better moves\n985\tare false. The free move, the memo and the repaired definite move are known pathwidth results. The subset\n986\trule, the repaired better move and the old move have no counterpart we found, and are proved here, as is\n987\tthe matching test. The comparison is limited to the pathwidth papers we hold (Suchan and Villanger, 2009;\n988\tBodlaender et al., 2012; Coudert et al., 2014, 2016; Kobayashi et al., 2014; Kitsunai et al., 2016); “none\n989\tfound” is not a claim of priority.\n990\t\n991\t                                                       19\n992\t\fTable 4.2: Two published rules are false; the free move, the memo and the repaired definite move are known pathwidth\n993\tresults; the subset rule, the repaired better move and the old move, like the matching test, are proved here. Each rule\n994\tof the customer search against the exact-pathwidth literature.\n995\t\n996\tRule                      Published counterpart                                          Status\n997\tdefinite move,            –                                                              false (Cex. 4.4)\n998\tpublished\n999\tbetter move, published    none found                                                     false\n1000\tfree move                 full sets (Suchan and Villanger, 2009); Kitsunai et al.        known\n1001\t                          (2016, Prop. 3)\n1002\tdefinite move,            depth-one commitments; greedy step; push rule                  known\n1003\topen ≤ 1\n1004\tdefinite move,            commitment lemma (Kitsunai et al., 2016, Lemma 1)              known; matching test\n1005\trepaired                                                                                 (Thm 4.7) not found\n1006\tmemo                      subset DP (Bodlaender et al., 2012); prefix and failure        known; with the old move,\n1007\t                          tables                                                         proved here (Thm B.2)\n1008\tsubset rule               none found                                                     proved here\n1009\tbetter move, repaired     none found                                                     proved here\n1010\told move                  none found                                                     proved here\n1011\t\n1012\t\n1013\tPrior reports. We found no earlier report of the error in the 56 works we read in full of the 141 that cite\n1014\tthe customer search’s papers (Appendix B.6). The pathwidth literature has the correct form of the rule, as a\n1015\tcommitment, and none of the pathwidth papers we hold mentions MOSP.\n1016\t\n1017\t\n1018\t5      Computational results and a dataset\n1019\tSection 4 proved the repaired search sound. This section asks four practical questions about it and answers\n1020\teach with a measurement. What does the repair cost? Under the repaired rules, the total number of search\n1021\tnodes over each benchmark set changes by at most 2%. Do the published benchmark optima survive? All\n1022\t115 that rested on the published rules were proved again, and none changed. Can a reader check a refutation\n1023\twithout trusting our code? Yes, up to about 75 customers, through a certificate and an independent checker.\n1024\tFinally, what does it make possible? A dataset of 17,714 graphs from twenty benchmark collections. For\n1025\t16,087 of them the pathwidth is certified, and that gives the exact value of every problem in the exact core.\n1026\t\n1027\t5.1    Experimental setup\n1028\tMachine and code. Every run in this section used one machine, an AMD Ryzen 9 9950X (16 cores,\n1029\t32 hardware threads) with 128 GB of memory under Ubuntu Linux. Both searches, the open-stacks cus-\n1030\ttomer search and its port to graphs, are written in C (gcc 15.2, -O3 -march=native) and driven from\n1031\tPython 3.12. A Python implementation of the same rules serves as a reference for cross-checks and emits\n1032\tthe certificates of Section 5.3. The repaired tests are the default in every implementation; the published tests\n1033\tremain available as an option.\n1034\t\n1035\tRuns. The basic job asks one question: is the optimum of this instance at most k? A worker is one single-\n1036\tthreaded process that answers such questions, one at a time. A node is one state visited by the search, the\n1037\tunit in which we report effort. Up to 32 workers ran at once on the machine, so wall-clock times carry load\n1038\tnoise. We therefore compare node counts and report time only in core-hours. A pair runs the same question\n1039\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d20ab00f-ced5-4701-8f82-053c98f1df8f","uuid":"ebb1fa2e-cdef-494f-9e80-c0ed42289e13","timestamp":"2026-10-06T20:14:46.096Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/main.txt","content":"\nFigure 3.1: Pathwidth has more relevant works than the other eleven names together, three times over, and three names\nhave none. Works using each problem’s name, in title or abstract, in the sense of the Linhares–Yanasse table; all years\nto September 2026. Colour gives the discipline that the table assigns the problem.\n\n\n\n\n                                                              10\n\fTable 3.1: Besides pathwidth, the most active name over 2005–2024 is MOSP, although vertex separation led in 2015–\n2019. Relevant works per five-year period, for the three names with the most works over 2005–2024; the next are edge\nsearch (28) and node search (20).\n\n                    Name                 2005–09     2010–14       2015–19      2020–24     2005–24\n                    Pathwidth                 114         179           267          311         871\n                    MOSP                       17          17             5           12          51\n                    Vertex separation           7          10            15            0          32\n\nTable 3.2: The open-stacks papers are read apart from graph theory. Works citing the papers that the Linhares–Yanasse\ntable cites, by the discipline of the papers they cite. Of 135 works citing an operations-research paper, 6 also cite a\ngraph-theory paper (4%); of 285 citing a VLSI paper, 65 do (23%). The 2002 paper itself counts as operations research.\nOpenAlex, 29 September 2026; 844 citing works in all.\n\n                                                                . . . of which also cite\n                      Works citing           All    operations research       VLSI     graph theory\n                      operations research   135                         –       14               6\n                      VLSI                  285                        14        –              65\n                      graph theory          507                         6       65               –\n\n\n3.1   Pathwidth has absorbed the family\nFigure 3.1 gives the counts. Pathwidth has 1,213 relevant works, 21 times as many as MOSP and more than\nthree times as many as the other eleven names together (380). Three names have no relevant work at all:\nnarrowness, split bandwidth and edge separation. Two more are rare: interval thickness has 6 works and\none-dimensional logic 11. Pathwidth is also the only name still growing: its 311 works in 2020–2024 are\nmore than the whole history of any other name (Appendix C). So anything one would want to know about\nalgorithms for this class of problems was most likely published under “pathwidth”.\n\n3.2   Besides pathwidth, open stacks\nThe VLSI names belong to the 1980s: since 2005 gate matrix layout has 9 relevant works and PLA folding\n6. Table 3.1 shows the three names with the most works over 2005–2024. MOSP is second, with 51, ahead\nof vertex separation (32) and edge search (28). Vertex separation led only in 2015–2019. The open-stacks\ncommunity, in operations research, is thus the one most active besides pathwidth.\n    It has also worked largely apart from graph theory. Table 3.2 counts the 844 works that cite the papers\nof the table, by the disciplines of the papers they cite. Only six works cite both an open-stacks paper and\na graph-theory paper. Two are by the table’s authors, one is open-stacks work on graph properties, and\nthree are pathwidth or vertex-separation work from computer science. The VLSI papers are read with graph\ntheory about five times as often.\n\n3.3   From an open-stacks challenge to a pathwidth solver\nIt is not a coincidence that the best exact algorithm for MOSP came from the open-stacks community and\nis also, on the published evidence, among the strongest exact algorithms for pathwidth. The account is in\nChu and Stuckey (2009, §1). MOSP was the subject of the first Constraint Modelling Challenge (Smith\nand Gent, 2005), posed in May 2005, which drew 13 entries. The winning entry, by García de la Banda\nand Stuckey, combined dynamic programming with branch and bound and was an order of magnitude faster\n\n\n                                                          11\n\fthan any other (Garcia de la Banda and Stuckey, 2007). Chu and Stuckey (2009) built on it. They branched\non which customer’s stack closes next rather than on which pattern is made next, recorded the states already\nrefuted, added dominance rules, and became five to six orders of magnitude faster on hard instances. Their\nsolver closed every open instance of the challenge within ten seconds. MOSP’s busiest decade in Table 3.1,\n2005–2014, is the decade of this work.\n    The customer search reads an instance only through its MOSP graph (Chu and Stuckey, 2009, p. 4), so it\ndecides pathwidth (Section 4). Ported to graphs, it proves more instances than reported on the benchmarks\nwhere published exact pathwidth results exist, those of Coudert et al. (2016). It proves the pathwidth of\n11,194 of the 11,534 Rome graphs (97.1%), against their 95.6%, both within 600 seconds per graph. It\nproves all 50 VSPLIB trees, against their 30, and 39 of the 73 Harwell–Boeing graphs, against their 26.\nThese are comparisons with published numbers obtained on other hardware, not a head-to-head run.\n    Two consequences shape the rest of the paper. First, a result on any of the twelve problems should be\nstated, and searched for, as a result on pathwidth. Section 4 does this for the customer search. It finds that\nthe search’s first dominance rule is, in correct form, a theorem already known in the pathwidth literature,\nand that two of its published theorems are false. Second, a benchmark for any problem in the exact core is\na pathwidth benchmark, which is how Section 5.4 builds its dataset.\n\n\n4     An exact open-stacks search, read as a pathwidth algorithm\nIf MOSP is pathwidth (Theorem 2.3), then the best exact MOSP algorithm is a pathwidth algorithm, and its\nrules can be checked against what the pathwidth literature knows. This section does that for the customer\nsearch of Chu and Stuckey (2009), the search that Section 3 traced from the open-stacks challenge. It\nsearches over the orders in which the customers’ stacks close, not over pattern sequences, and it prunes with\ndominance rules. Three of the rules are stated as theorems (their Theorems 1, 2 and 3) and a fourth, the\nsubset rule, in their §2. Chu and Stuckey (2009) place MOSP among “graph path-width and gate matrix\nlayout”, citing Linhares and Yanasse (2002), but they do not use pathwidth in the search. Any graph is the\nMOSP graph of the instance with one customer per vertex and one pattern per edge, so the search decides\npathwidth for every graph.\n    The stakes are these. Two of the published dominance theorems, the definite move and the better move,\nare false, and so are their restatements in Chu’s thesis (Chu, 2011) and, with a stronger premise, in Fink\n(2012). Yet no wrong answer is known. No whole instance has been found on which the published rules\nwrongly answer “no”. And when every benchmark optimum that rested on the search was proved again with\nrepaired rules, no value changed (Section 5). The correct form of the definite move was already known for\npathwidth, as Tamaki’s commitment lemma, which says when an exact pathwidth search may commit to one\nextension of a vertex set and try no other (Section 4.6). We show that it can be tested by one small bipartite\nmatching per candidate. The main result, Theorem 4.8, says informally: with both rules repaired, the search\nis sound; whenever it answers that k stacks do not suffice, they do not, and the graph has pathwidth at least\nk. The repair costs at most 2% more search nodes on each benchmark set (Section 5).\n    Section 4.1 defines the search and shows that it decides pathwidth, in both vocabularies. Section 4.2\ngives the algorithm. Sections 4.3 and 4.4 take the rules one at a time, Section 4.5 proves the repaired search\nsound, and Section 4.6 places each rule in the pathwidth literature.\n\n4.1   The search decides pathwidth\nChu and Stuckey (2009, p. 4) observe that the patterns matter only through the MOSP graph. So fix a graph\nG = (C, E) whose vertices are the customers, and an integer k ≥ 0. In this section k bounds the number of\nopen stacks, which is one more than a width: k stacks suffice exactly when the pathwidth is at most k − 1\n\n\n                                                     12\n\f(Theorem 4.2). For a customer c write N[c] for its closed neighbourhood, the stacks that must be open when\nc’s stack closes. For a set T ⊆ C of vertices, its border ∂ T is the set of vertices outside T with a neighbour\nin T . For a set T of closed customers write\n                 S\n       O(T ) =     c∈T N[c]                                     the stacks opened once T has closed,\n      fin(X) = {c : N[c] ⊆ X}                                   the customers free to close when X is open,\n       cl(T ) = fin(O(T ))                                      the free closure of T ,\n        b(T ) = |O(T ) \\ T | = |∂ T |                           the stacks open after T has closed,\n      o(c, T ) = N[c] \\ O(T ),    open(c, T ) = |o(c, T )|      the stacks closing c would newly open.\n\nThe open stacks after T has closed are exactly the border of T , and this one identity carries the whole\ntranslation to pathwidth.\n    A move at T closes one customer c ∈ / T , at cost cost(T, c) = |O(T ∪ {c}) \\ T | = b(T ∪ {c}) + 1, the stacks\nopen at that moment counting c. It is playable when cost(T, c) ≤ k. Write Extk (T ) (“T extends”) when some\norder of C \\ T has every step playable. On entering a node the search closes every free customer, so it stands\nonly at states, sets with S = cl(S). The child of S by c is S · c = cl(S ∪ {c}). The search’s predicate Solk (S)\nholds when S = C, or when some playable c has Solk (S · c) (Appendix A, A.15). Every node the search visits\nsatisfies the invariant b(S) ≤ k.\n\nAn example. Take the graph of Figure 1.1(d), with k = 3. At T = {a} we have O(T ) = {a, b}, so one\nstack, b, is open: b(T ) = 1 and ∂ T = {b}. No other customer is free, since N[b] contains c and d, so T is\na state. Closing b newly opens o(b, T ) = {c, d} and costs cost(T, b) = 3, the stacks b, c and d; the move is\nplayable. At {a, b, c} every neighbour of d is open, so d is free, and the child {a, b} · c is {a, b, c, d}. The\nclosing order a, b, c, e (with d and f closed free) costs 2, 3, 3, 2, the profile of order A in Figure 1.1(a).\n\nLemma 4.1 (free moves never hurt). If b(T ) ≤ k, then Extk (T ) ⇐⇒ Solk (cl(T )) (Appendix A, A.16).\n\n    Closing a customer whose neighbourhood is already open opens nothing, never makes a later step dearer,\nand leaves one fewer stack open. The hypothesis is needed: closing the free customers one by one costs\nb(T ) at each step.\n\nTheorem 4.2 (the search decides pathwidth). For nonempty C, Solk ( /0) ⇐⇒ pw(G) + 1 ≤ k. On the MOSP\ngraph of an instance M that has a 1, Solk ( /0) ⇐⇒ Z(M) ≤ k (Appendix A, A.17).\n\nProof. For a closing order c1 , . . . , cn with prefixes Si , the i-th step costs 1 + |∂ Si |. Reverse the order to get\na layout. Its first n − i vertices are C \\ Si , and those with a neighbour among the remaining ones are exactly\n∂ Si . So the cost of the closing order is the vertex separation of the reversed layout plus one, and Lemma 4.1\nat the root and Theorem 2.2 finish the proof.\n\n   So a refutation, ¬Solk ( /0), may only mean pw(G) > k − 1. The rest of the section asks whether the\npruning rules preserve that meaning.\n\nIn pathwidth language. The exact pathwidth literature writes N(T ) for the border and d(T ) for its size\n(Kobayashi et al., 2014, p. 390); we keep ∂ T and b(T ). A vertex sequence is w-feasible when every prefix\nhas border at most w, and vs(G) is the least w for which some permutation is w-feasible. Table 4.1 translates\neach notion of the search into this vocabulary. Read it row by row: every ingredient of the customer search\nis a known ingredient of an exact pathwidth algorithm, and the last two rows are the rules of Section 4.2.\n\n\n\n\n                                                          13\n\fTable 4.1: The customer search is, ingredient by ingredient, an exact pathwidth algorithm: each notion of the search\nbeside its name in the pathwidth literature. The memo and the definite move are described in Section 4.2.\n\nCustomer search                          Layout\nclosing order from /0                    vertex sequence, read forwards\nopen stacks b(T )                        border size |∂ T |\ncost(T, c)                               |∂ (T ∪ {c})| + 1\nSolk ( /0)                               vs(G) ≤ k − 1\nfree closure cl(T )                      full set of T (Suchan and Villanger, 2009, p. 328)\nmemo                                     failure table\ndefinite move, repaired                  commitment to the child\n\nAlgorithm 4.1 The customer search (Chu and Stuckey, 2009) is a plain depth-first search; all its pruning\nsits in the memo (lines 4 and 12), the filter (line 6) and the old moves (line 9). It decides Solk (S) for a graph\nG = (C, E) and a stack bound k, and is called as S EARCH( /0, /0). Q is the set of old moves the node inherits.\n 1    S EARCH(S, Q):\n 2       S ← cl(S)                                                            free moves (Lemma 4.1)\n 3       if S = C then return true\n 4       if S is in the memo then return false                                memo\n 5       A ← the c ∈  / S ∪ Q with cost(S, c) ≤ k, in index order             cost cut\n 6       L ← filter of A: definite move, then subset rule, then better move   Sections 4.3–4.4\n 7       seen ← Q \\ S\n 8       for each c ∈ L:\n 9          Q′ ← {q ∈ seen : cost(S ∪ {q}, c) ≤ k}                            old moves\n10          if S EARCH(S ∪ {c}, Q′ ) then return true\n11          seen ← seen ∪ {c}\n12       add S to the memo\n13       return false\n\n\n4.2     The algorithm\nAlgorithm 4.1 gives the customer search. It is a depth-first search over states. At each node it closes the\nfree customers, consults a table of states already refuted, keeps the playable candidates, filters them with the\ndominance rules and recurses on the survivors.\n    Line 2 is safe by Lemma 4.1, and the cost cut of line 5 is exact, since an unplayable move is not a move\nof Solk . The rest is pruning, and each piece has a one-sentence intuition.\n\n      • The memo (lines 4 and 12): whether a state can be finished does not depend on how it was reached, so\n        a state refuted once is refused when met again.\n\n      • Old moves (lines 7–11): a sibling whose subtree failed stays failed further down, as long as closing it\n        first would have kept each later step within the bound.\n\n      • The definite move: if closing q now leaves no more stacks open than before, it seems safe to close q at\n        once and try nothing else.\n\n      • The subset rule: if d newly opens a subset of what r newly opens, then d is at least as good a move as\n        r.\n\n      • The better move: if q would be a definite move right after r, then closing q before r is at least as good\n        as closing r first.\n\n\n                                                           14\n\fEach of the three filtering rules discards a move r by citing a covering, a kept move c with Solk (S · r) ⇒\nSolk (S · c). The filter runs the definite move, then the subset rule, then the better move, each once. If the\ndefinite move fires, it keeps its customer alone.\n    This loop is the decision procedure of Kobayashi et al. (2014, Algorithm 1, p. 393), which asks whether\na vertex set extends to a w-feasible permutation. It has four steps: replace the set by a committed extension,\nanswer from a failure table or at the full vertex set, recurse on each feasible one-vertex extension, and record\nfailures. The customer search is that loop with w = k − 1. The free closure and the definite move play the\npart of the commitment, the memo is the failure table, and the cost cut is the feasibility test. Coudert et al.\n(2014, §3.2) has the same three parts in a branch and bound. Neither line cites the other in the papers we\nhold: Chu and Stuckey (2009) predates the commitments and both 2014 papers, and none of the pathwidth\npapers cites it. This is the isolation measured in Table 3.2, at the level of one algorithm.\n\n4.3   The definite move is false as published, and its repair is a matching\nThe definite move is the strongest of the rules, since when it fires it discards every other candidate. Its\npremise looks like a safe accounting: closing q opens some new stacks and lets some customers close, and\nif it lets at least as many close as it opens, nothing seems lost by closing it first. Chu and Stuckey (2009,\n§3.1, p. 6) state:\n\n      Theorem 1. Suppose S ++ [q] is playable and close(q, S) ≥ open(q, S), then if U′ = S ++ R is\n      a solution, there exists a solution U = S ++ [q] ++ R′ .\n\nHere ++ is concatenation of closing sequences, R and R′ are closing sequences, a solution is a complete\nclosing order with every step playable, and close(q, S) counts the customers d with o(d, S) ⊆ o(q, S) (p. 4).\nThe rule keeps q alone. We read d as ranging over customers not yet closed, as our implementation does;\nread literally, closed customers count too, which weakens the premise, and both readings are refuted below.\nChu (2011, Thm 6.3.6, p. 142) restates the theorem with the same premise and proof, reworded for k stacks.\nWrite X = S · q. The first lemma confirms that the premise says what the intuition says.\n\nLemma 4.3 (what the premise says). close(q, S) = |X \\ S|. Hence the premise holds iff b(X) ≤ b(S): the\nchild has no more open stacks than the parent (Appendix A, A.24).\n\n   The premise compares only the two ends, S and X. The rule fails because a set between them can be\ncheaper still. Figure 4.1(a) shows the graph on which it fails, and panel (b) the cheaper set between.\n\nCounterexample 4.4. Let G be the graph of Figure 4.1, on customers 0, . . . , 13 with the 26 edges\n\n                  0–3, 0–4, 0–7, 0–11, 1–2, 1–5, 1–6, 2–3, 2–4, 5–9, 5–11, 5–12, 6–8,\n          6–13, 7–9, 7–10, 7–13, 8–9, 8–10, 8–11, 9–10, 9–11, 10–11, 10–12, 10–13, 12–13,\n\nwith S = {2}, k = 6 and q = 0. Then O(S) = {1, 2, 3, 4}, o(0, S) = {0, 7, 11} and cost(S, 0) = 6, so 0 is\nplayable. The customers with o(d, S) ⊆ {0, 7, 11} are 0, 3 and 4, so close(0, S) = 3 ≥ open(0, S), and 0\ncomes first in index order, so the rule keeps only 0. A solution exists from S: the order\n\n                                      1, 3, 4, 6, 12, 13, 0, 5, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11\n\ncosts at most 6. None exists from the child X = {0, 2, 3, 4}: only seven sets are reachable from X by moves\nof cost at most 6, and none is C (Appendix A, A.25).\n\n   The published implication, as a universal statement, is refuted in Lean under both readings of close,\nand so is the thesis’s Theorem 6.3.6 (Appendix A, A.26). Fink (2012, pp. 27–28) restates the rule with a\n\n                                                        15\n\f      (a) S = {2}: the definite move keeps 0                        (b) B = {2, 3, 4}: closing 3 and 4 first\n\n                                          13                                                                13\n             6                                                              6\n\n\n\n       1           3                  7                               1             3                   7\n                                                   10                                                                10\n             2              0                  8                            2                 0                  8\n\n                   4                 11                                             4                11\n\n\n                                           9                                                                  9\n                       5                                                                5\n\n                                 12                                                                12\n\n\n         closing 0 opens 0, 7, 11 and closes 0, 3, 4:                b(B) = 2 (open 0, 1) < 3 = b(X) (open 1, 7, 11),\n        open = close = 3; 3 and 4 need only stack 0                  X = {0, 2, 3, 4}: the repaired rule does not fire\n\n                        closed            customer 0, the move kept                         other customers\n                        open stack        (a) new stacks of 0; (b) open at X only\n\n\nFigure 4.1: The definite move fails because a set between parent and child is cheaper than both. (a) The graph of\nCounterexample 4.4 at the state where the rule fires. Customer 2 is closed; 1, 3 and 4 are open. Moving 0 opens the\nnew stacks 0, 7 and 11 and closes 0, 3 and 4, so open = close = 3 and the rule keeps 0 alone. But 3 and 4 have the\nsingle new stack 0 between them: closing them first gains two stacks for the price of one, which a solution starting\nwith 0 cannot match. (b) The intermediate set B = {2, 3, 4} leaves two stacks open, fewer than the three of the child\nX = {0, 2, 3, 4}, so the repaired rule of this section does not fire.\n\n\nstronger premise, counting only dominated customers of smaller index than q. The graph above refutes it\nunder no labelling, but a third twin of 3 and 4 gives a 15-customer graph that does (Appendix A, A.27). The\npublished proof moves q to the front of a solution and counts, after each prefix, the stacks q opens against\nthe customers it lets close. But customers that the solution has already closed no longer count as closed by\nq, while the stacks q opens may still be new. Appendix B.4 gives the full analysis, and Appendix B.5 the\nsearch for smaller counterexamples.\n\nThe repair. The fix is to require the comparison at every set between the two ends, not only at S. Call\nq hereditarily definite at S when b(X) ≤ b(B) for every B with S ⊆ B ⊆ X and q ∈  / B. By Lemma 4.3, the\npublished premise is the case B = S. In the counterexample the repair fails at B = {2, 3, 4} (Figure 4.1(b)),\nwhere b(B) = 2 < 3 = b(X) (Appendix A, A.28). The proof that the repair is sound needs one inequality.\n\nLemma 4.5 (open stacks are submodular). For all A, B ⊆ C, b(A ∪ B) + b(A ∩ B) ≤ b(A) + b(B) (Appendix A,\nA.29).\n\nProof. b(T ) = |O(T )| − |T |, O(A ∪ B) = O(A) ∪ O(B) and O(A ∩ B) ⊆ O(A) ∩ O(B); the customers them-\nselves balance exactly.\n\nTheorem 4.6 (the repaired definite move is sound). If q ∈      / S is hereditarily definite at S, then Extk (S) ⇒\nExtk (X). At a state with the invariant, Solk (S) ⇒ Solk (S · q) (Appendix A, A.30).\n\nProof. Take an order from S of cost at most k with prefixes Ti , in which q is the j-th step, and follow it from\nX, skipping customers already in X. A step i < j closes ci at Ti−1 ∪ X. We have S ⊆ Ti ∩ X ⊆ X and q ∈       / Ti ,\nso the hypothesis gives b(X) ≤ b(Ti ∩ X), and Lemma 4.5 gives b(Ti ∪ X) ≤ b(Ti ): the step costs no more\n\n                                                            16\n\fthan the original. After step j − 1 the closed set contains T j with the same opened set, and the rest of the\norder applies. The search’s form follows by Lemma 4.1.\n\n     This theorem is not new. The hereditary premise is the commitment condition of Tamaki, and the proof\nis the proof of the commitment lemma, Kitsunai et al. (2016, Lemma 1, p. 142); Section 4.6 makes the\ncorrespondence exact.\n\nThe matching test. The repaired rule seems to require checking exponentially many intermediate sets. It\ndoes not, and this cheap test is the algorithmic contribution of the section; we did not find it in the papers\nwe hold. In words: the rule is safe exactly when all but one of the new stacks can be paired with a different\nfreed customer who needed it. In the counterexample, closing 0 opens three new stacks and frees 3 and 4,\nboth of whom needed only stack 0: one pairing where two are required.\n\nTheorem 4.7 (the repair is a matching condition). Let Y = X \\ (S ∪ {q}), and join each d ∈ Y to the stacks\nof o(d, S) ⊆ o(q, S). Then q is hereditarily definite at S iff this bipartite graph has a matching with at least\nopen(q, S) − 1 edges (Appendix A, A.31).\n\nProof. The sets of the repair are B = S ∪ D, D ⊆ Y , and b(X) ≤ b(S ∪ D) iff |D| − |o(D)| ≤ δ with δ =\n|Y | + 1 − open(q, S), where o(D) is the union of the o(d, S). By the deficiency form of Hall’s theorem the\nlargest matching has size |Y | − maxD (|D| − |o(D)|), which is at least |Y | − δ = open(q, S) − 1 exactly when\nthe repair holds.\n\n    The bipartite graph has at most close − 1 customers on one side and open stacks on the other, and the\nmatching can stop at open − 1 edges. Every q with open(q, S) ≤ 1 passes at once, so the published rule is\nright there (Appendix A, A.32).\n\n4.4   The other rules\nEach of the remaining rules is quoted, proved sound in the form used, and shown to need the test or tie-break\nit carries.\n\nThe subset rule. “If o(ci , S) ⊆ o(c j , S) and i < j . . . move j can be removed” (Chu and Stuckey, 2009,\n§2, p. 5). As implemented, d dominates r when o(d, S) ⊆ o(r, S) and the inclusion is strict or d < r. Every\ndominated playable r is dropped, with a fallback that keeps A if all would go. The covering is sound for\nevery set S: if o(d, S) ⊆ o(r, S) and r is playable, then d is playable and Solk (S · r) ⇒ Solk (S · d), because\nd is free after r and closing d then r reaches the same state at no greater cost. With the index tie-break,\ndomination is a strict order, so an undominated move with a solution survives, and the fallback never fires\nwhen a solution exists (Appendix A, A.33). The tie-break is necessary: without it, two customers with equal\nnew stacks dominate each other and both go, which loses a solution on a 7-customer graph (Appendix A,\nA.34).\n\nThe better move. Chu and Stuckey (2009, §3.2, p. 6): “Suppose S ++ [q] and S ++ [r, q] are playable and\nclose(q, S ∪ {r}) ≥ open(q, S ∪ {r}) then if U′ = S ++ [r] ++ R is a solution there exists a solution U = S ++\n[q] ++ R′ .” Its premise is the definite move’s premise for q at the child cl(S ∪ {r}), together with playability\nof r then q, and its conclusion swaps q and r (Appendix A, A.35). The swap is sound, because both orders\nend at the same state and neither step overflows. The appeal to the definite move is not.\n    Counterexample. On the graph of Counterexample 4.4 at the root with k = 6, the definite move does\nnot fire, customers 0 and 2 both survive the subset rule, and the premise holds for r = 2, q = 0, so the rule\ndrops 2 citing 0. But {2} has a solution and {0} has none (Appendix A, A.36). At that node another kept\n\n                                                       17\n\fcustomer still has a solution, so this is a false link and not a lost node; no node where the better move loses\nthe last solution has been found. Chu (2011, Thm 6.3.8, p. 143) states the better move with a different\npremise, close(q, S) ≥ open(q, S ∪ {r}), and proves it through his Theorem 6.3.6. The same graph refutes it\nat S = {2}, r = 3, q = 0 (Appendix A, A.37).\n    Repair and test. Cite q for r only if q is hereditarily definite at cl(S ∪ {r}), which is one matching at the\nchild (Theorem 4.7); then the swap argument goes through (Appendix A, A.38).\n    Two requirements. The close count in the premise must not include the customers that r finishes on its\nown; counting them makes the rule’s own conclusion false on a 12-customer graph. And the rules must run\nin the order definite, subset, better, each citing only customers still standing. If the better move runs first,\nthe subset rule can cite a customer the better move has already dropped. An 8-customer graph then loses\na node through a cycle of links that are each true (Appendix A, A.39). The second is an instance of the\ngeneral observation of Beck et al. (2025, Prop. 3, Example 6) that mutually dominating transitions must be\ntie-broken.\n\nThe old move. Chu and Stuckey (2009, Thm 3, p. 7) let a child inherit a sibling q whose subtree failed,\nprovided that inserting q before each later step keeps that step playable (line 9 of Algorithm 4.1). The\nrule is sound as implemented: if cost(S ∪ {q}, c) ≤ k, then Solk (cl({q} ∪ S · c)) ⇒ Solk (S · q), so a refuted\nsibling stays refuted along any path whose steps pass the test (Appendix A, A.40). The test is necessary; the\nsmallest graph that shows it is the path on five vertices (Appendix A, A.41).\n\nThe memo. Whether a state with b(S) ≤ k has a solution depends on the state alone, not on the path that\nreached it (Appendix A, A.42). One concern is that the memo and the old move might not combine: a\nrefutation obtained with inherited old moves seems to depend on the ancestors, and one implementation of\nthe search declines to run both for that reason. A node taken on its own, with an old move that is not in fact\nrefuted, can indeed record a solvable state (Appendix A, A.43). Theorem B.2 (Appendix B.7) shows that\nthis never happens in a real run, because every old move is a refutation completed earlier in the same run.\n\n4.5   The repaired search is sound\nWhat a rule must satisfy. At a state S with old moves Q, the cost cut keeps the playable candidates A and\nthe filter keeps a sublist L ⊆ A. The filter is node-sound at (S, Q) if, whenever every q ∈ Q has ¬Solk (S · q),\nSolk (S) implies Solk (S · c) for some c ∈ L (Appendix A, A.44). In terms of coverings, a node is sound\nwhen two things hold. First, every link a rule cites is a true covering. Second, every chain of links from\na discarded candidate ends in L or in Q. The second condition can fail through a cycle even when every\nlink is true, which is why the order of the rules matters. Proposition B.1 shows that the repaired filter is\nnode-sound, and Theorem B.2 that node soundness, the memo and the old move together give sound runs;\nboth are in Appendix B.7. Together they give the main theorem.\n\nTheorem 4.8 (the repaired search is sound). Run the customer search with free moves, the memo, old moves\nand the filter definite → subset → better. Let the definite move fire only on a hereditarily definite customer,\nand the better move cite only under the repaired premise. If the search refutes k from the root, then no\nclosing order costs at most k, pw(G) > k − 1, and on the MOSP graph of an instance M that has a 1,\nZ(M) > k (Appendix A, A.45).\n\n    In words: each rule throws away only moves that a kept move is proved to be at least as good as, and the\nmemo and the old moves remember only failures that really happened earlier in the same search. So when\nthe search says “no”, no closing order fits in k stacks.\n\n\n\n                                                       18\n\fWhat is proved and what is tested. The theorem is about the search as specified; that our C and Python\nimplementations run it is checked, not proved. Three checks link them:\n      • a transcription of the Lean filter agreed with the implemented filter on 16,244,090 node checks over\n        graphs on 1 to 17 vertices;\n      • the matching agreed with the hereditary premise on 36,939,226 candidates;\n      • a search written from the theorems agreed with both implementations and an exact oracle on 17,431,232\n        runs over 52,592 graphs on 1 to 17 vertices.\nSection 5.3 adds a certificate checker for individual refutations.\n\nThe published rules. Under the published rules the filter is not node-sound (Appendix A, A.46). In a\ncheck of 570,206 runs with memo and old move, it lost the last solution at a node in 58 runs, all on graphs\nbuilt around Counterexample 4.4 and all at k equal to the optimum. Every one found a solution through\nanother branch and answered correctly. No whole instance on which the published rules wrongly answer\n“no” is known.\n\n4.6     The rules in pathwidth language\nThe definite move has an exact counterpart in the pathwidth literature, and this subsection makes the corre-\nspondence precise. Kitsunai et al. (2016, p. 142), stating Tamaki’s notion, call an extension τ of a vertex\nsequence σ committable when |∂ X ′ | ≥ |∂V (τ)| for every X ′ with V (σ ) ⊆ X ′ ⊆ V (τ). Their Lemma 1 says\nthat if σ extends to a feasible permutation, so does τ.\nLemma 4.9. For q ∈   / S and X = cl(S ∪ {q}): the published premise holds iff b(X) ≤ b(S), the commitment\ncondition at the single set X ′ = S; and the repaired premise holds iff X is a commitment from S, the condition\nat every X ′ between them (Appendix A, A.47).\n    So Theorem 4.6 is the commitment lemma at the child, and the published definite move is a commitment\nwhose interior condition was dropped. In Counterexample 4.4 the endpoint test passes with b(X) = 3 = b(S),\nwhile the interior set {2, 3, 4} has border 2 (Appendix A, A.48). The published premise is not wasted: by\nKitsunai et al. (2016, Lemma 10, Cor. 2, pp. 148–149) it guarantees a commitment to the least-border\nset between S and X, which need not contain q (Appendix A, A.49). In the counterexample that set is\n{2, 3, 4} (Appendix A, A.50). Committing there when the matching fails is a second repair that gives up no\npruning; we have not built it.\n    A commitment of depth one, S to S ∪ {q}, holds iff open(q, S) ≤ 1 (Appendix A, A.51). The greedy step\nof Coudert et al. (2014, Lemma 3, p. 49) is exactly that condition (Appendix A, A.52), and the push rule\nof Suchan and Villanger (2009, Rule 1, p. 330) is a case of it. So every published pathwidth rule of this\nkind lies in the region open ≤ 1, where the published definite move is right; Kobayashi et al. (2014, p. 388,\nTable 2) report depth-one commitments as “extremely effective”. Chu and Stuckey’s definite move reaches\ninto open ≥ 2, and that is where it fails. For the cost of the test, Kitsunai et al. (2016, Cor. 2) find the least\nborder between the ends of a given extension by a minimum s–t separator; Theorem 4.7 is that computation\nfor the single target X, where it becomes a bipartite matching.\n    Table 4.2 sums up, and its rows are grouped by what they say. The published definite and better moves\nare false. The free move, the memo and the repaired definite move are known pathwidth results. The subset\nrule, the repaired better move and the old move have no counterpart we found, and are proved here, as is\nthe matching test. The comparison is limited to the pathwidth papers we hold (Suchan and Villanger, 2009;\nBodlaender et al., 2012; Coudert et al., 2014, 2016; Kobayashi et al., 2014; Kitsunai et al., 2016); “none\nfound” is not a claim of priority.\n\n                                                       19\n\fTable 4.2: Two published rules are false; the free move, the memo and the repaired definite move are known pathwidth\nresults; the subset rule, the repaired better move and the old move, like the matching test, are proved here. Each rule\nof the customer search against the exact-pathwidth literature.\n\nRule                      Published counterpart                                          Status\ndefinite move,            –                                                              false (Cex. 4.4)\npublished\nbetter move, published    none found                                                     false\nfree move                 full sets (Suchan and Villanger, 2009); Kitsunai et al.        known\n                          (2016, Prop. 3)\ndefinite move,            depth-one commitments; greedy step; push rule                  known\nopen ≤ 1\ndefinite move,            commitment lemma (Kitsunai et al., 2016, Lemma 1)              known; matching test\nrepaired                                                                                 (Thm 4.7) not found\nmemo                      subset DP (Bodlaender et al., 2012); prefix and failure        known; with the old move,\n                          tables                                                         proved here (Thm B.2)\nsubset rule               none found                                                     proved here\nbetter move, repaired     none found                                                     proved here\nold move                  none found                                                     proved here\n\n\nPrior reports. We found no earlier report of the error in the 56 works we read in full of the 141 that cite\nthe customer search’s papers (Appendix B.6). The pathwidth literature has the correct form of the rule, as a\ncommitment, and none of the pathwidth papers we hold mentions MOSP.\n\n\n5      Computational results and a dataset\nSection 4 proved the repaired search sound. This section asks four practical questions about it and answers\neach with a measurement. What does the repair cost? Under the repaired rules, the total number of search\nnodes over each benchmark set changes by at most 2%. Do the published benchmark optima survive? All\n115 that rested on the published rules were proved again, and none changed. Can a reader check a refutation\nwithout trusting our code? Yes, up to about 75 customers, through a certificate and an independent checker.\nFinally, what does it make possible? A dataset of 17,714 graphs from twenty benchmark collections. For\n16,087 of them the pathwidth is certified, and that gives the exact value of every problem in the exact core.\n\n5.1    Experimental setup\nMachine and code. Every run in this section used one machine, an AMD Ryzen 9 9950X (16 cores,\n32 hardware threads) with 128 GB of memory under Ubuntu Linux. Both searches, the open-stacks cus-\ntomer search and its port to graphs, are written in C (gcc 15.2, -O3 -march=native) and driven from\nPython 3.12. A Python implementation of the same rules serves as a reference for cross-checks and emits\nthe certificates of Section 5.3. The repaired tests are the default in every implementation; the published tests\nremain available as an option.\n\nRuns. The basic job asks one question: is the optimum of this instance at most k? A worker is one single-\nthreaded process that answers such questions, one at a time. A node is one state visited by the search, the\nunit in which we report effort. Up to 32 workers ran at once on the machine, so wall-clock times carry load\nnoise. We therefore compare node counts and report time only in core-hours. A pair runs the same question\n","numLines":520,"startLine":520,"totalLines":1925}}}
