{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VeUHR9YNpnpShBotoDZWKf","type":"tool_result","content":"1039\t\n1040\t\n1041\t                                                          20\n1042\t\fTable 5.1: The repair never changed an answer and changes the total node count by at most 2%. Each pair runs\n1043\tthe same question under the published and the repaired rules in one worker. Totals are over the pairs that finished\n1044\tunder both rules; a pair stopped by its budget says nothing about cost. Largest pair ratio: the largest ratio of repaired\n1045\tto published nodes over single pairs. Open-stacks pairs refute the optimum minus one, at 600 s per call at 50–100\n1046\tcustomers and 2 × 108 nodes at 125. The graph pairs run whole descents on VSPLIB, the colouring and named graphs,\n1047\tand a sample of 500 Rome graphs.\n1048\t\n1049\tInstances                                  Vertices   Pairs finished   Nodes, published   Nodes, repaired   Total ratio   Largest pair ratio\n1050\tOpen stacks: one refutation of the optimum minus one per pair\n1051\t  MOSP corpus, base                        9–40       6,135 of 6,135           228,147           228,154      1.00003                 1.009\n1052\t  MOSP corpus, with better move            9–40       6,135 of 6,135           202,962           202,971      1.00004                 1.040\n1053\t  Chu & Stuckey, with better move          50–100         118 of 125     1,358,096,671     1,362,205,210      1.00303                 1.013\n1054\t  Chu & Stuckey, cap 2 × 108 nodes         125              11 of 23        90,969,508        90,961,419      0.99991                 1.000\n1055\tGraph pathwidth: one whole descent per pair, 120 s each\n1056\t  VSPLIB, colouring, named, 500 Rome 4–2,916            731 of 880       2,977,849,807     3,038,670,717        1.020                     –\n1057\t\n1058\t\n1059\t\n1060\ttwice in the same worker, once under the published rules and once under the repaired rules, alternating\n1061\twhich goes first. A descent solves a whole graph: it lowers k from an upper bound until a refutation proves\n1062\tthe last value optimal. Each table states its time or node budget.\n1063\t\n1064\tConfigurations. The open-stacks runs use two configurations of the rules of Section 4, which differ only\n1065\tin the better move. The base configuration leaves it off. The configuration with better move switches it on\n1066\tfor instances whose customers need at most five patterns on average. This is the setting under which the\n1067\tcustomer search certified the benchmark values it proved.\n1068\t\n1069\tInstances. The MOSP corpus is the 6,376 instance files of five public open-stacks collections. They are\n1070\tthe 2005 Constraint Modelling Challenge (Smith and Gent, 2005) with a second copy of 46 of its files,\n1071\tthe random instances of Chu and Stuckey (2009) (30 to 125 customers), those of Faggioli and Bentivoglio\n1072\t(1998), and the 24 industrial SCOOP instances. They are the first rows of Table 5.3. A Chu–Stuckey name\n1073\tsuch as Random-125-125-2-4_0 gives the number of customers, the number of patterns, the generator’s\n1074\tdensity class and the instance number. The graph benchmarks are those of Section 3 and Table 5.3.\n1075\t\n1076\tProvenance.         Every stored value comes with a witness, an order that attains it, and one of three prove-\n1077\tnances:\n1078\t      • certified by search: a search proved that no order does better, by refuting the value minus one;\n1079\t      • certified by bound: a lower bound equals the witness’s value, so no search was needed;\n1080\t      • upper bound only: the witness is all there is, and optimality is not proved.\n1081\tOf the 6,376 corpus values, 6,374 are certified and two are upper bounds only.\n1082\t\n1083\t5.2     The cost of the repair, and the benchmark values\n1084\tThe repaired search adds one matching test to the definite move and to the better move (Section 4.3). A\n1085\tcandidate that fails the matching is passed over, so the repaired tree can only differ where the matching fails.\n1086\tTable 5.1 shows how rarely that matters.\n1087\t    In every row of Table 5.1 the repaired and the published rules gave the same answer, and the total node\n1088\tcounts differ by at most 2%. Single pairs differ by at most 4%. The cost per node is also small: about 2%\n1089\tin the C implementation, measured on six hard instances at 75 to 125 customers. The published test passes\n1090\tand the matching then fails on only 0.07–0.27% of definite-move candidates, a rate that rises with size.\n1091\t\n1092\t                                                                   21\n1093\t\fThe benchmark values survive. No published optimum changed under the repaired rules. Of the 6,374\n1094\tcertified values in the MOSP corpus, 6,259 do not depend on the customer search at all. Each was proved in\n1095\tone of four ways:\n1096\t      • by an exhaustive computation over all subsets of customers (up to 15 customers);\n1097\t      • by a refutation from a SAT (Boolean satisfiability) solver whose proof an independent checker ac-\n1098\t        cepted, in DRAT, the standard proof format for SAT solvers;\n1099\t      • by an earlier SAT search;\n1100\t      • or by a lower bound equal to the value.\n1101\tThe other 115 rested on the customer search under the published rules. The repaired search refuted the value\n1102\tminus one on all 115, so all 115 values stand (as of 6 October 2026).\n1103\t    The seven hardest are 125-customer Chu–Stuckey instances at density classes 2 and 4. They are too\n1104\tlarge for one worker, so their search was split: the top levels of the tree are expanded into independent\n1105\tsubtrees, each refuted on its own worker, and the value is refuted when every subtree is. The split is proved\n1106\tsound (Appendix A, A.53). All seven were refuted at 18 to 369 core-hours each, 1,112.6 in total (Table B.1,\n1107\tAppendix B.8).\n1108\t\n1109\tTwo values in flight. The corpus also holds two values that were never certified by any method: 25 for\n1110\tRandom-125-125-2-2_0 and 21 for Random-125-125-2-3_0. The same split is refuting the value\n1111\tminus one on each. As of 6 October 2026 it has refuted 2,196 and 2,601 subtrees, using 443 and 453 core-\n1112\thours, and found no order of the smaller value. If the run finishes it certifies both values; an order of the\n1113\tsmaller value would improve the stored value instead.\n1114\t\n1115\t5.3     A checkable proof of a refutation\n1116\tA refutation by the search is a claim about a tree of up to 1012 nodes, and Theorem 4.8 proves the rules\n1117\tsound, not the code that runs them. So the search emits a certificate: the tree it explored, every pruning\n1118\tstep with its rule and its witness, and for each definite and better step the matching of Theorem 4.7. An\n1119\tindependent checker, which shares no code with the search, reads the instance matrix and the certificate. At\n1120\tevery node it checks that\n1121\t      • the premise of every pruning step holds, recomputed from the matrix;\n1122\t      • every matching is valid: distinct customers, distinct stacks, each stack newly opened by its customer,\n1123\t        and at least open − 1 edges;\n1124\t      • every candidate within the bound is either a child or covered by a pruning step, and every chain of\n1125\t        covers ends at a child or an old move, with no cycle;\n1126\tand it replays the tree from the root. Each accepted step is a Lean theorem. That an acyclic covering by such\n1127\tsteps is sound is a short paper argument, not yet a formal proof.\n1128\t     Table 5.2 gives the results. Every refutation emitted within its budget verifies, 6,275 of 6,286, and none\n1129\tis rejected. All 35.5 MB of certificates check in 147 s. The base configuration gives the same picture: 6,276\n1130\tof 6,286 verify and none is rejected. The checker also catches the error of Section 4.3. On the graph of\n1131\tCounterexample 4.4, rooted at {2}, the published rules emit a complete tree that claims there is no solution.\n1132\tA checker of the published premises accepts it. Ours rejects it, even with a largest matching attached,\n1133\tbecause the matching has one edge where two are needed. At 125 customers the trees are too large to ship\n1134\tas certificates.\n1135\t\n1136\t                                                      22\n1137\t\fTable 5.2: Every certificate the search emitted verifies, none is rejected, and checking takes milliseconds. Certificates\n1138\tfor the repaired rules, configuration with better move, refuting the optimum minus one on the certified MOSP corpus\n1139\tat 9–75 customers, with 60 s to emit each. Not emitted: the emitter did not finish in time. Size is the compressed\n1140\tcertificate; check time is the independent checker’s.\n1141\t\n1142\t   Customers     Instances   Verified    Rejected    Not emitted     Median size (bytes, gzip)   Median check (ms)\n1143\t   9–10             1,614       1,614           0                0                        332                   0.04\n1144\t   11–20            2,508       2,508           0                0                        356                   0.08\n1145\t   21–30            1,816       1,816           0                0                        414                   0.24\n1146\t   31–40              197         197           0                0                        650                   1.27\n1147\t   41–50              120         120           0                0                     12,045                  33.24\n1148\t   51–60                1           1           0                0                    126,508                 278.86\n1149\t   61–75               30          19           0               11                     26,516                 143.70\n1150\t\n1151\t\n1152\t5.4    A dataset with certified pathwidth\n1153\tSection 2 showed that one certified pathwidth w answers every problem in the exact core. Pathwidth, vertex\n1154\tseparation and the vertex separator game take the value w, and MOSP, gate matrix layout with multiple\n1155\tfolding, one-dimensional logic, interval thickness, narrowness and node search take w + 1. We therefore\n1156\tbuilt one dataset from every benchmark collection of those problems that we could obtain, including the\n1157\tMOSP corpus.\n1158\t    The unit of the dataset is the isomorphism class: the set of instances whose graphs are the same up\n1159\tto renaming the vertices. For a matrix instance the graph is its MOSP graph. Two instances in one class\n1160\thave the same value under every problem, so the dataset holds one record per class, with its members and\n1161\tthe renaming that maps each onto the record’s graph. Classes were found with nauty, a standard tool that\n1162\tcomputes a canonical form for each graph. Each record carries the graph, the width w, its provenance, and\n1163\ta witness layout of exactly that width. A record certified by bound also carries a minor certificate: a list\n1164\tof vertex deletions and edge contractions after which every vertex has degree at least w. Any checker can\n1165\treplay it, and it proves the pathwidth is at least w.\n1166\t    Table 5.3 gives the counts. The collections are the MOSP collections (Smith and Gent, 2005; Faggioli\n1167\tand Bentivoglio, 1998; Chu and Stuckey, 2009; de Carvalho and Soma, 2015; Frinhani et al., 2018), eleven\n1168\tVLSI gate matrix circuits (de Oliveira and Lorena, 2002), the VSPLIB sets (Duarte et al., 2012) and the\n1169\tSmall graphs (Martí et al., 2008), the Rome graphs (Di Battista et al., 1997), and treewidth collections used\n1170\tas pathwidth benchmarks (van den Broek and Bodlaender, n.d.; Larisch and Salfelder, n.d.; Dell et al., 2017,\n1171\t2018). Of the 17,714 classes, 16,087 carry a certified pathwidth with a witness layout and 862 a witnessed\n1172\tupper bound. The 610 large instances of Frinhani et al. (2018), with 400 to 1,000 customers, are included\n1173\tso that the dataset covers every published MOSP collection we hold. None of them has a value yet, because\n1174\tan exact search at that size is out of reach.\n1175\t    An independent checker, sharing no code with the solvers, verifies the dataset. It re-reads all 20,987\n1176\tmember files with its own readers, maps each onto its record’s graph by its renaming, recomputes the width\n1177\tof every witness layout and replays every minor certificate. It reports no failure on the 16,949 records. A\n1178\tvalue certified by search cannot be checked from its record; it rests on the repaired search, and for instances\n1179\twithin reach of the emitter the certificate of Section 5.3 is how a third party can check it.\n1180\t\n1181\tAgreement with published values. All 84 Small graphs agree with the pathwidths of Mallach (2018,\n1182\tTables 4–5). Ten of the eleven VLSI circuits are certified at their published best-known track counts\n1183\t(de Oliveira and Lorena, 2002, Table I); the eleventh, W4 with 202 nets, is open at 28 against 27.\n1184\t\n1185\t\n1186\t\n1187\t                                                           23\n1188\t\fTable 5.3: Twenty collections, 21,754 instance files, reduce to 17,714 isomorphism classes, and 16,087 of them have\n1189\ta certified pathwidth. The open classes are mostly in the PACE sets, the VSPLIB grids and Harwell-Boeing graphs,\n1190\tand the MOSP sets of Carvalho and Soma and of Frinhani et al. Classes: isomorphism classes within the collection\n1191\t(78 graphs above 5,000 vertices are compared by file content only). Counted here: classes whose first member, in the\n1192\torder of the table, is in this collection, so the column sums to the dataset’s class count. The next four columns split\n1193\tthose classes by provenance; No witnessed value: no layout is stored yet. a The same 46 files distributed a second time\n1194\tunder other names; every class is counted under its first copy.\n1195\t\n1196\t                                                                              Certified\n1197\tCollection                             Files   Classes   Counted here   by search   by bound      Upper bound only   No witnessed value\n1198\tMOSP: Challenge 2005                   5,806    3,169          3,169       3,168              1                 0                    0\n1199\tMOSP: Challenge 2005 (second copy)a       46       46              0           0              0                 0                    0\n1200\tMOSP: Chu & Stuckey                      200      200            200         198              0                 2                    0\n1201\tMOSP: Faggioli & Bentivoglio             300      287            274         274              0                 0                    0\n1202\tMOSP: SCOOP                               24       24             24          24              0                 0                    0\n1203\tVLSI gate matrix circuits                 11       11             11           5              5                 1                    0\n1204\tVSPLIB trees                              50       28             28          28              0                 0                    0\n1205\tVSPLIB grids                              50       50             50           9              0                19                   22\n1206\tVSPLIB Harwell-Boeing                     73       72             72          32              7                33                    0\n1207\tSmall (Marti et al. 2008)                 84       84             84          32             52                 0                    0\n1208\tRome graphs                           11,534   11,199         11,199       8,126          2,746               326                    1\n1209\tTreewidthLIB colouring                    58       58             58          18             13                27                    0\n1210\tfreetdi named graphs                     150      146            143          97             22                23                    1\n1211\tfreetdi control-flow graphs            1,817    1,070          1,064         398            661                 4                    1\n1212\tMOSP: Frinhani et al. 2018, large        610      610            610           0              0                 0                  610\n1213\tPACE 2016                                291      288            112          37             18                51                    6\n1214\tPACE 2017 exact                          200      199            193          61              7               115                   10\n1215\tPACE 2017 heuristic                      200      197            173          11              3                45                  114\n1216\tPACE 2017 bonus                          100      100            100          23              0                77                    0\n1217\tMOSP: Carvalho & Soma 2015               150      150            150          11              0               139                    0\n1218\tAll                                   21,754                  17,714      12,552          3,535               862                  765\n1219\t\n1220\t\n1221\t\n1222\tA caution about MOSP files. A MOSP file can be read with rows as customers or with rows as patterns,\n1223\tand the two readings give different graphs. The same public repository ships the de Carvalho and Soma\n1224\t(2015) instances as customers by patterns and the Chu and Stuckey (2009) instances beside them as patterns\n1225\tby customers. Only published densities and values tell them apart. Read the wrong way round, an instance\n1226\tcertifies at a different value.\n1227\t\n1228\t\n1229\t6     Conclusion\n1230\tWe asked which equalities of the Linhares–Yanasse table are true, and what follows when results, algorithms\n1231\tand benchmarks are moved between its twelve problems through pathwidth. Eight rows are exact, counting\n1232\tthe pathwidth row itself: each of those eight problems is pathwidth, or pathwidth plus one, on every input.\n1233\tTwo rows, split bandwidth and edge search, are bands one and two wide, and two, PLA folding and edge\n1234\tseparation, are false as stated, although a variant of each is exact (Table 2.3). Moving between the problems\n1235\tpaid twice. Read as a pathwidth algorithm, the best exact open-stacks search turned out to rest on two false\n1236\tdominance theorems, and the rule that corrects them was already known in the pathwidth literature. And\n1237\tone certified pathwidth answers every problem in the exact core at once, which is what made a single dataset\n1238\tfor nine problems possible.\n1239\t\n1240\tWhat transfers, and what does not. Within the exact core, anything proved or computed for one problem\n1241\tholds for the others with the offset of Table 2.3: an algorithm, a lower bound, a benchmark value. Across\n1242\ta band only an approximation transfers. Split bandwidth and edge search stay within one of the number\n1243\t\n1244\t\n1245\t                                                                24\n1246\t\fof open stacks, as the table’s literal reading says, but not at a fixed offset, so an exact value of one does\n1247\tnot give an exact value of the other. Across a false row nothing transfers. Simple PLA folding can need\n1248\tarbitrarily more tracks than the pathwidth suggests, and cutwidth is not within a constant of pathwidth on\n1249\tstars. Section 3 showed why these distinctions went unchecked for two decades: the communities rarely\n1250\tread one another, and most of what is known about the family is published under “pathwidth”.\n1251\t\n1252\tWhat the search teaches about dominance rules. The two false theorems were published in 2009 and\n1253\trestated in later work, and the implementations that use them reported no failure. They give no wrong\n1254\tanswer on any whole instance we know of: in every run of our checks where the published rules lost the\n1255\tlast solution at a node, another branch found a solution (Section 4.5). So the absence of wrong answers\n1256\twas never evidence that the rules were sound. Three things in this paper bear on that. Reading the rules in\n1257\tpathwidth language put the correct rule, the commitment lemma, beside the published one. Proving each\n1258\trule on its own was not enough: the order in which the rules run matters, because a cycle of true links can\n1259\tstill lose a node (Section 4.5). And a certificate whose checker recomputes every premise rejects the false\n1260\trefutation that a checker of the published premises accepts (Section 5.3). The repair is cheap, one bipartite\n1261\tmatching per candidate and at most 2% more nodes per benchmark set, and it changed none of the 115\n1262\tbenchmark optima it re-proved.\n1263\t\n1264\tWhat a reader can do differently. Cite the corrected table, Table 2.3, rather than the 2002 one. Look for\n1265\tresults on MOSP and gate matrix layout under “pathwidth”. And in any implementation of the rules of Chu\n1266\tand Stuckey (2009), including the code behind the optima of Frinhani et al. (2018) that Martin et al. (2022)\n1267\treuse (Appendix B.6), replace the published definite and better moves with the matching test. The values\n1268\tthose implementations report are not known to be wrong, but their refutations are not proved.\n1269\t\n1270\tLimits.   Four limits remain.\n1271\t\n1272\t    • The Lean model of the search and the C and Python code that run it are linked by testing and by\n1273\t      certificates, not by proof (Section 4.5).\n1274\t\n1275\t    • That an acyclic covering by checked steps is sound, which is the certificate checker’s acceptance\n1276\t      condition, is a paper argument (Section 5.3).\n1277\t\n1278\t    • LaPaugh’s equality es = pes is stated and not formalised. No row needs it (Section 2.2).\n1279\t\n1280\t    • One reference of the table, Kashiwabara and Fujisawa (1979), is not held. Its row, interval thickness,\n1281\t      is proved from two other references of the table (Section 2.2).\n1282\t\n1283\tOpen problems. Two values of the MOSP corpus, Random-125-125-2-2_0 and Random-125-125-2-3_0,\n1284\thave never been certified. As of 6 October 2026 the split search is still refuting the value minus one on each\n1285\tand has found no order of the smaller value (Section 5.2). Of the 17,714 classes of the dataset, 1,627 have\n1286\tno certified pathwidth. One more budget step for every open class within the size our implementation han-\n1287\tdles costs at most 500 core-hours, but on the Rome graphs each such step has closed only about 40% of\n1288\twhat it attempted, so the graph sets will not close by budget alone. The 610 MOSP instances with 400 to\n1289\t1,000 customers, and the 154 classes above 1,024 vertices per component, the limit of our implementation,\n1290\tare beyond an exact search. Beyond budgets, a class is certified without search only when a lower bound\n1291\tmeets its witness (Section 5.4). The open classes are therefore also a test set for stronger lower bounds on\n1292\tpathwidth.\n1293\t\n1294\t\n1295\t\n1296\t                                                     25\n1297\t\fData, code and proofs\n1298\tEverything this paper reports can be checked from its repository, URL to be added on release . The repos-\n1299\titory holds:\n1300\t\n1301\t    • the Lean 4 development, with every theorem the paper names (Appendix A) and an axiom check that\n1302\t      covers all of them;\n1303\t\n1304\t    • the source of both solvers, the open-stacks customer search and the graph pathwidth solver, in C and\n1305\t      in Python, with the published and the repaired rules;\n1306\t\n1307\t    • the dataset of Section 5.4, the certificates of Section 5.3 and the independent checkers of both;\n1308\t\n1309\t    • the bibliometric data of Section 3: the cached OpenAlex responses, the relevance labels and the\n1310\t      mechanical rule;\n1311\t\n1312\t    • a script for every table, figure and number in the paper, each of which regenerates it from the recorded\n1313\t      data.\n1314\t\n1315\t\n1316\tReferences\n1317\tJ. Christopher Beck, Ryo Kuroiwa, Jimmy H. M. Lee, Peter J. Stuckey, and Allen Z. Zhong. Transition\n1318\t   dominance in domain-independent dynamic programming. In 31st International Conference on Princi-\n1319\t   ples and Practice of Constraint Programming (CP 2025), volume 340 of LIPIcs, pages 5:1–5:23, 2025.\n1320\t   doi: 10.4230/LIPIcs.CP.2025.5.\n1321\t\n1322\tDaniel Bienstock and Paul Seymour. Monotonicity in graph searching. Journal of Algorithms, 12(2):239–\n1323\t  245, 1991. doi: 10.1016/0196-6774(91)90003-H.\n1324\t\n1325\tHans L. Bodlaender, Fedor V. Fomin, Arie M. C. A. Koster, Dieter Kratsch, and Dimitrios M. Thilikos. A\n1326\t  note on exact algorithms for vertex ordering problems on graphs. Theory of Computing Systems, 50(3):\n1327\t  420–432, 2012. doi: 10.1007/s00224-011-9312-0.\n1328\t\n1329\tGeoffrey Chu and Peter J. Stuckey. Minimizing the maximum number of open stacks by customer search.\n1330\t  In Principles and Practice of Constraint Programming (CP 2009), volume 5732 of Lecture Notes in\n1331\t  Computer Science, pages 242–257. Springer, 2009. doi: 10.1007/978-3-642-04244-7_21.\n1332\t\n1333\tGeoffrey G. Chu. Improving Combinatorial Optimization. PhD thesis, Department of Computer Science and\n1334\t  Software Engineering, The University of Melbourne, 2011. Minerva Access, http://hdl.handle.\n1335\t  net/11343/36679.\n1336\t\n1337\tDavid Coudert, Dorian Mazauric, and Nicolas Nisse. Experimental evaluation of a branch and bound algo-\n1338\t  rithm for computing pathwidth. In Experimental Algorithms (SEA 2014), volume 8504 of Lecture Notes\n1339\t  in Computer Science, pages 46–58. Springer, 2014. doi: 10.1007/978-3-319-07959-2_5.\n1340\t\n1341\tDavid Coudert, Dorian Mazauric, and Nicolas Nisse. Experimental evaluation of a branch-and-bound algo-\n1342\t  rithm for computing pathwidth and directed pathwidth. ACM Journal of Experimental Algorithmics, 21:\n1343\t 1–23, 2016. doi: 10.1145/2851494.\n1344\t\n1345\tMarco Antonio Moreira de Carvalho and Nei Yoshihiro Soma. A breadth-first search applied to the min-\n1346\t imization of the open stacks. Journal of the Operational Research Society, 66(6):936–946, 2015. doi:\n1347\t 10.1057/jors.2014.60.\n1348\t\n1349\t                                                     26\n1350\t\fA. C. M. de Oliveira and L. A. N. Lorena. A constructive genetic algorithm for gate matrix layout problems.\n1351\t  IEEE Transactions on Computer-Aided Design of Integrated Circuits and Systems, 21(8):969–974, 2002.\n1352\t  doi: 10.1109/TCAD.2002.800454.\n1353\t\n1354\tHolger Dell, Thore Husfeldt, Bart M. P. Jansen, Petteri Kaski, Christian Komusiewicz, and Frances A.\n1355\t  Rosamond. The first parameterized algorithms and computational experiments challenge. In 11th Inter-\n1356\t  national Symposium on Parameterized and Exact Computation (IPEC 2016), volume 63 of LIPIcs, pages\n1357\t  30:1–30:9, 2017. doi: 10.4230/LIPIcs.IPEC.2016.30.\n1358\t\n1359\tHolger Dell, Christian Komusiewicz, Nimrod Talmon, and Mathias Weller. The PACE 2017 parameterized\n1360\t  algorithms and computational experiments challenge: The second iteration. In 12th International Sym-\n1361\t  posium on Parameterized and Exact Computation (IPEC 2017), volume 89 of LIPIcs, pages 30:1–30:12,\n1362\t  2018. doi: 10.4230/LIPIcs.IPEC.2017.30.\n1363\t\n1364\tGiuseppe Di Battista, Ashim Garg, Giuseppe Liotta, Roberto Tamassia, Emanuele Tassinari, and Francesco\n1365\t  Vargiu. An experimental comparison of four graph drawing algorithms. Computational Geometry, 7(5–6):\n1366\t  303–325, 1997. doi: 10.1016/S0925-7721(96)00005-3.\n1367\t\n1368\tAbraham Duarte, Laureano F. Escudero, Rafael Martí, Nenad Mladenovic, Juan José Pantrigo, and Jesús\n1369\t  Sánchez-Oro. Variable neighborhood sea