al of the ACM, 40(2):224–245,\n1430\t 1993. doi: 10.1145/151261.151263.\n1431\t\n1432\tL. Larisch and F. Salfelder. freetdi: named graphs and control-flow graphs. https://github.com/\n1433\t  freetdi/named-graphs, https://github.com/freetdi/CFGs, n.d. Licence CC0 1.0.\n1434\t\n1435\tThomas Lengauer. Black-white pebbles and graph separation. Acta Informatica, 16(4):465–475, 1981. doi:\n1436\t  10.1007/BF00264496.\n1437\t\n1438\tAlexandre Linhares. Industrial Pattern Sequencing Problems: Some Complexity Results and New Local\n1439\t  Search Models. PhD thesis, Instituto Nacional de Pesquisas Espaciais (INPE), São José dos Campos,\n1440\t  Brazil, 2001. Doctoral thesis in Applied Computing, advisor H. H. Yanasse.\n1441\t\n1442\tAlexandre Linhares and Horacio Hideki Yanasse. Connections between cutting-pattern sequencing, VLSI\n1443\t  design, and flexible machines. Computers & Operations Research, 29(14):1759–1772, 2002. doi: 10.\n1444\t  1016/S0305-0548(01)00054-5.\n1445\t\n1446\tSven Mallach. Linear ordering based MIP formulations for the vertex separation or pathwidth problem.\n1447\t  Journal of Discrete Algorithms, 52–53:156–167, 2018. doi: 10.1016/j.jda.2018.11.012.\n1448\t\n1449\tRafael Martí, Vicente Campos, and Estefanía Piñana. A branch and bound algorithm for the matrix\n1450\t  bandwidth minimization. European Journal of Operational Research, 186(2):513–528, 2008. doi:\n1451\t  10.1016/j.ejor.2007.02.004.\n1452\t\n1453\tMateus Martin, Horacio Hideki Yanasse, and Maria José Pinto. Mathematical models for the minimization\n1454\t of open stacks problem. International Transactions in Operational Research, 29(5):2944–2967, 2022.\n1455\t doi: 10.1111/itor.13053.\n1456\t\n1457\t\n1458\t\n1459\t                                                   28\n1460\t\fRolf H. Möhring. Graph problems related to gate matrix layout and PLA folding. In Computational Graph\n1461\t  Theory, volume 7 of Computing Supplementum, pages 17–51. Springer-Verlag, Wien, 1990. doi: 10.1007/\n1462\t  978-3-7091-9076-0_2.\n1463\t\n1464\tTatsuo Ohtsuki, Hajimu Mori, Ernest S. Kuh, Toshinobu Kashiwabara, and Toshio Fujisawa. One-\n1465\t  dimensional logic gate assignment and interval graphs. IEEE Transactions on Circuits and Systems,\n1466\t  26(9):675–684, 1979. doi: 10.1109/TCS.1979.1084695.\n1467\t\n1468\tJason Priem, Heather Piwowar, and Richard Orr. OpenAlex: A fully-open index of scholarly works, authors,\n1469\t  venues, institutions, and concepts. arXiv:2205.01833, 2022.\n1470\t\n1471\tBarbara M. Smith and Ian P. Gent. Constraint modelling challenge 2005. In Fifth Workshop on Modelling\n1472\t  and Solving Problems with Constraints, held at IJCAI 2005, Edinburgh, 2005.\n1473\t\n1474\tKarol Suchan and Yngve Villanger. Computing pathwidth faster than 2n . In Parameterized and Exact Com-\n1475\t  putation (IWPEC 2009), volume 5917 of Lecture Notes in Computer Science, pages 324–335. Springer,\n1476\t  2009. doi: 10.1007/978-3-642-11269-0_27.\n1477\t\n1478\tJ.-W. van den Broek and Hans L. Bodlaender. TreewidthLIB. http://www.cs.uu.nl/research/\n1479\t   projects/treewidthlib/, n.d. Graph files no longer served; colouring subset obtained from\n1480\t   https://mat.tepper.cmu.edu/COLOR/instances.html.\n1481\t\n1482\tOmar Wing, S. Huang, and R. Wang. Gate matrix layout. IEEE Transactions on Computer-Aided Design of\n1483\t Integrated Circuits and Systems, 4(3):220–231, 1985. doi: 10.1109/TCAD.1985.1270118.\n1484\t\n1485\tHoracio Hideki Yanasse. A transformation for solving a pattern sequencing problem in the wood cut industry.\n1486\t Pesquisa Operacional, 17(1):57–70, 1997a.\n1487\t\n1488\tHoracio Hideki Yanasse. On a pattern sequencing problem to minimize the maximum number of open"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d20ab00f-ced5-4701-8f82-053c98f1df8f","uuid":"d3b9b54c-8ad1-4b85-840c-6a147a721b64","timestamp":"2026-10-06T20:14:51.219Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/main.txt","content":"\n\n                                                          20\n\fTable 5.1: The repair never changed an answer and changes the total node count by at most 2%. Each pair runs\nthe same question under the published and the repaired rules in one worker. Totals are over the pairs that finished\nunder both rules; a pair stopped by its budget says nothing about cost. Largest pair ratio: the largest ratio of repaired\nto published nodes over single pairs. Open-stacks pairs refute the optimum minus one, at 600 s per call at 50–100\ncustomers and 2 × 108 nodes at 125. The graph pairs run whole descents on VSPLIB, the colouring and named graphs,\nand a sample of 500 Rome graphs.\n\nInstances                                  Vertices   Pairs finished   Nodes, published   Nodes, repaired   Total ratio   Largest pair ratio\nOpen stacks: one refutation of the optimum minus one per pair\n  MOSP corpus, base                        9–40       6,135 of 6,135           228,147           228,154      1.00003                 1.009\n  MOSP corpus, with better move            9–40       6,135 of 6,135           202,962           202,971      1.00004                 1.040\n  Chu & Stuckey, with better move          50–100         118 of 125     1,358,096,671     1,362,205,210      1.00303                 1.013\n  Chu & Stuckey, cap 2 × 108 nodes         125              11 of 23        90,969,508        90,961,419      0.99991                 1.000\nGraph pathwidth: one whole descent per pair, 120 s each\n  VSPLIB, colouring, named, 500 Rome 4–2,916            731 of 880       2,977,849,807     3,038,670,717        1.020                     –\n\n\n\ntwice in the same worker, once under the published rules and once under the repaired rules, alternating\nwhich goes first. A descent solves a whole graph: it lowers k from an upper bound until a refutation proves\nthe last value optimal. Each table states its time or node budget.\n\nConfigurations. The open-stacks runs use two configurations of the rules of Section 4, which differ only\nin the better move. The base configuration leaves it off. The configuration with better move switches it on\nfor instances whose customers need at most five patterns on average. This is the setting under which the\ncustomer search certified the benchmark values it proved.\n\nInstances. The MOSP corpus is the 6,376 instance files of five public open-stacks collections. They are\nthe 2005 Constraint Modelling Challenge (Smith and Gent, 2005) with a second copy of 46 of its files,\nthe random instances of Chu and Stuckey (2009) (30 to 125 customers), those of Faggioli and Bentivoglio\n(1998), and the 24 industrial SCOOP instances. They are the first rows of Table 5.3. A Chu–Stuckey name\nsuch as Random-125-125-2-4_0 gives the number of customers, the number of patterns, the generator’s\ndensity class and the instance number. The graph benchmarks are those of Section 3 and Table 5.3.\n\nProvenance.         Every stored value comes with a witness, an order that attains it, and one of three prove-\nnances:\n      • certified by search: a search proved that no order does better, by refuting the value minus one;\n      • certified by bound: a lower bound equals the witness’s value, so no search was needed;\n      • upper bound only: the witness is all there is, and optimality is not proved.\nOf the 6,376 corpus values, 6,374 are certified and two are upper bounds only.\n\n5.2     The cost of the repair, and the benchmark values\nThe repaired search adds one matching test to the definite move and to the better move (Section 4.3). A\ncandidate that fails the matching is passed over, so the repaired tree can only differ where the matching fails.\nTable 5.1 shows how rarely that matters.\n    In every row of Table 5.1 the repaired and the published rules gave the same answer, and the total node\ncounts differ by at most 2%. Single pairs differ by at most 4%. The cost per node is also small: about 2%\nin the C implementation, measured on six hard instances at 75 to 125 customers. The published test passes\nand the matching then fails on only 0.07–0.27% of definite-move candidates, a rate that rises with size.\n\n                                                                   21\n\fThe benchmark values survive. No published optimum changed under the repaired rules. Of the 6,374\ncertified values in the MOSP corpus, 6,259 do not depend on the customer search at all. Each was proved in\none of four ways:\n      • by an exhaustive computation over all subsets of customers (up to 15 customers);\n      • by a refutation from a SAT (Boolean satisfiability) solver whose proof an independent checker ac-\n        cepted, in DRAT, the standard proof format for SAT solvers;\n      • by an earlier SAT search;\n      • or by a lower bound equal to the value.\nThe other 115 rested on the customer search under the published rules. The repaired search refuted the value\nminus one on all 115, so all 115 values stand (as of 6 October 2026).\n    The seven hardest are 125-customer Chu–Stuckey instances at density classes 2 and 4. They are too\nlarge for one worker, so their search was split: the top levels of the tree are expanded into independent\nsubtrees, each refuted on its own worker, and the value is refuted when every subtree is. The split is proved\nsound (Appendix A, A.53). All seven were refuted at 18 to 369 core-hours each, 1,112.6 in total (Table B.1,\nAppendix B.8).\n\nTwo values in flight. The corpus also holds two values that were never certified by any method: 25 for\nRandom-125-125-2-2_0 and 21 for Random-125-125-2-3_0. The same split is refuting the value\nminus one on each. As of 6 October 2026 it has refuted 2,196 and 2,601 subtrees, using 443 and 453 core-\nhours, and found no order of the smaller value. If the run finishes it certifies both values; an order of the\nsmaller value would improve the stored value instead.\n\n5.3     A checkable proof of a refutation\nA refutation by the search is a claim about a tree of up to 1012 nodes, and Theorem 4.8 proves the rules\nsound, not the code that runs them. So the search emits a certificate: the tree it explored, every pruning\nstep with its rule and its witness, and for each definite and better step the matching of Theorem 4.7. An\nindependent checker, which shares no code with the search, reads the instance matrix and the certificate. At\nevery node it checks that\n      • the premise of every pruning step holds, recomputed from the matrix;\n      • every matching is valid: distinct customers, distinct stacks, each stack newly opened by its customer,\n        and at least open − 1 edges;\n      • every candidate within the bound is either a child or covered by a pruning step, and every chain of\n        covers ends at a child or an old move, with no cycle;\nand it replays the tree from the root. Each accepted step is a Lean theorem. That an acyclic covering by such\nsteps is sound is a short paper argument, not yet a formal proof.\n     Table 5.2 gives the results. Every refutation emitted within its budget verifies, 6,275 of 6,286, and none\nis rejected. All 35.5 MB of certificates check in 147 s. The base configuration gives the same picture: 6,276\nof 6,286 verify and none is rejected. The checker also catches the error of Section 4.3. On the graph of\nCounterexample 4.4, rooted at {2}, the published rules emit a complete tree that claims there is no solution.\nA checker of the published premises accepts it. Ours rejects it, even with a largest matching attached,\nbecause the matching has one edge where two are needed. At 125 customers the trees are too large to ship\nas certificates.\n\n                                                      22\n\fTable 5.2: Every certificate the search emitted verifies, none is rejected, and checking takes milliseconds. Certificates\nfor the repaired rules, configuration with better move, refuting the optimum minus one on the certified MOSP corpus\nat 9–75 customers, with 60 s to emit each. Not emitted: the emitter did not finish in time. Size is the compressed\ncertificate; check time is the independent checker’s.\n\n   Customers     Instances   Verified    Rejected    Not emitted     Median size (bytes, gzip)   Median check (ms)\n   9–10             1,614       1,614           0                0                        332                   0.04\n   11–20            2,508       2,508           0                0                        356                   0.08\n   21–30            1,816       1,816           0                0                        414                   0.24\n   31–40              197         197           0                0                        650                   1.27\n   41–50              120         120           0                0                     12,045                  33.24\n   51–60                1           1           0                0                    126,508                 278.86\n   61–75               30          19           0               11                     26,516                 143.70\n\n\n5.4    A dataset with certified pathwidth\nSection 2 showed that one certified pathwidth w answers every problem in the exact core. Pathwidth, vertex\nseparation and the vertex separator game take the value w, and MOSP, gate matrix layout with multiple\nfolding, one-dimensional logic, interval thickness, narrowness and node search take w + 1. We therefore\nbuilt one dataset from every benchmark collection of those problems that we could obtain, including the\nMOSP corpus.\n    The unit of the dataset is the isomorphism class: the set of instances whose graphs are the same up\nto renaming the vertices. For a matrix instance the graph is its MOSP graph. Two instances in one class\nhave the same value under every problem, so the dataset holds one record per class, with its members and\nthe renaming that maps each onto the record’s graph. Classes were found with nauty, a standard tool that\ncomputes a canonical form for each graph. Each record carries the graph, the width w, its provenance, and\na witness layout of exactly that width. A record certified by bound also carries a minor certificate: a list\nof vertex deletions and edge contractions after which every vertex has degree at least w. Any checker can\nreplay it, and it proves the pathwidth is at least w.\n    Table 5.3 gives the counts. The collections are the MOSP collections (Smith and Gent, 2005; Faggioli\nand Bentivoglio, 1998; Chu and Stuckey, 2009; de Carvalho and Soma, 2015; Frinhani et al., 2018), eleven\nVLSI gate matrix circuits (de Oliveira and Lorena, 2002), the VSPLIB sets (Duarte et al., 2012) and the\nSmall graphs (Martí et al., 2008), the Rome graphs (Di Battista et al., 1997), and treewidth collections used\nas pathwidth benchmarks (van den Broek and Bodlaender, n.d.; Larisch and Salfelder, n.d.; Dell et al., 2017,\n2018). Of the 17,714 classes, 16,087 carry a certified pathwidth with a witness layout and 862 a witnessed\nupper bound. The 610 large instances of Frinhani et al. (2018), with 400 to 1,000 customers, are included\nso that the dataset covers every published MOSP collection we hold. None of them has a value yet, because\nan exact search at that size is out of reach.\n    An independent checker, sharing no code with the solvers, verifies the dataset. It re-reads all 20,987\nmember files with its own readers, maps each onto its record’s graph by its renaming, recomputes the width\nof every witness layout and replays every minor certificate. It reports no failure on the 16,949 records. A\nvalue certified by search cannot be checked from its record; it rests on the repaired search, and for instances\nwithin reach of the emitter the certificate of Section 5.3 is how a third party can check it.\n\nAgreement with published values. All 84 Small graphs agree with the pathwidths of Mallach (2018,\nTables 4–5). Ten of the eleven VLSI circuits are certified at their published best-known track counts\n(de Oliveira and Lorena, 2002, Table I); the eleventh, W4 with 202 nets, is open at 28 against 27.\n\n\n\n                                                           23\n\fTable 5.3: Twenty collections, 21,754 instance files, reduce to 17,714 isomorphism classes, and 16,087 of them have\na certified pathwidth. The open classes are mostly in the PACE sets, the VSPLIB grids and Harwell-Boeing graphs,\nand the MOSP sets of Carvalho and Soma and of Frinhani et al. Classes: isomorphism classes within the collection\n(78 graphs above 5,000 vertices are compared by file content only). Counted here: classes whose first member, in the\norder of the table, is in this collection, so the column sums to the dataset’s class count. The next four columns split\nthose classes by provenance; No witnessed value: no layout is stored yet. a The same 46 files distributed a second time\nunder other names; every class is counted under its first copy.\n\n                                                                              Certified\nCollection                             Files   Classes   Counted here   by search   by bound      Upper bound only   No witnessed value\nMOSP: Challenge 2005                   5,806    3,169          3,169       3,168              1                 0                    0\nMOSP: Challenge 2005 (second copy)a       46       46              0           0              0                 0                    0\nMOSP: Chu & Stuckey                      200      200            200         198              0                 2                    0\nMOSP: Faggioli & Bentivoglio             300      287            274         274              0                 0                    0\nMOSP: SCOOP                               24       24             24          24              0                 0                    0\nVLSI gate matrix circuits                 11       11             11           5              5                 1                    0\nVSPLIB trees                              50       28             28          28              0                 0                    0\nVSPLIB grids                              50       50             50           9              0                19                   22\nVSPLIB Harwell-Boeing                     73       72             72          32              7                33                    0\nSmall (Marti et al. 2008)                 84       84             84          32             52                 0                    0\nRome graphs                           11,534   11,199         11,199       8,126          2,746               326                    1\nTreewidthLIB colouring                    58       58             58          18             13                27                    0\nfreetdi named graphs                     150      146            143          97             22                23                    1\nfreetdi control-flow graphs            1,817    1,070          1,064         398            661                 4                    1\nMOSP: Frinhani et al. 2018, large        610      610            610           0              0                 0                  610\nPACE 2016                                291      288            112          37             18                51                    6\nPACE 2017 exact                          200      199            193          61              7               115                   10\nPACE 2017 heuristic                      200      197            173          11              3                45                  114\nPACE 2017 bonus                          100      100            100          23              0                77                    0\nMOSP: Carvalho & Soma 2015               150      150            150          11              0               139                    0\nAll                                   21,754                  17,714      12,552          3,535               862                  765\n\n\n\nA caution about MOSP files. A MOSP file can be read with rows as customers or with rows as patterns,\nand the two readings give different graphs. The same public repository ships the de Carvalho and Soma\n(2015) instances as customers by patterns and the Chu and Stuckey (2009) instances beside them as patterns\nby customers. Only published densities and values tell them apart. Read the wrong way round, an instance\ncertifies at a different value.\n\n\n6     Conclusion\nWe asked which equalities of the Linhares–Yanasse table are true, and what follows when results, algorithms\nand benchmarks are moved between its twelve problems through pathwidth. Eight rows are exact, counting\nthe pathwidth row itself: each of those eight problems is pathwidth, or pathwidth plus one, on every input.\nTwo rows, split bandwidth and edge search, are bands one and two wide, and two, PLA folding and edge\nseparation, are false as stated, although a variant of each is exact (Table 2.3). Moving between the problems\npaid twice. Read as a pathwidth algorithm, the best exact open-stacks search turned out to rest on two false\ndominance theorems, and the rule that corrects them was already known in the pathwidth literature. And\none certified pathwidth answers every problem in the exact core at once, which is what made a single dataset\nfor nine problems possible.\n\nWhat transfers, and what does not. Within the exact core, anything proved or computed for one problem\nholds for the others with the offset of Table 2.3: an algorithm, a lower bound, a benchmark value. Across\na band only an approximation transfers. Split bandwidth and edge search stay within one of the number\n\n\n                                                                24\n\fof open stacks, as the table’s literal reading says, but not at a fixed offset, so an exact value of one does\nnot give an exact value of the other. Across a false row nothing transfers. Simple PLA folding can need\narbitrarily more tracks than the pathwidth suggests, and cutwidth is not within a constant of pathwidth on\nstars. Section 3 showed why these distinctions went unchecked for two decades: the communities rarely\nread one another, and most of what is known about the family is published under “pathwidth”.\n\nWhat the search teaches about dominance rules. The two false theorems were published in 2009 and\nrestated in later work, and the implementations that use them reported no failure. They give no wrong\nanswer on any whole instance we know of: in every run of our checks where the published rules lost the\nlast solution at a node, another branch found a solution (Section 4.5). So the absence of wrong answers\nwas never evidence that the rules were sound. Three things in this paper bear on that. Reading the rules in\npathwidth language put the correct rule, the commitment lemma, beside the published one. Proving each\nrule on its own was not enough: the order in which the rules run matters, because a cycle of true links can\nstill lose a node (Section 4.5). And a certificate whose checker recomputes every premise rejects the false\nrefutation that a checker of the published premises accepts (Section 5.3). The repair is cheap, one bipartite\nmatching per candidate and at most 2% more nodes per benchmark set, and it changed none of the 115\nbenchmark optima it re-proved.\n\nWhat a reader can do differently. Cite the corrected table, Table 2.3, rather than the 2002 one. Look for\nresults on MOSP and gate matrix layout under “pathwidth”. And in any implementation of the rules of Chu\nand Stuckey (2009), including the code behind the optima of Frinhani et al. (2018) that Martin et al. (2022)\nreuse (Appendix B.6), replace the published definite and better moves with the matching test. The values\nthose implementations report are not known to be wrong, but their refutations are not proved.\n\nLimits.   Four limits remain.\n\n    • The Lean model of the search and the C and Python code that run it are linked by testing and by\n      certificates, not by proof (Section 4.5).\n\n    • That an acyclic covering by checked steps is sound, which is the certificate checker’s acceptance\n      condition, is a paper argument (Section 5.3).\n\n    • LaPaugh’s equality es = pes is stated and not formalised. No row needs it (Section 2.2).\n\n    • One reference of the table, Kashiwabara and Fujisawa (1979), is not held. Its row, interval thickness,\n      is proved from two other references of the table (Section 2.2).\n\nOpen problems. Two values of the MOSP corpus, Random-125-125-2-2_0 and Random-125-125-2-3_0,\nhave never been certified. As of 6 October 2026 the split search is still refuting the value minus one on each\nand has found no order of the smaller value (Section 5.2). Of the 17,714 classes of the dataset, 1,627 have\nno certified pathwidth. One more budget step for every open class within the size our implementation han-\ndles costs at most 500 core-hours, but on the Rome graphs each such step has closed only about 40% of\nwhat it attempted, so the graph sets will not close by budget alone. The 610 MOSP instances with 400 to\n1,000 customers, and the 154 classes above 1,024 vertices per component, the limit of our implementation,\nare beyond an exact search. Beyond budgets, a class is certified without search only when a lower bound\nmeets its witness (Section 5.4). The open classes are therefore also a test set for stronger lower bounds on\npathwidth.\n\n\n\n                                                     25\n\fData, code and proofs\nEverything this paper reports can be checked from its repository, URL to be added on release . The repos-\nitory holds:\n\n    • the Lean 4 development, with every theorem the paper names (Appendix A) and an axiom check that\n      covers all of them;\n\n    • the source of both solvers, the open-stacks customer search and the graph pathwidth solver, in C and\n      in Python, with the published and the repaired rules;\n\n    • the dataset of Section 5.4, the certificates of Section 5.3 and the independent checkers of both;\n\n    • the bibliometric data of Section 3: the cached OpenAlex responses, the relevance labels and the\n      mechanical rule;\n\n    • a script for every table, figure and number in the paper, each of which regenerates it from the recorded\n      data.\n\n\nReferences\nJ. Christopher Beck, Ryo Kuroiwa, Jimmy H. M. Lee, Peter J. Stuckey, and Allen Z. Zhong. Transition\n   dominance in domain-independent dynamic programming. In 31st International Conference on Princi-\n   ples and Practice of Constraint Programming (CP 2025), volume 340 of LIPIcs, pages 5:1–5:23, 2025.\n   doi: 10.4230/LIPIcs.CP.2025.5.\n\nDaniel Bienstock and Paul Seymour. Monotonicity in graph searching. Journal of Algorithms, 12(2):239–\n  245, 1991. doi: 10.1016/0196-6774(91)90003-H.\n\nHans L. Bodlaender, Fedor V. Fomin, Arie M. C. A. Koster, Dieter Kratsch, and Dimitrios M. Thilikos. A\n  note on exact algorithms for vertex ordering problems on graphs. Theory of Computing Systems, 50(3):\n  420–432, 2012. doi: 10.1007/s00224-011-9312-0.\n\nGeoffrey Chu and Peter J. Stuckey. Minimizing the maximum number of open stacks by customer search.\n  In Principles and Practice of Constraint Programming (CP 2009), volume 5732 of Lecture Notes in\n  Computer Science, pages 242–257. Springer, 2009. doi: 10.1007/978-3-642-04244-7_21.\n\nGeoffrey G. Chu. Improving Combinatorial Optimization. PhD thesis, Department of Computer Science and\n  Software Engineering, The University of Melbourne, 2011. Minerva Access, http://hdl.handle.\n  net/11343/36679.\n\nDavid Coudert, Dorian Mazauric, and Nicolas Nisse. Experimental evaluation of a branch and bound algo-\n  rithm for computing pathwidth. In Experimental Algorithms (SEA 2014), volume 8504 of Lecture Notes\n  in Computer Science, pages 46–58. Springer, 2014. doi: 10.1007/978-3-319-07959-2_5.\n\nDavid Coudert, Dorian Mazauric, and Nicolas Nis