{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FN7c4TeAwm4qwNttKdYSkY","type":"tool_result","content":"1489\t  stacks. European Journal of Operational Research, 100(3):454–463, 1997b. doi: 10.1016/S0377-\n1490\t  2217(97)84107-0.\n1491\t\n1492\tHoracio Hideki Yanasse and Edson Luiz França Senne. The minimization of open stacks problem: A review\n1493\t  of some properties and their use in pre-processing operations. European Journal of Operational Research,\n1494\t  203(3):559–567, 2010. doi: 10.1016/j.ejor.2009.09.017.\n1495\t\n1496\t\n1497\t\n1498\tA    Formal names\n1499\tEvery theorem, lemma and counterexample of this paper is checked in the Lean 4 proof assistant with the\n1500\tMathlib library, unless the text says otherwise. The reader need not read Lean to follow the paper. This\n1501\tappendix is for a reader who wants to find the formal statement of a result: Table A.1 maps each result,\n1502\tin the order in which it appears, to the names of the Lean declarations that state it. Where a row cites a\n1503\tdefinition rather than a theorem, it is the definition the text uses.\n1504\t    All the theorems named depend only on the three standard axioms of Lean’s classical logic, propext,\n1505\tClassical.choice and Quot.sound; an axiom check in the repository prints this for every theorem\n1506\tnamed in the paper. The development has one sorry, Lean’s marker for a statement left unproved: a\n1507\tconjecture about lower bounds that no result of this paper uses. The part of the development behind Section 4\n1508\thas no sorry at all.\n1509\t    Two Lean proofs take a route that the text does not describe. The proof of Theorem 4.2 goes through\n1510\tnarrowness (Kornai and Tuza, 1992), which counts the closing order forwards rather than reversing it. The\n1511\t\n1512\t                                                     29\n1513\t\fproof of Theorem 4.7 reduces the deficiency form of Hall’s theorem to Mathlib’s Hall theorem by adding δ\n1514\tdummy stacks. One Lean proof takes a different route from the published one: the proof that node search\n1515\tis monotone (Theorem 2.4(5)) follows the crusade argument of Bienstock and Seymour (1991), not the\n1516\treduction to edge search of LaPaugh (1993) on which Kirousis and Papadimitriou (1986) rely. Three kinds\n1517\tof fact are computed rather than proved. The values of K2 , K1,3 and K3,3 after Theorem 2.5, which show that\n1518\tevery end of the two bands occurs, are computed by brute force from each quantity’s own definition. The\n1519\tsearch for smaller counterexamples (Appendix B.5) and the results of Section 5 are computations.\n1520\tTable A.1: Every result of the paper has a formal statement in Lean, listed here in the order in which the results appear.\n1521\t\n1522\tEntry     Result in the paper                                    Lean names\n1523\tSection 2, the equivalences\n1524\tA.1       Lemma 2.1, cliques sit in a bag                        PathDecomposition.exists_bag_of_\n1525\t                                                                 isClique\n1526\tA.2       Theorem 2.2, vertex separation is pathwidth            vertexSeparation_eq_pathwidth\n1527\tA.3       Theorem 2.3, MOSP is pathwidth plus one                MOSPInstance.mospValue_eq_pathwidth_\n1528\t                                                                 add_one\n1529\tA.4       The pattern graph gives no bound: the star K1,4        star_mospValue,\n1530\t          (after Theorem 2.3)                                    star_pathwidth_agreementGraph,\n1531\t                                                                 star_refutes_pattern_graph_bound\n1532\tA.5       Theorem 2.4(1), gate matrix layout                     NetGateMatrix.tracks_eq_mospValue,\n1533\t                                                                 NetGateMatrix.tracks_eq_pathwidth_add_\n1534\t                                                                 one\n1535\tA.6       