         exec_repairedFullFilter_pathwidth,\n                                                                exec_repairedFullFilter_mospValue\nA.46    Under the published rules the filter is not             not_codeFilterSound_cexGraph\n        node-sound\n\n\n                                                           31\n\fEntry     Result in the paper                                    Lean names\nA.47      Lemma 4.9, the premises as commitment                  isDefinite_iff_endpoint,\n          conditions                                             isHereditarilyDefinite_iff_\n                                                                 isCommittable\nA.48      In Counterexample 4.4 the endpoint test                cex_isDefinite_not_isCommittable\n          passes and the commitment fails\nA.49      The published premise gives a commitment to            exists_isCommittable_of_isDefinite\n          the least-border set\nA.50      In Counterexample 4.4 that set is {2, 3, 4}            cex_isCommittable_234\nA.51      A commitment of depth one holds iff                    isCommittable_insert_iff\n          open(q, S) ≤ 1\nA.52      The greedy step of Coudert, Mazauric and               isGreedyStep_iff\n          Nisse is that condition\nSection 5, computational results\nA.53      The split of the root into independent tasks is        execSplit_repairedFullFilter_sound,\n          sound                                                  execSplit_repairedFullFilter_\n                                                                 mospValue\nAppendix B, supporting material\nA.54     Node search equalities need an edge                     nodeSearch_ne_intervalThickness_of_\n         (Appendix B.2)                                          edgeless, monotoneNodeSearch_ne_\n                                                                 vertexSeparation_add_one_of_edgeless\nA.55      Lengauer’s Theorem 4 fails at k = 0                    isPositiveVSG_triangleGraph_\n          (Appendix B.2)                                         counterexample\nA.56      t = ns fails on the matrix [1] (Appendix B.2)          tracks_ne_monotoneNodeSearch_one\nA.57      The Kirousis–Papadimitriou claim fails on              kirousisPapadimitriou_claim2_false\n          K1,3 (Appendix B.2)\nA.58      Progressive pebbling is in the exact core              pbw_lengauerD_eq_pathwidth,\n          (Appendix B.3)                                         pbw_eq_mospValue_pebbleMatrix,\n                                                                 mpb_eq_pathwidth_add_one\nA.59      Proposition B.1, the repaired filter is                repairedFullFilter_sound\n          node-sound\nA.60      Theorem B.2, runs are sound                            Exec.sound\n\n\n\nB       Supporting material\nThis appendix holds the material that supports Sections 2, 4 and 5 without being needed to follow them.\n\nB.1     Formal definitions of eight problems\nThese are the formal definitions of the eight problems that Table 2.2 states in one line. As in Section 2.1,\neach is a decision problem with an integer k, and its value is the least k for which the answer is yes.\n\nOne-dimensional logic. Given gates, nets and the nets each gate connects, is there an order of the gates\n     whose net intervals have an interval graph of clique number at most k? Its net graph H joins two nets\n     that share a gate. A boundary variant pins two gates to the ends (Ohtsuki et al., 1979, §§II–IV).\n\nPLA folding pla(M). As gate matrix layout, with at most two nets per track. With no such bound (multiple\n     folding) it is gate matrix layout itself (Möhring, 1990, pp. 25, 36).\n\nInterval thickness θ (G). Is there an interval supergraph of G on the same vertices with clique number at\n      most k? (Kirousis and Papadimitriou, 1985, p. 182); (Möhring, 1990, p. 31).\n\n\n                                                            32\n\fNode search ns(G). Can searchers, placed on and removed from vertices, clear every edge with at most k\n     on the graph at once? An edge is cleared when both ends are guarded, and a clear edge is recontam-\n     inated by a searcher-free path to a contaminated one. mns(G) is the same over strategies that never\n     recontaminate (Kirousis and Papadimitriou, 1985, p. 181); (Kirousis and Papadimitriou, 1986, §2).\n\nEdge search es(G). The same game with a third move, sliding a searcher along an edge, which clears that\n     edge; pes(G) for progressive strategies, which never recontaminate an edge (Kirousis and Papadim-\n     itriou, 1986, p. 208); (Ellis et al., 1994, p. 53).\n\nNarrowness ν(G). Is there an order v1 , . . . , vn such that the following process never holds more than k\n     vertices: at step i add vi , then remove every v j , j ≤ i, with no neighbour vℓ , ℓ > i? (Kornai and Tuza,\n     1992, §2).\n\nSplit bandwidth sb(G). Is there a graph obtained from G by repeatedly splitting a vertex into an edge, its\n      neighbours partitioned between the two ends, with bandwidth at most k? (Fomin, 1998, §3.2).\n\nEdge separation. The Linhares–Yanasse table cites Lengauer (1981), which supports three readings: cutwidth\n     cw(G), the least over layouts of the most edges crossing a gap (p. 468); modified cutwidth mcw(G),\n     counting only edges that pass strictly over a vertex (Definition 6); and Lengauer’s vertex separator\n     game VSG(G), the least k such that the vertices can be pebbled in an order that leaves at most k\n     unpebbled vertices adjacent to pebbled ones (p. 467).\n\nB.2    Edge cases and a gap in a published proof\nThree of the published equalities fail on degenerate inputs, and each failure is proved in Lean. On a nonempty\ngraph with no edges, ns = 0 while vs + 1 = θ = 1, so both equalities of Kirousis and Papadimitriou (1985,\n1986) need an edge (Appendix A, A.54). For a matrix with no 1s, Z = 0 while pw + 1 = 1. Lengauer’s\nTheorem 4 fails at k = 0 on an edgeless graph (Appendix A, A.55). Möhring (1990, Thm 3.9), t = ns, fails\nliterally on the 1 × 1 matrix [1] (Appendix A, A.56).\n     The published proof of vs ≤ ns − 1 (Kirousis and Papadimitriou, 1986, Thm 4.1) has a gap. It orders the\nvertices by when they first receive a searcher and claims that every vertex counted at the worst cut still holds\na searcher, “since the vertices in Di0 are joined to vertices not yet cleared” (p. 217). A strategy may place a\nsearcher and remove it again before clearing anything, which recontaminates nothing. On K1,3 , placing and\nremoving a searcher on each leaf before guarding the centre is an optimal recontamination-free strategy for\nwhich the claim fails (Appendix A, A.57). The theorem is true. The repair orders the vertices by clearing\ntime, as our Lean proof does, or uses the paper’s own normal form, its Corollary 2.4.\n\nB.3    A thirteenth member\nProgressive black-white pebbling, which is not in the Linhares–Yanasse table, belongs to the exact core.\nWrite pbw for its value and mpb for the minimum progressive pebbling number of Kirousis and Papadim-\nitriou (1986). Lengauer (1981, Thm 3) gives vs(G) ≤ k ⇐⇒ pbw(Gd ) ≤ k + 2 on the dag Gd built from G.\nHis Theorem 2 gives pbw(D) = vs(Du ) + 1 on a dag D, where Du is the MOSP graph of the matrix whose\ncolumn v is v with its predecessors. And mpb = pw + 1 (Kirousis and Papadimitriou, 1986, Thm 3.1). All\nthree are proved in Lean, each with its hypothesis: Lengauer’s Theorem 2 in its instance form fails at k = 1\non a dag with no arcs, and mpb = ns fails on edgeless graphs (Appendix A, A.58).\n\n\n\n\n                                                      33\n\fB.4   Why the published proof of the definite move fails\nThe proof moves q to the front of a solution and claims that after each prefix “we have at most open(q, S)\nextra stacks open, but at least close(q, S) extra stacks closed” (p. 6). At a prefix T ⊇ S that does not yet\ncontain q, moving q forward changes the open stacks by b(T ∪ X) − b(T ) = |N[q] \\ O(T )| − |X \\ T |. At\nT = S this is open − close ≤ 0. But customers of X that the solution has already closed are in T and no\nlonger count as extra stacks closed, while the stacks q opens may still be new. In the example, a solution\nmay begin with 3, after which 4 is free: at T = {2, 3, 4}, moving 0 forward costs one more stack. A second,\nindependent slip: even where the conclusion holds, the literal sequence S ++ [q] ++ R′ need not be a solution,\nbecause a customer that q frees stays open until its turn; the argument needs the free moves.\n\nB.5   The search for smaller counterexamples\nThe smallest graph on which the rule loses the last solution has 14 customers: there is none on any graph to 9\nvertices, by exhaustion, and none on the 88,802 connected graphs on 10 vertices with at most 14 edges. The\ncounterexample was found by a search over a family of gadgets built from the failed proof, and minimised\nby deletion.\n\nB.6   The search for prior reports of the error\nWe looked for any earlier report of the error in the definite move. We listed the works citing Chu and Stuckey\n(2009), Chu’s thesis and its IJCAI 2013 abstract in OpenAlex, Semantic Scholar and OpenCitations: 141\nworks. Of the 38 that cite the CP paper we read 24 in full (63%), and of the 103 that cite only the thesis,\n32 (31%). No work read gives a counterexample, an erratum or a correction. One, Fink (2012), restates the\ndefinite move as true with a stronger premise, refuted in Section 4.3. Three use an implementation of the\nrules and report no failure; among them Frinhani et al. (2018) ran the original code to obtain the optima that\nMartin et al. (2022) reuse.\n\nB.7   The filter and the runs are sound\nProposition B.1. At every state with the invariant, for every set Q of refuted old moves, the filter definite →\nsubset → better with both repairs is node-sound (Appendix A, A.59).\n\nProof. If the definite move fires, Theorem 4.6 applies. Otherwise the subset rule keeps a survivor with a\nsolution; let u be the earliest. If the better move dropped u, it cited an earlier survivor that the repaired\nargument proves solvable, contradicting the choice of u.\n\nTheorem B.2 (runs are sound). Let the filter be node-sound and return only playable customers. Suppose\nthat at the start of a run the invariant holds, every memo entry is a genuine refutation, and every old move\nis refuted. Then the run leaves only genuine memo entries, and if it answers false at S, then ¬Solk (S). This\nholds for any child order and any memo policy, with the old move on or off (Appendix A, A.60).\n\n    The proof is an induction over the run, in the order in which subtrees complete. Every old move a node\nholds is a sibling subtree that completed with false earlier in the same run, or an inherited one that passed\nthe reinsertion test.\n\nB.8   The split re-certification\nTable B.1 gives the seven hardest re-certifications of Section 5.2 and the two values never certified, which\nthe same split is refuting. Each instance’s tree was expanded at the top into independent subtrees (tasks),\n\n\n                                                      34\n\fand each task was refuted by one worker. The seven re-certifications ended with every task refuted and the\nvalue unchanged. No task of any instance has found an order of the smaller value.\n\nTable B.1: All seven hardest values re-certify unchanged, at 18 to 369 core-hours each; the two values never certified\nare in flight, with no task satisfiable so far (read 6 October 2026). Value: the stored optimum; k: the value minus one,\nwhich must be refuted. For an instance in flight, the nodes and core-hours are those of the tasks finished so far.\n\n        Instance                          Value     k   State       Tasks refuted        Nodes     Core-hours\n        Random-125-125-4-1_0                 57    56   refuted              669    4.97 × 1010          18.1\n        Random-125-125-2-4_0                 24    23   refuted            1,686    4.98 × 1011         154.1\n        Random-125-125-4-5_0                 46    45   refuted              616    7.96 × 1010          26.1\n        Random-125-125-4-2_0                 57    56   refuted              842    7.64 × 1010          30.2\n        Random-125-125-2-1_0                 24    23   refuted            4,096    1.30 × 1012         366.5\n        Random-125-125-2-5_0                 20    19   refuted            4,168    1.28 × 1012         368.8\n        Random-125-125-4-4_0                 51    50   refuted            2,504    4.12 × 1011         148.8\n        Random-125-125-2-3_0                 21    20   in flight          2,601    1.29 × 1012         453.2\n        Random-125-125-2-2_0                 25    24   in flight          2,196    1.06 × 1012         443.2\n\n\n\n\nC     The bibliometric study\nThis appendix gives the method behind Section 3 in full, the counts per name and when each name was in\nuse.\n\nSearch. We measured how much each name is used with OpenAlex (Priem et al., 2022), on 29 Septem-\nber 2026. For each of the twelve problems we searched titles and abstracts for its name and its common\nvariants, and restricted the search to the four fields in which the problems are studied: Computer Science,\nMathematics, Engineering and Decision Sciences. Without that restriction several names are ordinary words:\n“narrowness” returns over a million works, and “edge separation” returns aerodynamics.\n\nRelevance. Inside those fields a search hit is still not a work that uses the name. OpenAlex’s search\nstems words and ignores punctuation, so a quoted phrase matches more than the phrase. “Narrowness”\nmatches “narrowing” in term rewriting; “node searching” matches look-ups in peer-to-peer networks; “split\nbandwidth” matches radar interferometry and 5G spectrum assignment; “path-width” matches the widths of\nroads, tool paths and datapaths. We therefore fetched all 2,271 hits with their titles, abstracts, topics and\nvenues, and kept a work only if it is about the problem in the sense of the Linhares–Yanasse table. For\nthe eleven smaller names the 700 hits were judged one by one. For pathwidth, the 1,571 hits were filtered\nby a rule: keep a work that uses the one-word spelling, that OpenAlex places in a theory subfield, or that\nmentions graphs, treewidth or minors and not roads, vehicles, trajectories or datapaths. We checked the rule\nby reading samples of what it kept and what it dropped. Of the 2,271 hits, 1,593 are relevant: 1,213 for\npathwidth and 380 for the other eleven names. The two search-game names keep 27% and 40% of their hits.\n\nRobustness. The counts are lower bounds. A work can study a problem without naming it in its abstract,\nOpenAlex has few abstracts before about 1990, and its citation counts run below Google Scholar’s. The\njudgements were made by one annotator with model assistance, record a yes or no per work and not the\nreason, and have not been checked by a second annotator. As a second reading we applied a mechanical\nrule: the literal phrase with a context term and no exclusion term. It agrees with the judgements on 543 of\n\n\n                                                          35\n\fTable C.1: Citations do not track usage: the most-cited papers are cited far more often than their problem’s name is\nused. Works that use each problem’s name in the sense of the Linhares–Yanasse table, and citations of the paper the\ntable cites for it. Relevant: hits kept by the relevance check. Citations: OpenAlex cited_by_count, 29 September\n2026. Kashiwabara and Fujisawa (1979) has no OpenAlex record. a OpenAlex returned one pathwidth record twice:\nthe counts are of records, as Figure 3.1 draws them, and there are 1,212 distinct relevant pathwidth works.\n\nProblem                   Discipline            Relevant      Hits   Reference in the 2002 table         Citations\nPathwidth                 graph theory             1,213a    1,571   Kinnersley (1992)                        214\nGate matrix layout        VLSI                        110      125   Möhring (1990); Wing et al.          134 / 89\n                                                                     (1985)\nPLA folding               VLSI                         68       74   Möhring (1990)                           134\nMOSP                      operations research          58       60   Yanasse (1997b); Fink and Voß         75 / 71\n                                                                     (1999)\nVertex separation         graph theory                 55     100    Kinnersley (1992)                         214\nEdge search               graph theory                 38      95    Kirousis and Papadimitriou                293\n                                                                     (1986)\nNode search               graph theory                 34     127    Kirousis and Papadimitriou                124\n                                                                     (1985)\nOne-dimensional logic     VLSI                         11       18   Ohtsuki et al. (1979)                     108\nInterval thickness        graph theory                  6       10   Kashiwabara and Fujisawa                    –\n                                                                     (1979)\nNarrowness                graph theory                  0       35   Kornai and Tuza (1992)                     44\nSplit bandwidth           graph theory                  0       35   Fomin (1998)                               19\nEdge separation           graph theory                  0       21   Lengauer (1981)                            77\nAll twelve                                          1,593    2,271\n\n\nthe 700 judged works (78%). Under both readings pathwidth has more than three times as many works as\nthe other eleven names together (3.2 and 3.6 times), the three empty names stay empty apart from one 5G\nfalse positive, and the search games lose most of their hits. MOSP’s rank over all years is the one conclusion\nthat depends on the reading: fourth under the judgements, fifth under the rule.\n\nCounts and citations. Table C.1 gives the counts per name. Citation counts do not track name usage.\nKirousis and Papadimitriou (1986) is the most-cited paper in the table (293 citations), while only 38 works\nuse “edge search” in its sense. The paper is cited as a foundational result on graph searching, not because\npeople work on the edge search game under that name. The split is starker for Ohtsuki et al. (1979): 108\ncitations against 11 works using “one-dimensional logic”. Split bandwidth is Fomin’s own term in the paper\nthe table cites (Fomin, 1998), and no one adopted it.\n\nWhen each name was in use. Figure C.1 shows the counts over time. PLA folding and gate matrix layout\npeak in 1980–1989 and fall to almost nothing after 2010, and one-dimensional logic has no relevant work\nafter 2009. MOSP first appears in the 1990s, peaks in 2005–2014 and is still active. Pathwidth’s rate has\nroughly doubled since the 1990s, from about 15 to 27 works per million, and its 311 relevant works in\n2020–2024 are more than the whole history of any other name. Rows with a few works per period move by\nwhole cells on one paper, and should be read as anecdote rather than trend.\n\n\n\n\n                                                        36\n\f                                                                                                                             1.0\n           PLA folding (68)                      19       19        12     4      8      5         1\n\n\n  gate matrix layout (110)                       9        30        30     3      29     4         2       2      1\n                                                                                                                             0.8\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                                                                   rate / that name's peak rate\none-dimensional logic (11)               1                4         1      2      1      2\n\n\n          node search (34)                       1        2         2      3      6      9         7       3      1          0.6\n\n          edge search (38)                                          4      2      4      12       12       1      3\n\n\n      interval thickness (6)                              1         2                    2         1                         0.4\n\n\n    vertex separation (53)                       1        3         4      6      7      7        10      15\n\n                                                                                                                             0.2\n                 MOSP (57)                                                 1      5      17       17       5      12\n\n\n          pathwidth (1014)               1       1                  39    53      49    114       179    267     311\n                                                                                                                             0.0\n                               1970    1975     1980    1985    1990     1995   2000    2005     2010    2015   2020\n                                –74     –79      –84     –89     –94      –99    –04     –09      –14     –19    –24\n\n\n                          operations research         VLSI design        graph theory        before 1990: thin abstract coverage\n\n                                   Not shown, no relevant works: narrowness, split bandwidth, edge separation.\n\n\nFigure C.1: The VLSI names belong to the 1980s, the search games peak later and fade, MOSP peaks in 2005–2014,\nand only pathwidth is still rising. The shade is the name’s rate, relevant works per million works in the same four fields\nin each five-year period, as a fraction of that name’s own peak rate; the numbers are raw counts. Rows are ordered by\nthe period of their peak. Counts cover 1970–2024, since 2025–2026 is not fully indexed, so row totals are below those\nof Table C.1. Before 1990 (hatched) OpenAlex has few abstracts.\n\n\n\n\n                                                               37\n\f","numLines":437,"startLine":1489,"totalLines":1925}}}
