{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JLmQen96nJ98cD6i9wsfsH","type":"tool_result","content":"Problem & Published source & Relation & Status \\\\\n\\midrule\nMOSP & \\citet{Yanasse1997a}; \\citet{FellowsLangston1989}; \\citet{LY2002} &\n  $Z(M)=\\pw(G_M)+1$ ($M$ has a 1) & exact \\\\\nGate matrix layout & \\citet{LY2002ref6} & $t(M)=Z(M)=\\pw(G_M)+1$ & exact \\\\\nOne-dim.\\ logic & \\citet{LY2002ref7} & tracks $=\\theta(H)=\\pw(H)+1$; boundary variant $\\ge\\pw(H)+1$ & exact \\\\\nPLA folding & \\citet{LY2002ref6} & $\\max(\\pw+1,\\lceil|N|/2\\rceil)\\le\\pla$, gap unbounded; multiple folding $=\\pw+1$ & false; variant exact \\\\\nInterval thickness & \\citet{LY2002ref6}; \\citet{LY2002ref9} & $\\theta=\\pw+1$ ($V\\ne\\emptyset$) & exact \\\\\nNode search & \\citet{LY2002ref9,LY2002ref10}; \\citet{BienstockSeymour1991} & $\\ns=\\mns=\\vs+1=\\pw+1=\\theta$ ($E\\ne\\emptyset$) & exact \\\\\nEdge search & \\citet{EllisSudboroughTurner1994}; \\citet{LY2002ref10} & $\\vs\\le\\es\\le\\vs+2$, also for $\\pes$ & band \\\\\nNarrowness & \\citet{LY2002ref11} & $\\nu=\\pw+1$ ($V\\ne\\emptyset$) & exact \\\\\n\\begin{theorem}[two rows are false]\\label{thm:false}\n\\leavevmode\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item PLA folding: for a matrix with a 1,\n  $\\max(\\pw(G_M)+1,\\lceil|N|/2\\rceil)\\le\\pla(M)$, and the gap to $\\pw+1$ is\n  unbounded. The $5\\times5$ identity needs 3 tracks while $\\pw(G_M)+1=1$, and the\n  path $P_n$ as a matrix, one gate per edge, has $t=2$ and\n  $\\pla\\ge\\lceil n/2\\rceil$, so the gap is unbounded on connected instances too\n  \\citep[Prop.~3.15]{LY2002ref6}~\\leanref{false-pla}.\nbenchmark values it proved.\n\n\\paragraph{Instances.} The \\emph{MOSP corpus} is the 6,376 instance files of\nfive public open-stacks collections. They are the 2005 Constraint Modelling\nChallenge \\citep{SmithGent2005} with a second copy of 46 of its files, the random\ninstances of \\citet{ChuStuckey2009} (30 to 125 customers), those of\n\\citet{FaggioliBentivoglio1998}, and the 24 industrial SCOOP instances. They\nare the first rows of Table~\\ref{tab:dataset}. A Chu--Stuckey name such as\n\\texttt{Random-125-125-2-4\\_0} gives the number of customers, the number of\nafter which every vertex has degree at least $w$. Any checker can replay it,\nand it proves the pathwidth is at least $w$.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{Twenty collections, 21,754 instance files, reduce to 17,714\nisomorphism classes, and 16,087 of them have a certified pathwidth. The open\nclasses are mostly in the PACE sets, the VSPLIB grids and Harwell-Boeing\ngraphs, and the MOSP sets of Carvalho and Soma and of Frinhani et al. \\emph{Classes}: isomorphism classes within the\ncollection (78 graphs above 5,000 vertices are compared by file content only).\n\\emph{Counted here}: classes whose first member, in the order of the table,\nis in this collection, so the column sums to the dataset's class count. The\nnext four columns split those classes by provenance; \\emph{No witnessed\nproblems and proves what is true. Section~\\ref{sec:names} measures which names\nand communities the literature uses. Section~\\ref{sec:search} reads the exact\nopen-stacks search as a pathwidth algorithm, finds the two false rules and\nproves the repaired search sound. Section~\\ref{sec:results} reports its cost\nand the dataset, and Section~\\ref{sec:closing} answers the question above.\nEvery theorem we state is machine-checked in the Lean~4 proof assistant. The\nreader need not read Lean: the proofs are given in the text, and\nAppendix~\\ref{app:lean} maps each result to its formal statement.\nAppendix~\\ref{app:machinery-all} holds the supporting material,\nAppendix~\\ref{app:names} the bibliometric method, and the data,\ncode and proofs are described at the end of the paper (page~\\pageref{sec:data}).\nsec6_conclusion.tex:21:what made a single dataset for nine problems possible.\nsec5_results.tex:186:separator game take the value $w$, and MOSP, gate matrix layout with multiple","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d20ab00f-ced5-4701-8f82-053c98f1df8f","uuid":"4fc81d8c-0583-4bdf-a726-785df590b190","timestamp":"2026-10-06T20:16:20.071Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"Problem & Published source & Relation & Status \\\\\n\\midrule\nMOSP & \\citet{Yanasse1997a}; \\citet{FellowsLangston1989}; \\citet{LY2002} &\n  $Z(M)=\\pw(G_M)+1$ ($M$ has a 1) & exact \\\\\nGate matrix layout & \\citet{LY2002ref6} & $t(M)=Z(M)=\\pw(G_M)+1$ & exact \\\\\nOne-dim.\\ logic & \\citet{LY2002ref7} & tracks $=\\theta(H)=\\pw(H)+1$; boundary variant $\\ge\\pw(H)+1$ & exact \\\\\nPLA folding & \\citet{LY2002ref6} & $\\max(\\pw+1,\\lceil|N|/2\\rceil)\\le\\pla$, gap unbounded; multiple folding $=\\pw+1$ & false; variant exact \\\\\nInterval thickness & \\citet{LY2002ref6}; \\citet{LY2002ref9} & $\\theta=\\pw+1$ ($V\\ne\\emptyset$) & exact \\\\\nNode search & \\citet{LY2002ref9,LY2002ref10}; \\citet{BienstockSeymour1991} & $\\ns=\\mns=\\vs+1=\\pw+1=\\theta$ ($E\\ne\\emptyset$) & exact \\\\\nEdge search & \\citet{EllisSudboroughTurner1994}; \\citet{LY2002ref10} & $\\vs\\le\\es\\le\\vs+2$, also for $\\pes$ & band \\\\\nNarrowness & \\citet{LY2002ref11} & $\\nu=\\pw+1$ ($V\\ne\\emptyset$) & exact \\\\\n\\begin{theorem}[two rows are false]\\label{thm:false}\n\\leavevmode\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item PLA folding: for a matrix with a 1,\n  $\\max(\\pw(G_M)+1,\\lceil|N|/2\\rceil)\\le\\pla(M)$, and the gap to $\\pw+1$ is\n  unbounded. The $5\\times5$ identity needs 3 tracks while $\\pw(G_M)+1=1$, and the\n  path $P_n$ as a matrix, one gate per edge, has $t=2$ and\n  $\\pla\\ge\\lceil n/2\\rceil$, so the gap is unbounded on connected instances too\n  \\citep[Prop.~3.15]{LY2002ref6}~\\leanref{false-pla}.\nbenchmark values it proved.\n\n\\paragraph{Instances.} The \\emph{MOSP corpus} is the 6,376 instance files of\nfive public open-stacks collections. They are the 2005 Constraint Modelling\nChallenge \\citep{Smith